(window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS=window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS||{})["m2-simple"]={"id":"simple","label":"Simpler · answer first, one step per sentence","revision":"ling406-modern-m2-simple-2","scripts":{"speech1-01":[],"speech1-02":[{"text":"Listeners get one clear message out of a speech signal that is messy and that changes from speaker to speaker. This is because **speech perception** (getting a language message out of the sound of speech) has to undo two problems.","children":[]},{"text":"The first problem is that the sounds run together. In the signal for “I can see you,” the energy of each sound runs into the next one. Nothing in the signal cleanly marks where letters or even words begin and end. Real-time MRI of the **vocal tract** (the passage from the throat to the lips) from USC SPAN explains why. The tongue, lips and other articulators keep moving without a pause. The movement for one sound overlaps the movement for the next, so their sounds overlap too.","children":[]},{"text":"The second problem is that one word sounds different from each speaker. People have different voices and different vocal tract sizes, so the same word comes out with different acoustic properties. Across **76 speakers**, each of ten vowels covers a wide range of first and second formant values. These ranges **overlap** with each other. So one pair of formant values can be one vowel for one speaker and a different vowel for another speaker.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the signal varies so much, recognizing speech means finding a **stable linguistic identity** inside all that physical variation.","children":[]}],"speech1-03":[{"text":"Listeners have to turn a changing, **analog signal** (one that varies continuously) into something more abstract, so the language system can work with it. This is because the raw signal can shift in frequency, loudness and timing by tiny amounts. It has no built-in markers of where one speech sound ends and the next starts. Many small changes still leave the speech sound the same.","children":[]},{"text":"The abstract form is a **symbolic representation** (a label for a language unit that ignores the fine physical detail). The unit is the **phoneme** (a sound category that tells words apart in a language). The /b/ in “bat” and the /p/ in “pat” are different phonemes, because swapping them changes the word. Many slightly different acoustic versions of /b/ still all count as one /b/.","children":[]},{"text":"**Categorical perception** is thought to reflect this **“translation” from physical to symbolic**. Listeners are worse at hearing differences inside one category. They are better at hearing differences of the same size that cross from one category to another. So a change is easy to notice when it changes which phoneme the listener hears. The same size of change is easy to miss when both sounds stay the same phoneme.","children":[]},{"text":"Categorical perception is only one idea about how this translation happens, and there are other ideas.","children":[]}],"speech1-04":[{"text":"The shape of the vocal tract decides which frequencies get made louder, and those louder frequencies are the formants. This is because, following **Ladefoged (1996)**, the shape of the **vocal tract** is tied in a regular way to **which resonant frequencies get amplified**. The vocal tract is the passage from the larynx through the throat and mouth to the lips.","children":[]},{"text":"A **resonant frequency** is a frequency that a space naturally makes stronger. A tube of one shape boosts some frequencies and weakens others. The tongue, jaw and lips change the shape of the vocal tract. So moving them changes which frequencies get the biggest boost. These boosted frequencies are the **formants**, and each vowel has its own set.","children":[]},{"text":"For **heed, hid, head and had**, the tongue goes from high and front to low. In heed, the second formant is far above the first. Through hid, head and had, the two formants get closer together.","children":[]},{"text":"For **hod, hawed, hood and who’d**, the tongue moves back and the lips get more rounded. The boosted frequencies gather at the low end, so the first and second formants sit close together.","children":[]},{"text":"A vowel is defined by the **relationship among its resonances**, which stays similar across speakers even when the exact frequencies differ.","children":[]},{"text":"A **formant transition** (a formant’s frequency changing over time) happens when the mouth moves from a consonant into a vowel. The steady part of a vowel tells you about the vowel. The moving part tells you how the neighboring sound is made.","children":[]}],"speech1-05":[{"text":"Vowels look different on a spectrogram mainly because their two lowest formants are spaced differently. This is because a **spectrogram** (a display of sound energy over time and frequency) shows each vowel as several bands of strong energy. These bands are the vowel’s **formants**. **F1** is the lowest formant and **F2** is the next one up. Both run through the whole vowel at the same time.","children":[]},{"text":"The words **bead, bid, bade, bed, bad, bod, baud, bode, “bood” and booed** come from Rob Hagiwara’s English vowel recordings. They all start with /b/ and end with /d/. Since the consonants are the same, the differences between the words come mainly from the vowels.","children":[]},{"text":"In **bead**, F1 is very low and F2 is very high, above 2,000 Hz. So the two bands are far apart.","children":[]},{"text":"Going through **bid, bade, bed and bad**, F1 goes up and F2 comes down. So the two bands get closer together.","children":[]},{"text":"In the back rounded vowels **bode, “bood” and booed**, F2 drops low, near 1,000 Hz or below. So F2 ends up sitting near F1.","children":[]},{"text":"Bead has a low F1 with a high F2, while booed has a low F1 with a low F2. Since F1 and F2 change this way from vowel to vowel, these two formants carry most of what tells vowels apart.","children":[]}],"speech1-06":[{"text":"A speech sound can be split into a source and a filter. This recording of /u/ has a low first and a low second formant. This is because **source-filter synthesis** builds speech sounds from two parts. The **source** is the sound energy the voice makes. The **filter** is how the vocal tract shapes that energy. Splitting them lets you change one part while keeping the other the same.","children":[]},{"text":"The work is done in **Praat** (free speech analysis software from praat.org). Praat can show recordings, measure formants and rebuild sounds.","children":[]},{"text":"The recording is a speaker saying **/u/**, the vowel in “booed.” The **waveform** (a record of air pressure changing over time) shows timing and loudness. The **spectrogram** splits the same sound into its frequencies, so it shows the resonance pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"In this /u/, the **first formant is at about 300 Hz**. The **second formant is very low, around 900 Hz**. Both run through the whole vowel. A low F1 together with a low F2 fits the back rounded sound of /u/.","children":[]},{"text":"If only the filter is changed and the vowel changes, then the vowel’s identity must come from the filter. Since the filter is the pattern of formants, these measured formants become the starting point for making new filters.","children":[]}],"speech1-07":[{"text":"Praat finds five formants in the /u/, but only the first two are changed in this example. This is because **Praat can find formants automatically**. It follows each resonance through the recording and marks its frequency at each moment. In this /u/, it finds **five formants** running at the same time. Nobody has to guess the frequencies by eye.","children":[]},{"text":"**Only the first three formants are usually important for phonemic processing** (telling phonemes apart). The numbers give the order from low to high. F1 is the lowest, F2 is next and F3 is third. F1, F2 and F3 carry most of the information that separates phonemes. The higher formants depend more on the particular person’s voice.","children":[]},{"text":"The example only changes **the first two formants**, which sit near 300 Hz and 900 Hz in this /u/. These two formants carry most of the vowel information. So changing just these two is enough to turn one vowel into another.","children":[]},{"text":"F3 and the higher formants are kept the same. Since nothing else changes, any change listeners hear has to come from F1 or F2.","children":[]}],"speech1-08":[{"text":"Taking the filter out of the recorded /u/ leaves the **source**, and the source sounds like a buzz. This is because Praat builds a model of the filter from the measured formant frequencies. It then uses that model to remove the filter’s effect from the recording. What is left is a **more-or-less “unfiltered” version of the voice**, the sound energy the vocal folds made. It is only “more or less” unfiltered, because the split is an estimate.","children":[]},{"text":"The buzz still has energy and pitch, but it no longer sounds like /u/. Its energy is spread across many frequencies, without the strong low formants of /u/. The pitch stays, because pitch comes from the vocal folds vibrating. The vowel sound goes away, because the vowel sound comes from the filter.","children":[]},{"text":"The split is **a little bit like doing a multiple regression analysis**. In multiple regression, you estimate how much one predictor contributes and take it out, so you can look at what is left. Here the filter’s contribution is estimated and taken out, and what is left is treated as the source.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the source is now on its own, new filters can be put on it. Keeping the same source while swapping filters lets you test what the resonance pattern does.","children":[]}],"speech1-09":[{"text":"Three different filters are put on the same source, and each one changes only one part of the resonance pattern. This is because a **filter** says which frequencies get more boost and which get less, so each filter is a resonance pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"The **first filter** is just the first four formants from the original /u/ recording. So putting it on the source should bring back the original /u/.","children":[]},{"text":"The **second filter** raises **the first formant a little**. The other formants stay about where they were.","children":[]},{"text":"The **third filter** raises **the second formant a lot**. F1 and the higher formants stay about where they were.","children":[]},{"text":"In the original filter, F1 and F2 are both low, which fits /u/. Raising F1 moves only the lowest resonance up. Raising F2 moves only the second resonance up, by a much bigger amount. So the filters do not shift every frequency together.","children":[]},{"text":"The **sound source stays the same** for all three filters, and no new speaker or recording is needed. Since only the filter changes, any change in the vowel sound comes from the filter. Comparing the three results shows what changing the lowest resonance does and what changing the second resonance does.","children":[]}],"speech1-10":[{"text":"Putting one source through three different filters makes three different vowels. This is because **multiplying the source by the filters gives new vowels**. The output is the source shaped by the filter. The source gives the raw sound energy. The filter decides how much of that energy stays strong in each frequency range. Each filter boosts a different set of frequencies, so each one makes a different vowel.","children":[]},{"text":"With the **original filter**, F1 and F2 stay low. So the result still sounds like /u/.","children":[]},{"text":"With **F1 raised a little**, the lowest resonance goes up. So the vowel changes.","children":[]},{"text":"With **F2 raised a lot**, the second resonance goes up far. So the vowel changes in a different way, and the back rounded /u/ moves toward a more front vowel.","children":[]},{"text":"**Source-filter synthesis** pulls apart two things that normal speech combines. The voice makes the sound, and the vocal tract resonances shape what vowel it is. All three outputs have the same buzz, pitch and timing, yet they sound like different vowels. Since only the filter changed, a researcher can change the vowel just by swapping the filter. Nobody has to record each version.","children":[]}],"speech1-11":[{"text":"Formant values change from speaker to speaker, but they still describe a careful talker’s vowels pretty well. This is because the vowel a speaker means to say only **partly** decides the formant values. Three other things change them too.","children":[]},{"text":"**Anatomical features** differ between speakers of the same language. Vocal tracts differ in length and shape, and a longer one gives lower resonances overall.","children":[]},{"text":"**Different rates of speech** matter. Fast speech gives the tongue and lips less time to reach their targets.","children":[]},{"text":"**Context** matters, meaning the **phones** (particular spoken versions of speech sounds) made before and after the vowel. The tongue and lips are already heading to the next sound or coming from the last one.","children":[]},{"text":"So across 76 speakers, each of ten vowels covers a wide range of formant values, and the ranges overlap. The same F1 and F2 values can be different vowels for different speakers.","children":[]},{"text":"Even so, formant values give a pretty good description of **vowel space** (how a language’s vowels are laid out by their formant frequencies). This works best for **one talker speaking carefully**, because that talker’s vowels separate much more cleanly. So, all else being equal, you can make **reasonable approximations of vowels** by changing **steady-state formants** (the resonances in the stable middle of a vowel).","children":[]}],"speech1-12":[{"text":"The **pattern playback synthesizer** turns a painted spectrogram back into sound. This is because researchers paint bands on a clear sheet wherever they want sound energy in time and frequency. The machine then plays that painted pattern out loud. So changing the painting changes the sound.","children":[]},{"text":"The machine works by shining light through the painted pattern. A light source shines through a **cylindrical lens** onto a spinning **tone wheel**. The rings on the tone wheel make the light flicker at many frequencies at once. A lens and a **mirror** send that light onto the painted **spectrogram**. The painted parts let the matching frequencies through. A **light collector** and a **photocell** turn the light into an electrical signal. An **amplifier** and a **loudspeaker** turn that signal into sound.","children":[]},{"text":"This let researchers make controlled, speech-like sounds instead of using natural recordings. Natural recordings differ in many ways at once that nobody controls. With the machine, a researcher can change just one formant band or one transition and hear the result right away. Everything else in the pattern stays exactly the same.","children":[]},{"text":"Since only one thing changed, any change in what listeners hear comes from that one painted change. That is how series of patterns that differed in tiny steps showed how formant transitions signal consonants.","children":[]}],"speech1-13":[{"text":"Stop consonants are told apart by their **formant transitions** (the way their formants glide over time). This is because **/b/, /d/ and /g/** are made by closing the vocal tract for a moment and then opening it. The part of a /b/, /d/ or /g/ that you can hear happens **while the jaw is opening**. During that opening, the shape of the vocal tract changes fast. So the formants glide from the consonant’s position to the vowel’s position. Since the consonant is heard during this glide, its identity is in how the formants move.","children":[]},{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957)** used the **pattern playback synthesizer** to make speech-like sounds that copied these formant transitions. Each painted pattern had two formants. The **lower formant** was nearly the same in every stimulus. The **upper formant** started at a different frequency in each stimulus. It then settled at the same steady vowel level.","children":[]},{"text":"In some stimuli, the upper formant started low and **rose** into the vowel, and listeners heard /b/. In some, it was nearly **flat**, and listeners heard /d/. In others, it started high and **fell** into the vowel, and listeners heard /g/.","children":[]},{"text":"The fourteen patterns kept the vowel the same and changed only where the upper transition started. Since only the transition differed, what listeners heard could be tied directly to the transition.","children":[]}],"speech1-14":[{"text":"The speech continuum changes one thing in equal steps, so every neighboring pair of sounds differs by the same physical amount. This is because a **speech continuum** (a series of sounds that change step by step along one property) was built by **keeping F1 constant**. Only the **F2 onset frequency** (the frequency where the second formant starts its glide) was changed. It was **varied linearly** from stimulus 1 to stimulus 13, meaning it moved by the same step every time. So **adjacent stimuli differ by the same amount across the entire continuum**.","children":[]},{"text":"Along the series, the F2 glide slowly changes. At the start, F2 begins well below the vowel frequency, so it rises. As the starting point goes up, the rise gets smaller until the glide is nearly flat. Later, F2 starts above the vowel frequency, so it falls.","children":[]},{"text":"Equal steps make a fair test of whether hearing follows the sound or the categories. Some neighboring pairs fall inside one consonant category. Other pairs cross a boundary between two categories. A listener who only tracked the sound would hear every step as the same size. A listener with categorical perception would hear steps inside a category as alike and steps across a boundary as different. Since all steps are physically equal, any advantage for boundary pairs has to come from the boundary.","children":[]}],"speech1-15":[{"text":"If perception were not categorical, the labels would change slowly and smoothly from /b/ to /d/ to /g/. This is because a simple **ideal observer model** assumes perception is **non-categorical**. An ideal observer model is a prediction of how a listener would respond under one idea of how perception works. That means hearing follows the sound gradually, with no sharp category boundaries. The model is a baseline, so real data can be compared with it.","children":[]},{"text":"The first prediction is that **categorization gets “blurry” as you move away from category prototypes**. A **prototype** is the clearest example of a category. The ends of the continuum are /b/ and /g/, with /d/ in the middle. Between these points, **stimulus ambiguity should scale with the distance from the prototype**. So the farther a sound is from the /b/ prototype, the less it sounds like /b/ and the more it sounds like /d/.","children":[]},{"text":"**Stimulus 2** would be labeled **75% /b/ and 25% /d/**. This is because it sits a quarter of the way from the /b/ prototype toward the /d/ prototype.","children":[]},{"text":"Under this idea, **labeling** changes smoothly. The /b/ labels go down in a straight slope to 0 at stimulus 6, where /d/ reaches its peak. The /d/ labels then go down to 0 at stimulus 10, where /g/ reaches the top. Halfway between two prototypes, the two labels are equally likely. No label ever jumps suddenly.","children":[]}],"speech1-16":[{"text":"If perception were linear, people would be equally good at telling apart every pair, no matter how they label the sounds. This is because a **linear** ideal observer hears equal sound differences as equally big. So it predicts that **discrimination performance should be independent of labeling**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Labeling** asks a listener to name a sound as /b/, /d/ or /g/. **Discrimination** only asks whether two sounds are the **same or different**. These are two separate tasks, even when they use the same sounds.","children":[]},{"text":"Labeling **has to have a “peaked” function**. This is because there are three categories and listeners have to pick between labels. So each category’s labels go up toward its prototype and go down away from it. These peaks come from how the labeling task is set up.","children":[]},{"text":"Discrimination works in a different way. The pairs are two steps apart, from **1 and 3** through **9 and 11**, so every pair has the same physical gap. If people heard F2 onset differences equally well across the whole continuum, accuracy would be the same for every pair. So the prediction is a flat line, at about 0.8.","children":[]},{"text":"A **flat** result would mean category boundaries do not affect hearing differences. A **peaked** result would mean the boundaries make some differences easier to hear.","children":[]}],"speech1-17":[{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957)** found that labels switch suddenly and that people are best at telling sounds apart right at the switch. This pattern is **categorical perception**, and it is very different from the linear prediction. Listeners labeled each sound on the /b/, /d/ and /g/ continuum. They also judged pairs that were **one step**, **two steps** or **three steps** apart.","children":[]},{"text":"The labels switched **sharply**. Stimuli 1 through about 3 were labeled /b/ almost 100% of the time. Around stimuli 4 and 5, /b/ dropped steeply and /d/ climbed to nearly 100%. /d/ stayed on top through about stimulus 8. Around stimuli 9 and 10, /d/ dropped steeply and /g/ rose to near 100%. The narrow spot where the label flips is the **category boundary**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Listeners were best at discriminating sounds near the category boundaries**. Accuracy peaked at the /b/ to /d/ boundary and again at the /d/ to /g/ boundary. Pairs inside one category were close to **chance** (the score you get by guessing). Bigger steps raised accuracy overall, but the peaks stayed at the boundaries.","children":[]},{"text":"Liberman and colleagues also predicted discrimination from the labels, assuming people only hear a difference when they give two different labels. This prediction matched much of the real pattern. Real accuracy was often a little higher, especially inside categories. So listeners still hear some sound differences beyond the labels.","children":[]}],"speech1-18":[],"speech1-19":[{"text":"Each listener is best at telling sounds apart near the point where that listener’s own label switches. This is because students in prior years were tested on a **beach to peach continuum**, where the words differ only in /b/ or /p/. In **identification**, each listener labeled every sound from 1 to 9. In **discrimination**, each listener judged pairs two steps apart, from 1 and 3 through 7 and 9.","children":[]},{"text":"The first listener says one word for the early sounds and then **switches to the other word** around the middle. Answers are mixed only in a small area near the switch. So the sound changes slowly, but the label changes in a narrow spot.","children":[]},{"text":"That first listener is best at the pair 4 and 6 and the pair 5 and 7. These pairs cross that listener’s boundary. Pairs well inside one category are hard to tell apart, even though the physical gap is the same. The pair 6 and 8 falls in between, because stimulus 6 itself got mixed labels.","children":[]},{"text":"The second listener **switches later** along the continuum. That listener is best at telling sounds apart near that later switch.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the two switches are in different places, different people can have their boundaries at different points. For each person, the best discrimination still lands near that person’s **own identification boundary**.","children":[]}],"speech1-20":[{"text":"Not everyone has the same category boundary, but people are generally best at telling apart sounds they hear as different categories. This is because a sound near the beach to peach boundary for one listener can be well inside a category for another listener. So a pair that crosses one person’s boundary may stay inside one category for someone else. When people’s boundaries differ, averaging the group can flatten the peak at the boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"**In general, discrimination is best for stimuli perceived as being in different categories**. Listeners can miss a physical difference between two versions of the same phoneme. The same size of difference is much easier to hear when the two sounds are heard as different words.","children":[]},{"text":"The discrimination test had **only “different” trials**, so the lab could be done in a short time. Every pair had two sounds that really were physically different. So the correct answer on every trial was **“different,”** if the listener could hear the difference. Saying “same” meant missing a real difference.","children":[]},{"text":"Accuracy here is how often a listener heard the pair as different. Someone who always said “different” would get a perfect score. Since every pair really differed, low accuracy on pairs inside one category means listeners often missed differences that were really there.","children":[]}],"speech1-21":[{"text":"The data from prior years bounce around because real experiments are noisy and this one had weak spots. This is because of the one iron-clad law of experimental research, which is that **stuff happens**. Even with a carefully built continuum, answers can jump up and down.","children":[]},{"text":"For the **bear to pear** continuum, the labels switch fairly clearly from one word to the other. But discrimination jumps between 0 and 1 from one pair to the next. It even misses a pair that crosses the boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"Another listener’s **beach to peach** data bounce even more. The labels zigzag instead of switching once. Discrimination jumps between high and low from pair to pair.","children":[]},{"text":"Identification (which word a listener picks) and discrimination (whether a difference is heard) can vary in different ways across trials and people. So a single spike or dip in discrimination reflects noise in a few answers.","children":[]},{"text":"The experiment was not the best in two ways, and it could be done differently. Only “different” trials were used, so nothing measured how often people said “different” when the sounds were identical. The short session gave each listener **few trials per pair**, so one answer could move accuracy a lot. A better version would add trials with identical sounds and more trials for each pair. Then you could check whether better discrimination lines up with where the labels change across the whole continuum.","children":[]}],"speech1-22":[{"text":"Being worse at hearing some differences helps because it keeps word recognition stable. **Categorical perception** makes a listener worse at hearing some differences between sounds, and that looks like a weakness because the listener throws away sound detail.","children":[]},{"text":"It helps because every time someone says a phoneme, it comes out physically a little different. Speakers, speaking rates and neighboring sounds all change it. If every small change were heard as a new sound, everyday versions of one word would sound like unrelated sounds. Being less sensitive inside a category lets all those versions count as **one phoneme**.","children":[]},{"text":"Differences **between categories** stay easy to hear. So the differences that change one word into another are kept. Differences inside a category are ignored, because they do not change the word.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the listener ignores the small differences that do not matter, the listener can focus on the differences that change meaning. That is the advantage of being worse at hearing some differences.","children":[]}],"speech1-23":[],"speech1-24":[{"text":"Researchers test **categorical perception in infants** by watching a behavior that changes when the infant notices something new. This is because infants cannot name sounds, so they cannot do a labeling task. But infants react to new things, and that reaction can be measured. The experimenter controls exactly which sound difference the infant hears.","children":[]},{"text":"The **conditioned head turn procedure** comes from Janet Werker’s infant studies lab. An infant learns to turn toward a sound when the sound changes. A correct turn gets an interesting visual reward, such as a moving toy. Once the infant learns this, a head turn after a change means the infant noticed the change.","children":[]},{"text":"The results that matter here come from **high-amplitude sucking**. The infant sucks on a pacifier hooked to a recording device, and strong sucks make a sound play. When the same sound keeps repeating, the infant gets used to it and sucks less. Getting used to a repeated sound is **habituation**. If a new sound makes the infant suck **more again**, the infant noticed the change.","children":[]},{"text":"Since these methods need no labels or words, they work for infants who are still months away from talking.","children":[]}],"speech1-25":[{"text":"**Eimas and colleagues (1971)** found that **4-month-old infants** notice a voicing change only when it crosses the adult category boundary. We know this because they measured **high-amplitude sucking** to sounds that differed in **voice onset time**, or **VOT**. VOT is the delay between releasing a consonant and the vocal folds starting to vibrate. Adults hear a short VOT as a voiced consonant like /b/. Adults hear a longer VOT as a voiceless consonant like /p/. With a VOT of +100 milliseconds, voicing starts much later than with +10 milliseconds.","children":[]},{"text":"Infants heard one synthetic syllable again and again until their sucking slowed down. Then the sound changed or stayed the same. In group **20D**, the VOT changed from **+20 to +40 milliseconds**, which crosses the adult boundary between two consonants. In group **20S**, the VOT also changed by **20 milliseconds**, from −20 to 0 or from +60 to +80. Both of those sounds are in the **same** adult category. In group **0**, the control group kept hearing the same sound.","children":[]},{"text":"In every group, sucking went up at first and then down as the sound got familiar. In 20D, sucking **jumped back up** after the change. In 20S, sucking kept going down with little recovery. In 0, sucking kept going down steadily.","children":[]},{"text":"Both changes were the same 20 milliseconds. So the only reason 20D was different is that its change crossed the boundary. Since infants noticed only the crossing change, they already hear voicing **categorically** at four months.","children":[]}],"speech1-26":[{"text":"English-learning infants lose the ability to hear some non-English sound differences during their first year. This is called **perceptual narrowing** (experience tuning hearing to the sounds of the language around you). Along the way, infants get worse at some differences from other languages.","children":[]},{"text":"**Werker and Tees (1984)** tested two **non-English contrasts**. The **Hindi contrast** is two “ta”-like sounds that English does not tell apart. The **Salish contrast** is “ki” versus “qi” in Nthlakampx, a Salish language, which English also does not use. They measured the percentage of infants who reached a **discrimination criterion** at each age.","children":[]},{"text":"English-learning infants got worse with age. At **6 to 8 months**, about 95% passed for Hindi and about 80% for Salish. At **8 to 10 months**, about 67% passed for Hindi and about 52% for Salish. At **10 to 12 months**, only about 20% passed for Hindi and about 10% for Salish.","children":[]},{"text":"**Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants** passed close to 100% of the time for the contrast in their own language. So the drop does not happen just from getting older. It depends on which language the infant hears.","children":[]},{"text":"This is described as **perceptual reorganization during the first year of life**. Young infants can hear these nonnative differences. Since English never uses them, English-learning infants mostly stop hearing them by about their first birthday.","children":[]}],"speech1-27":[{"text":"The “very compelling narrative” says categorical perception is a special language ability that babies have early and then tune to their own language. This is because the earlier findings seemed to support three claims.","children":[]},{"text":"The first claim is that speech perception depends on **categorical perception abilities that are a special property of the language system**. So the machinery that sorts sounds into categories would be built just for language.","children":[]},{"text":"The second claim is that these abilities are **present very early in development**. The infant sucking and head-turn studies suggest they are there long before children talk.","children":[]},{"text":"The third claim is that children **start off as “citizens of the world,”** able to hear **all** contrasts. They then **rapidly tune their perceptual systems to their specific language**. “Citizens of the world” means a baby could learn any human language and is ready to hear the differences in every one. Tuning means the differences in the baby’s own language stay, while differences from other languages fade, as in the Werker and Tees results.","children":[]},{"text":"So the narrative explains both where babies start (the **starting state**) and how they change (**developmental change**). It says babies start with broad language abilities and mostly narrow down from there. Each early result seems to fit this story.","children":[]}],"speech1-28":[{"text":"The compelling narrative has **some issues**, and they weaken the idea that categorical perception is special to language and starts out universal. This is because each issue goes against one part of the narrative.","children":[]},{"text":"Categorical perception is **not limited to language**. It also happens with sounds that are not speech. So it does not look special to language.","children":[]},{"text":"Categorical perception is **not limited to humans**. Animals also hear speech sounds categorically. So it does not look special to humans.","children":[]},{"text":"Categorical perception is **task-dependent**. How categorical the results look depends on what the listener is asked to do. Picking between labels makes answers look more categorical than a task that measures small sound differences.","children":[]},{"text":"**Infant speech perception** is **not actually “universal.”** Some contrasts are harder for infants to hear than others right from the start. So development includes differences infants start with, not just changes from experience.","children":[]},{"text":"Since all four issues hold, the strong claim fails. That claim says every speech difference starts out equally easy to hear in a system built only for language.","children":[]}],"speech1-29":[{"text":"**Cutting and Rosner (1974)** found categorical perception for sounds that are not speech. This is because they used **non-speech sounds** that differed in **rise time** (how fast a sound reaches full loudness after it starts). A **rapid rise time** sounds **plucked**, like a plucked string. A **slow rise time** sounds **bowed**, like a bowed string. Pluck and bow are names for two kinds of sound starts, not phonemes.","children":[]},{"text":"The sounds included **sawtooth waves**, **sine waves** and **speech syllables**, made with short and long rise times such as 10 and 70 milliseconds. With a 10-millisecond rise, the sound is at full strength almost right away. With a 70-millisecond rise, the sound builds up slowly.","children":[]},{"text":"For the sawtooth waves, short rise times were called pluck almost 100% of the time. Long rise times were called bow almost 100% of the time. The label switched steeply around 30 to 50 milliseconds.","children":[]},{"text":"For telling apart pairs 10 milliseconds apart, accuracy stayed around 55 to 60% for most pairs, just a bit above chance. Accuracy **peaked near 75%** for pairs around 30 to 50 milliseconds, right where the label switched.","children":[]},{"text":"Since this happened for plucked and bowed sounds, the link between a label switch and better discrimination does not need language at all.","children":[]}],"speech1-30":[{"text":"**Jusczyk and colleagues (1977)** found that infants also hear non-speech sounds categorically. This is because they used **high-amplitude sucking** with **infants** and **non-speech sounds**. Infants heard one sound over and over until their sucking slowed down. Then the sound was shifted in one of three ways.","children":[]},{"text":"With **no change**, sucking stayed low.","children":[]},{"text":"With a change **within categories**, sucking also stayed low. So the infants showed no sign of noticing that change.","children":[]},{"text":"With a change **between categories**, sucking jumped back up. It then went down again as the new sound got familiar. So the infants noticed that change.","children":[]},{"text":"This is the same pattern **Eimas and colleagues (1971)** found with speech sounds. But infants cannot be using what they know about words or phonemes when they hear plucked and bowed sounds. So their categorical response has to come from more general hearing. Since general hearing works this way, the same process could also explain their categorical response to speech.","children":[]}],"speech1-31":[{"text":"**Chinchillas** hear speech sounds categorically, just like humans. **Ohlemiller and colleagues (1999)** found this, following the earlier work of **Kuhl and Miller (1975)**. The animals were trained with **operant conditioning** (learning where rewards change how often a behavior happens). Each chinchilla learned that either the **0-millisecond lag** sound or the **80-millisecond lag** sound was an **SD** for food. **SD** stands for **discriminative stimulus** (a signal that a response will be rewarded). The lag is **voice onset time**. Humans hear a 0-millisecond lag as /ga/ and an 80-millisecond lag as /ka/.","children":[]},{"text":"After training, chinchillas and humans were tested on the VOTs in between, along the **/ga/ to /ka/** continuum. Short VOTs were called /ga/ almost every time. Long VOTs were almost never called /ga/. The switch happened in a narrow range, mostly between about 30 and 70 milliseconds. The chinchilla curves had the same steep shape as the human curves.","children":[]},{"text":"The study also changed the **burst** (the short noise when a stop consonant is released). It also changed the **aspiration noise** (the breathy noise after a voiceless release). Making them louder moved the boundary to shorter VOTs, made the switch steeper, or both. This happened for one chinchilla and one human, so both species reacted in a similar way.","children":[]},{"text":"Since animals without human language sort speech sounds into categories, this argues against the view that categorical perception is special to human language.","children":[]}],"speech1-32":[{"text":"Some sound differences are hard for infants to hear at first, even ones in their own language, and experience makes them easier. This is because some contrasts are **less salient** (less easy to notice) than others. The contrasts here differ in **place of articulation** (where in the mouth a sound is made). In **[ma]**, the nasal is made with the lips. In **[na]**, it is made with the tongue near the ridge behind the teeth. In **[ŋa]**, it is made with the back of the tongue against the soft palate, like the sound at the end of “sing.”","children":[]},{"text":"Researchers measured **looking time** on **same** trials, where the syllable stayed the same, and **change** trials, where it switched. Longer looking on change trials means the infant noticed the change.","children":[]},{"text":"English-hearing infants at **4 to 5 months** looked longer on change trials for the native **[ma] and [na]** contrast, about 5.4 seconds against 3.3 seconds. For **[na] and [ŋa]**, they looked about the same, about 4.2 seconds against 4.5 seconds. So they did not hear that change, and English never starts a syllable with [ŋ].","children":[]},{"text":"**Filipino-learning infants** have [na] and [ŋa] in their own language. At **6 to 8 months**, they looked about 3.8 seconds on both kinds of trials, so they did not hear the change yet. By **10 to 12 months**, they looked about 4.8 seconds on change trials and about 3.3 seconds on same trials.","children":[]},{"text":"Since hearing the language made this contrast easier, development includes **gains** as well as losses.","children":[]}],"speech2-01":[],"speech2-02":[{"text":"**Klatt (1979)** describes a very old and very general model in which speech is recognized in a row of stages. This is because each stage takes the output of the stage before it and changes it.","children":["The **speech waveform** goes into the **peripheral auditory system**, which does a **spectral analysis** (the **cochlea** splits the sound into its frequencies).","**Acoustic property detectors** respond to particular properties of the sound.","**Phonetic feature detectors** figure out how likely features such as voicing are at each moment.","**Segmental analysis** puts the features together into a sequence of speech segments.","**Lexical search** compares that sequence with stored words and comes up with **lexical hypotheses** (candidate words)."]},{"text":"**Categorical perception** fits this model. This is because **information is lost** somewhere between the **analog signal** from the cochlea and the **activation of phonemes**. Many physically different sounds end up as the same phoneme. This **loss of information is helpful**, because it lets the listener reach higher levels of representation. So the model assumes that to identify a word, you **first identify its phonemes**.","children":[]},{"text":"Two problems make this simple view harder to keep.","children":["The first is **cascaded processing** (partial information moves on to the next level before the earlier decision is done).","The second is **feedback** (a higher level, such as words, sends influence back down to a lower level, such as phonemes)."]}],"speech2-03":[{"text":"On the beach and peach continuum, the same size change in sound is easy to hear only when it crosses the boundary. This is because the two words differ in **voice onset time**, or **VOT**. VOT is the delay between opening the lips and the start of vocal fold vibration. Beach starts with /b/ and peach starts with /p/. VOT gets shorter in equal steps from “peach” to “beach”. A long delay sounds like peach, and a short delay sounds like beach.","children":[]},{"text":"In **identification**, one listener's labels switch suddenly.","children":["Stimuli 1 through 5 always get one label.","Stimulus 6 gets mixed labels, near 0.33.","Stimuli 7 through 9 always get the other label.","The **category boundary** is the narrow spot where the label switches."]},{"text":"In **discrimination**, the same listener can only tell pairs apart across the boundary.","children":["Pairs inside one category are almost never heard as different.","Pairs 4 and 6 and 5 and 7 cross the boundary, so they are heard as different every time.","Pair 6 and 8 is in between, near 0.33, and pair 7 and 9 drops back to 0."]},{"text":"Since a step across the boundary changes the word and a step inside a category does not, equal VOT steps sound very different.","children":[]}],"speech2-04":[{"text":"How does categorical perception happen? One answer is a **threshold model**, where categorical perception happens at phoneme identification. This is because activity only passes from one level to the next once it crosses a **threshold**. A threshold is a set level that has to be reached first. Activity below the threshold can go up and down without changing the next level at all.","children":[]},{"text":"Take the voicing evidence for a sound between /b/ and /p/.","children":["At the **feature level**, the voicing evidence goes up and down between a **+voiced** threshold and a **−voiced** threshold.","At the **phoneme level**, /b/ and /p/ both stay silent while the evidence is still going back and forth.","When the evidence finally crosses the +voiced threshold, **/b/ turns on all at once**."]},{"text":"An ambiguous sound either crosses a threshold or it does not. So the word level gets a finished /b/ or a finished /p/. The earlier uncertainty stays behind at the feature level. Two different sounds that both become /b/ look exactly the same to word recognition. Since the switch is all or none, categorical perception happens right at the phoneme level.","children":[]}],"speech2-05":[{"text":"How does categorical perception happen in a **cascaded model**? It might happen later on. It might even be an **artifact of the task structure** (a result made by how something is measured, not by the process being studied). This is because activity is passed forward continuously over time, while decisions are still forming.","children":[]},{"text":"Feature activity and phoneme activity go up and down together.","children":["When evidence for **+voiced** grows, **/b/** activation grows.","When the evidence moves toward **−voiced**, activation moves toward **/p/**.","When the evidence is mixed, /b/ and /p/ each get some activation, matching how much evidence each one has."]},{"text":"So graded activation at the phoneme level passes graded activation to words. A sound near the boundary **partly activates both beach and peach**. Two sounds that both get labeled /b/ can still differ in how much they supported /p/ along the way.","children":[]},{"text":"The answer still looks categorical because of the task. An identification task makes the listener click beach or peach, even if both words were partly active. Since the response choices are categorical, the final answer is categorical too.","children":[]}],"speech2-06":[{"text":"**Making furniture** is a way to think about **thresholded activity**. This is because each operation takes one specific input and turns it into one specific output.","children":["The **lumber mill** turns **trees** into **lumber**.","The **furniture factory** turns **lumber** into **unassembled furniture**.","The **IKEA customer** turns **unassembled furniture** into finished **furniture**."]},{"text":"Each step has to wait for a finished product it can use. Trees have to become lumber before any furniture is made. Lumber has to become furniture parts before anyone can put them together. No part of one stage's work moves on until the whole job is done.","children":[]},{"text":"**Thresholded processing** works the same way. A higher level waits until the lower level hands it the representation it needs. Since nothing moves until it is finished, information moves forward **only as a completed product**.","children":[]}],"speech2-07":[{"text":"In the furniture example, each **“layer”** can only take an input that the layer before it has **completely transformed**. This is because the factory has to wait for the lumber shipment before it can start making anything. Many computer programs work this way. A lower stage can keep working while the higher stage just sits and waits.","children":[]},{"text":"In speech, a strict threshold account means **word processing depends on an identified phoneme**. A partly figured-out sound stays at the lower level until it is identified as a phoneme. Then the word level gets only the finished segment.","children":[]},{"text":"This setup also decides **what information is lost**. When different sounds become the same phoneme, their earlier differences never reach word recognition. That helps the listener treat varied sounds the same way. Since only the finished output is passed on, later stages never find out how uncertain the earlier stages were.","children":[]}],"speech2-08":[{"text":"A **food web** is a way to think about **cascaded processing**. This is because its **“levels” continuously interact** with one another. The desert food web connects desert plants, insects, kangaroo rats, small lizards, large lizards, scorpions, snakes, desert foxes and hawks by who eats whom.","children":["A drought that cuts down the plants **immediately** affects the health of kangaroo rats.","The change in kangaroo rats then affects the snakes that eat them.","No animal waits for the level below it to be finished before it is affected."]},{"text":"Cascaded processing works the same way. When the feature evidence changes, phoneme activity changes right away. When phoneme activity changes, the support for words changes right away. So each level reacts to partial information as it comes in.","children":[]},{"text":"The food web also has clear **feedback loops**. What does adding more hawks do? More hawks change the numbers of snakes, lizards and rats. Those changes then affect the hawks’ own food supply. In a recognition model, **feedback** is influence going from a higher level back down to a lower level.","children":[]},{"text":"Cascading and feedback are not the same thing. Cascading sends developing evidence **forward**. Feedback lets a higher level **change an earlier representation**.","children":[]}],"speech2-09":[{"text":"**Phoneme identification for word recognition** works more like the food web than the lumber mill, so it is **cascaded** and not **thresholded**. This is because listeners' eye movements show a competing word getting support before the final choice. Only a cascaded system predicts that. The two accounts make different predictions before the final choice.","children":["In a **thresholded account**, word recognition starts only after the phoneme decision is finished.","In a **cascaded account**, a word can get support while the phoneme is still uncertain."]},{"text":"Take two sounds that are both labeled **/b/**, where one sounds a bit more like **/p/**.","children":["A thresholded system turns both into the same finished /b/. So pear should **not compete** with bear.","A cascaded system passes along more /p/ support for the less clear sound. So pear should **compete with bear for a while**."]},{"text":"Both accounts can predict the same final word. They only differ before the final choice. Since that is where they differ, the deciding test has to measure **temporary support for a competing word** during recognition.","children":[]}],"speech2-10":[{"text":"**McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)** tested whether phoneme identification is thresholded or cascaded by tracking eye movements. They looked for **gradient effects of within-category VOT on lexical access**.","children":["**Lexical access** means getting a word out of memory during recognition.","**Gradient** means changing by degrees.","**Within-category VOT** means differences in voice onset time between sounds that are all heard as the same phoneme."]},{"text":"Listeners identified sounds from a continuum between /b/ and /p/ in a **“visual world” display**. **Eye-tracking** recorded where they looked among the pictures while they heard speech. Each display had a **target**, such as pear, and a **competitor**, such as bear. It also had two unrelated **fillers**, a lamp and a ship. The target and competitor differ only in their first consonant.","children":[]},{"text":"This method works because eye movements happen fast and continuously while a word is being recognized. Listeners look at objects as they think about them, often before they know they have decided. So a listener can click bear after a quick look at the pear. Since the looks come before the click, the number and timing of looks show **how active the competitor was**.","children":[]}],"speech2-11":[{"text":"The clicks alone make perception look completely categorical. This is because participants used the **mouse to select** whichever of the pictures best matched what they heard, such as a “bear” or a “pear”. Each trial had one sound from the VOT continuum and the four pictures. The click shows which word the listener finally settled on.","children":[]},{"text":"The clicks gave a **typical, step-like identification function**.","children":["From 0 to about 15 milliseconds of VOT, /p/ responses stay near 0, so listeners almost always clicked bear.","Between about 15 and 20 milliseconds, /p/ responses jump up steeply.","From about 20 to 40 milliseconds, /p/ responses stay near 1, so listeners almost always clicked pear.","The **category boundary** is at **17.5 milliseconds**, plus or minus 0.83 milliseconds."]},{"text":"On its own, this sudden switch would seem to support the threshold model. But a click only records the word chosen at the very end. Eye movements record which other words got support before that choice. Since the two measure different moments, a sharp click curve can still go along with gradual differences in competition.","children":[]}],"speech2-12":[{"text":"**Eye movements reveal that ambiguity is gradient**. There are **more looks to the competitor close to the boundary**. This is because a sound near the boundary keeps the other word active longer, even when the final click is the same.","children":[]},{"text":"The comparison only uses trials with the same final click.","children":["**When listeners clicked bear**, the measure is looks to the **pear**, at VOTs of 0, 5, 10 and 15 milliseconds. Looks to the pear go up after the word starts, peak around 400 to 600 milliseconds, and then drop as bear wins.","The 15 millisecond sound, closest to the boundary, gets more looks to the pear, and they last longer than for 0 milliseconds.","**When listeners clicked pear**, the measure is looks to the **bear**, at VOTs of 20, 25, 30, 35 and 40 milliseconds. The 20 millisecond sound, just past the boundary, gets the most looks to the bear. The 40 millisecond sound, the clearest /p/, gets the fewest."]},{"text":"Since every trial in a comparison ended with the same click, the differences cannot come from listeners changing their answer. VOT mostly changes the **duration of activation** (how long the competitor stays active). So the system keeps track of how ambiguous each sound was. A threshold model would throw that information away.","children":[]}],"speech2-13":[{"text":"Looks to the competitor go up as the VOT gets closer to the boundary, from both sides. This is because sounds near the **category boundary** give more support to the rival word.","children":[]},{"text":"The comparison runs across the whole **VOT** range, with the boundary in the middle.","children":["**On the bear side**, listeners clicked bear, and the measure is how often they looked at the pear.","**On the pear side**, listeners clicked pear, and the measure is how often they looked at the bear."]},{"text":"Looks were measured in two time windows.","children":["The **early** window is 300 to 1100 milliseconds after the word starts.","The **late** window is 1100 to 1900 milliseconds after the word starts."]},{"text":"Both windows show the same pattern.","children":["On the bear side, late looks to the pear go up from about 0.02 at 0 milliseconds to about 0.045 near 15 milliseconds.","On the pear side, late looks to the bear are about 0.04 near 20 milliseconds and drop to about 0.01 by 40 milliseconds.","Early looks are higher overall, but they follow the same pattern. The rise is gradual, not sudden."]},{"text":"Since the effect shows up in both windows, uncertainty about the first sound affects looking through the whole recognition process. A sound can support the winning word and still support its rival.","children":[]}],"speech2-14":[{"text":"The eye-movement result fits the idea that **activation flows in a cascaded way from features to phonemes**. This is because even sounds that are identified as /b/ **100% of the time** can differ in how strongly the **“voiced” feature** is activated. A clear /b/ turns on voicing strongly. A /b/ near the boundary turns it on only weakly.","children":[]},{"text":"/b/ and /p/ share almost all their features.","children":["Both are **bilabial** (the two lips close).","Both are **stops** (the air is fully blocked and then let go).","A sound that is obviously **+bilabial** and **+stop** supports /b/ and /p/ equally.","Only voicing tells them apart, so weak voicing makes a **momentary ambiguity between /b/ and /p/**.","That ambiguity shows up in the **eye movements** while the participant decides where to click."]},{"text":"The **TRACE** model shows this cascade with three layers.","children":["The **feature layer** takes a **pseudo-spectral input representation** (a simplified version of the sound’s frequencies over time). Its detectors are POW, VOC, DIFF, ACU, GRD, VOI and BUR, for power, vocalic, diffuseness, acuteness, gradualness of onset, voicing and burst.","The **phoneme layer** has units such as /b/, /p/, /r/ and /t/, repeated at every point in time.","The **word layer** has word units, also repeated across time.","Activity passes upward, and units in the same layer compete through **inhibition**."]}],"speech2-15":[{"text":"**Andruski and colleagues (1994)** found that **partial activation can reach all the way to the lexical level** (the level of stored words and their meanings). We know this because they got **semantic priming** (a faster response to a word after hearing a related word) even from **nonwords**.","children":["The nonword “beace” primes “war”. Beace is peace with a voiced first consonant, and peace is related to war.","The nonword “ging” primes “queen”. Ging is king with a voiced first consonant, and king is related to queen.","In **word to word** items, the changed prime is another real word, so “bill” primes “drug” because bill is a changed form of pill."]},{"text":"Participants did a **lexical decision task** (deciding as fast as possible whether a target is a real word). Primes were **unaltered**, had VOT cut by **one third**, had it cut by **two thirds**, or were **unrelated**. A response faster than after an unrelated prime counts as priming.","children":[]},{"text":"The response times went like this.","children":["Unrelated primes gave the slowest responses, near 950 to 970 milliseconds.","Unaltered primes gave much faster ones, around 810 to 860 milliseconds.","With one third cut, responses stayed fast, around 825 to 855 milliseconds.","With two thirds cut, word to nonword items stayed near 820 milliseconds. Word to word items slowed to about 890 milliseconds, because the changed prime also partly activates a competing real word."]},{"text":"Since beace matches peace closely enough to partly activate it, **incomplete or imperfect input can still activate words**. That activation spreads to meaning and speeds up the response to war, which fits **cascaded processing**.","children":[]}],"speech2-16":[{"text":"A **thresholded model** has a hard time explaining the bear and pear eye-tracking result, so the result is evidence for **cascaded processing**. A **minimal pair** is two words that differ in one sound, such as bear and pear. This is because looks to the other member of the minimal pair go up gradually toward the boundary. That happens even when the final click is the same, so it comes from graded activation inside one category.","children":[]},{"text":"In a thresholded model, graded feature activity never gets to the phoneme level.","children":["Feature activation goes back and forth between +voiced and −voiced.","Phoneme activation stays flat until /b/ suddenly turns on.","So every sound in a category sends the **same finished phoneme** to the word level.","Then word-level competition would be the same for every /b/, and nothing could make competitor looks change gradually."]},{"text":"In a cascaded model, graded information does get to the word level. More support for the competing phoneme passes on more support for its word. Since that support grows near the boundary, competitor looks grow near the boundary too.","children":[]}],"speech2-17":[{"text":"The **TRACE** model also has **feedback from the lexical layer to the phoneme layer**. This means activity goes up from phonemes to words and also back down from words to phonemes. **Lexical feedback** (an active word sends support back to its own phonemes) works one step at a time. An unclear sound partly activates a word. Then that word strengthens the reading of the sound that fits it.","children":[]},{"text":"So influence goes both ways.","children":["The sound evidence changes which words are active.","The active words change the balance between possible phonemes while the phoneme decision is still forming.","This helps listeners deal with noisy or ambiguous input."]},{"text":"**Feedback is not the same as cascading**.","children":["Cascading sends incomplete evidence forward, from features to phonemes and from phonemes to words.","Feedback sends influence from words back down to phonemes.","One interactive model can have both."]},{"text":"The reason to add feedback comes from experiments where knowing words changes how sounds are heard, such as the **Ganong effect**.","children":[]}],"speech2-18":[{"text":"**Ganong (1980)** tested whether knowing which sound sequences are real words changes how an ambiguous sound is heard. This is the **Ganong effect**. The line “Gisses and kifts for Valentine’s day” plays on it by swapping the voicing of the first sounds in “kisses and gifts”.","children":[]},{"text":"He made **word and nonword pairs** such as “corpse/gorpse”.","children":["Hearing /k/ makes corpse, which is a real word.","Hearing /g/ makes gorpse, which is not a word.","The continua crossed the phoneme boundary in **5 millisecond steps** of VOT."]},{"text":"This setup works because it combines an **uncertain sound** with a **difference in being a word**. The rest of the word, “orpse”, stays the same at every step. So the ending tells the listener which first sound would make a real word. If word knowledge matters, an unclear first consonant should be heard more often as the sound that makes a word. Since word knowledge has the most room to act where the sound is unclear, the important test is the **ambiguous middle** of the continuum.","children":[]}],"speech2-19":[{"text":"One prediction is a **late “correction” process**, where the listener categorizes the sound as usual but then leans toward reporting a word. This is because in this account **categorical perception works as usual**, and the word knowledge only comes in at the response stage. The result is a **bias to report having heard a word**.","children":[]},{"text":"Three kinds of continua are compared.","children":["In **word-nonword** continua, the voiced end makes a word.","In **nonword-word** continua, the voiceless end makes a word.","In **neutral** continua, neither end has a word advantage."]},{"text":"A bias added after perception pushes answers toward the word by the same amount everywhere.","children":["For word-nonword, voiced answers stay near 0.25 at the long VOT end and never fall to 0.","For nonword-word, voiced answers start near 0.75 at the short VOT end, not at 1.","So even a clear sound would sometimes be reported as the word."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** (the difference in voiced answers between the two word conditions) would be **the same size at every step**, near 0.25. Since the bias only nudges the reported answer, perception and word knowledge stay separate.","children":[]}],"speech2-20":[{"text":"A second prediction is that **word knowledge shifts the category boundary**. Then the word effect should be **strongest for the items closest to the boundary**. This is because those are the sounds where knowing the word can help clear up which first phoneme it was.","children":[]},{"text":"In this account, word knowledge changes where the listener switches from one phoneme to the other. Near an unclear start, the reading that makes a real word gets extra support. So the categorization itself changes, not just the reported answer.","children":[]},{"text":"The conditions should only differ in the middle.","children":["At both ends, the sound is clear, so voiced answers start near 1 and end near 0 in every condition.","Near the boundary, the word-nonword condition switches later, and the nonword-word condition switches earlier."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** makes a **peak**. It is near 0 at the ends and goes up to about 0.35 for the items next to the boundary. At the ends, the strong sound evidence already settles the consonant, so word knowledge has little room to help.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the two accounts differ in shape, a **flat effect** points to a reporting bias, and a **peaked effect** points to a boundary shift.","children":[]}],"speech2-21":[{"text":"**Ganong’s results** match the **second model**, the boundary shift, very closely. This is because the word effect was biggest right at the boundary.","children":["At the short and long ends of the VOT continuum, the word-nonword and nonword-word conditions give almost the same answers.","Near the boundary, the two conditions pull apart the most.","The **lexical effect** goes from about 0.1 at the ends up to a peak near **0.3** at the boundary."]},{"text":"A peak at the boundary rules out a simple reporting bias. A reporting bias would be the same size everywhere. Here word knowledge matters most where the sound evidence is weakest. At the boundary, the word that fits tips the balance. At the ends, the sound decides the answer by itself.","children":[]},{"text":"Ganong raised several ideas for how this could happen. He focused on different kinds of **guessing strategies**, where listeners use word knowledge only when they are unsure of the sound.","children":[]},{"text":"Later models put more weight on **feedback**. In **interactive models**, an active word sends support back to its own phonemes. So the whole word can change how an unclear part of it is heard during recognition.","children":[]}],"speech2-22":[{"text":"A **TRACE simulation of the Ganong effect** shows the activation of words and phonemes changing as an unclear start turns into the word **plug**. This is because the model records **phoneme-level and word-level activations** at four moments, called frames, one as each new sound comes in. The input starts with a sound **ambiguous between /b/ and /p/**, then /l/, the vowel /ʌ/ and /g/.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **first frame**, only the unclear first sound has arrived. Both /b/ and /p/ are a little active, at about the same level, near 0.15 to 0.2. Word activation is still very low.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **second and third frames**, /l/ and the vowel arrive.","children":["Partial /b/ evidence supports words starting with bl, and partial /p/ evidence supports words starting with pl.","**Plug**, **blood** and **blush** all go up together, because all three fit what has been heard so far.","/p/ starts to pull ahead of /b/."]},{"text":"In the **fourth frame**, the final **/g/** arrives. The /g/ fits plug but does not fit blood or blush. So **plug** becomes the most active word, at about 0.45, and the others fall away. Since plug wins, **/p/ ends up more active than /b/**.","children":[]}],"speech2-23":[{"text":"In TRACE, the final /g/ makes **“plug”** win, and plug then pushes the unclear first sound toward /p/. This is because activity builds up for plug as the input comes in, most of all when the **final /g/** arrives. The /g/ does not fit “blood” or “blush”, which were also active in Frame 3. Blood and blush fit the early part, so they were possible for a while. Once /g/ arrives, their endings do not match, so they lose support.","children":[]},{"text":"The model explains **word effects on phoneme category boundaries** with **top-down processing** (higher-level knowledge changing lower-level analysis).","children":["Activity at the lexical level sends **feedback to the phoneme level**.","The feedback gives **more activation to the phoneme that fits a word**.","Plug is the winning word, so /p/ gets the extra support."]},{"text":"Since /p/ belongs to the word the whole sequence supports, the unclear first sound ends up heard as /p/. So information at the end of a word can change how an earlier sound is heard.","children":[]}],"speech2-24":[{"text":"The **TRACE** model is a version of the **Interactive Activation model** for **written word recognition**, from **McClelland and Rumelhart (1981)**. Many of the principles are the same. This is because both models connect levels in the same way.","children":["**Feature** units connect to **letter** units, and letter units connect to **word** units such as WEAR, WORK, WORD and WEAK.","Units that fit together at different levels support each other.","Units competing in the same level inhibit each other.","Active words send support back down to their own letters."]},{"text":"Say a reader sees **W, O and R** clearly, but ink blots cover part of the last letter.","children":["The visible part of the last letter fits both **R** and **K**.","W, O and R activate words such as WORK and WORD.","Only WORK fits the last letter, so WORK becomes the strongest word.","WORK sends support back to K, so the unclear letter is **seen as K**, just like plug supports /p/ in speech."]},{"text":"The big difference is that **speech unfolds over time**. A written word is all there at once. A spoken word comes in one sound at a time. So TRACE repeats feature, phoneme and word units at every point in time. That way a word can fit the beginning, get active, and then fade when the ending does not fit.","children":[]}],"speech2-25":[{"text":"**Categorical perception** still holds, but spoken word recognition is more interactive than a lumber mill. This is because given the same physical difference, it is **harder to tell apart sounds from the same category** than sounds from different categories. Nothing about cascading or feedback changes that basic finding.","children":[]},{"text":"But two findings about the **lexical-phonetic interface** add to it.","children":["**Ambiguity about phoneme identity does affect word recognition**. Sounds with the same label still differ in how strongly they activate a competing word.","**Word-level information seems to affect phoneme categorization**. In the Ganong effect, word knowledge moves the category boundary, and TRACE does this with lexical feedback."]},{"text":"So the word recognition system **cannot just sit there waiting** for the phoneme system to finish its job. It does not work the way a factory waits for the lumber mill. Partial information moves forward while uncertainty is still being worked out. Active words send support back down at the same time. Since information flows both ways, spoken word recognition works more like the food web. Labels can be categorical while the processing behind them is graded.","children":[]}],"speech2-26":[],"speech2-27":[{"text":"**Spoken word recognition is also very fast**, and words become active while the word is still being heard. We know this because **Allopenna and colleagues (1994)** used a visual world eye-tracking task and found looks to the target starting before the word ended. Participants saw a grid of pictures and geometric shapes and followed spoken instructions to move objects.","children":[]},{"text":"The pictures were picked for how much they sound like **beaker**.","children":["**Beaker** is the **referent** (the target).","**Beetle** is a **cohort competitor** (it shares the beginning of the word).","**Speaker** is a **rhyme competitor** (it shares the ending of the word).","**Carriage** is **unrelated** (it shares no sounds)."]},{"text":"The measure is the chance of looking at each item over the first 1,000 milliseconds. On average the spoken word ends a little before 400 milliseconds.","children":["Looks to beaker and beetle go up together from around 200 milliseconds, because both fit the beginning.","Looks to beetle peak near 0.2 around 500 milliseconds and then drop once the sounds that separate the words arrive.","Looks to beaker keep climbing, past 0.8 by about 800 milliseconds and close to 1 by 1,000 milliseconds.","Looks to speaker go up later and stay small, near 0.1. Looks to carriage stay near zero."]},{"text":"Since words that match the input get active and drop out when they stop matching, recognition happens while the word unfolds.","children":[]}],"speech2-28":[{"text":"A **TRACE simulation** predicts the eye movements moment by moment. So the looks make it possible to **see cognitive processing before a final response is issued**. This is because **eye movements give an idea of the changes in “activity”** for different competitors over time. The comparison is between real looks and the looks TRACE predicts over 1,000 milliseconds.","children":["For the **referent**, real and predicted looks both go up steadily from about 300 milliseconds and get close to 1 by 1,000 milliseconds.","For the **cohort** competitor, both go up early, peak near 0.17 to 0.2 around 500 milliseconds, and then drop.","For the **rhyme** competitor, both go up later and stay small, near 0.1.","So the TRACE predictions closely match the real looks."]},{"text":"The **granularity** of the simulation is fine (it predicts activity moment by moment, not just the final choice). Since the model and the eyes both track activity over time, the two can be compared at every moment.","children":[]},{"text":"This is like the **graded effects of category goodness** in the categorical perception task. Sounds that are better or worse examples of a category make different amounts of competitor activity. That happens even when the final label is the same.","children":[]}],"sentence-01":[],"sentence-02":[{"text":"“The horse raced past the barn fell” describes **two events**. Someone raced the horse past the barn, and then the horse fell.","children":[]},{"text":"It feels broken at first even though it is grammatical. This is because most readers take **raced** as the **main verb** (the verb that says what the subject did). Then “the horse raced past the barn” looks like a whole event where the horse did the racing. When **fell** shows up, there is no place left for it. So the sentence seems to fall apart.","children":[]},{"text":"The real meaning is “The horse that was raced past the barn fell”. Here “raced past the barn” tells which horse is meant. **Fell** is the main verb, and it says what happened to that horse.","children":[]},{"text":"You can **disambiguate** it (make only one reading possible) by putting back the missing words “that was”. This is because “that was” makes raced part of the description of the horse. So raced can no longer be read as the main verb. Since understanding depends on how the words relate, knowing each word’s meaning is not enough.","children":[]},{"text":"It could be called **the sentence that launched a thousand experiments**. This is because researchers have used it again and again to study how readers build structure as words come in.","children":[]}],"sentence-03":[{"text":"The horse sentence is the classic case of the **garden path effect** (getting led into one reading early and then finding out it was wrong). The name comes from “to lead someone down the garden path”, which means to mislead someone.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because readers build structure **word by word**. They pick one analysis before the sentence is over. In the horse sentence, raced looks like the main verb. Then fell clashes with the structure already built. So the reader has to go back and read “raced past the barn” as “that was raced past the barn”. If readers waited until the end, they would never be misled.","children":[]},{"text":"The horse sentence is almost as famous as “Colorless green ideas sleep furiously”. The two are hard in different ways. “Colorless green ideas sleep furiously” is grammatical but makes no sense. The horse sentence is grammatical and makes sense, but it is very hard to process.","children":[]},{"text":"So **grammaticality** and **processing difficulty** are different things. A sentence can have a fine structure and still be hard to follow as it unfolds. A dinosaur comic jokes that nobody would say the horse sentence. The reply is that farmers might, if their horses kept falling over. The joke only works because the sentence really is grammatical.","children":[]}],"sentence-04":[{"text":"Syntactic explanations of the garden path start with a **parser** (the mental process that builds a **syntactic tree** for each sentence we hear or read). A syntactic tree records how words group into phrases and how the phrases fit together.","children":[]},{"text":"The correct tree for the horse sentence is big. The subject is a large **NP** (noun phrase). It holds “the horse” plus a shortened clause meaning “that was raced past the barn”. The words “that” and “was” are silent, and the clause has an empty subject spot. Inside it, raced takes the **PP** (prepositional phrase) “past the barn”. The main **VP** (verb phrase) is just **fell**.","children":[]},{"text":"The failed parse is small. “The horse” is a plain NP, and “raced past the barn” is the main VP. This is why fell has nowhere to attach, so the parse breaks down.","children":[]},{"text":"The parser has to work with **limited memory** and **time constraints**. This is because words come in fast, so the parser cannot wait for the end of the sentence. It also can only keep a few structures going at once. Since it works under these limits, it needs some way to deal with ambiguous sentences.","children":[]}],"sentence-05":[{"text":"The parser first prefers the smaller tree, and this strategy is called **minimal attachment**. It means fitting each new word in with the fewest new **nodes**. Nodes are points in a tree that stand for a grouping, such as an NP, a VP or a sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"This strategy saves space. Fewer nodes means less structure to build. It also means less to hold in memory at that moment.","children":[]},{"text":"The correct version of the horse sentence is much more complex. This is because it has many more nodes. It needs extra nodes for the embedded clause. It also needs nodes for the silent “that” and “was”. The failed parse just makes “raced past the barn” the main VP, which takes far fewer nodes.","children":[]},{"text":"So minimal attachment picks **raced as the main verb**. This is because that reading needs the fewest nodes. The reading where “raced past the barn” describes the horse needs an embedded relative clause. So the parser passes it over at first. When fell arrives, it needs the bigger structure. Since the parser chose the small tree, the reader has to rebuild the tree, and that is the garden path.","children":[]}],"sentence-06":[{"text":"**Late closure** is a second parsing strategy. It is a bias to keep the current clause **open** as long as possible. Keeping a clause open means adding new words to it instead of closing it and starting something new.","children":[]},{"text":"In “The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite”, the phrase “with the dynamite” joins the big verb phrase starting with “blew open”. **Frazier (1987)** gives two trees for this sentence. In the **preferred** tree, the PP attaches to the VP. This is **verb-phrase attachment**, so the dynamite is the **instrument** (the tool) used to blow open the safe. In the **unpreferred** tree, the PP attaches to the NP “the safe”. This is **noun-phrase attachment**, so the phrase tells which safe, the one that had the dynamite.","children":[]},{"text":"Both are grammatical, but they mean different things. The parser picks the VP attachment. This is because late closure keeps the verb phrase open, so the new phrase joins it. Minimal attachment also likes the VP attachment, because it needs fewer nodes than an extra NP around “the safe with the dynamite”. Since the preferred parse is also the sensible one here, readers get the right meaning easily.","children":[]}],"sentence-07":[{"text":"People read “The burglar blew open the safe with the jewels” more slowly. This is because the sensible meaning needs the **unpreferred parse**.","children":[]},{"text":"Jewels are not a tool for blowing open a safe. The sensible meaning is that the burglar blew open **the safe that had the jewels**. That means “with the jewels” has to attach to the noun phrase “the safe”. That is the unpreferred NP attachment.","children":[]},{"text":"Careful tests of **reading time** and **eye movements** find the slowdown. Reading time is how long readers spend on each part of a sentence. Eye movements show where readers look and when they jump back to reread. The extra time shows up right where the parse has to change.","children":[]},{"text":"Readers **briefly think** the jewels were used to blow open the safe. This is because late closure makes the parser attach the phrase to the verb phrase first, just like with the dynamite. Then the meaning of jewels clashes with the tool reading. So the parser moves the attachment to the noun phrase, and readers **retrace their steps**. Since that repair takes time, reading is slower even though the final meaning is easy.","children":[]}],"sentence-08":[{"text":"The syntactic explanation assumes a parser that works **only on abstract syntactic structure**. It is like the **“lumber mill”** example from speech perception. In a lumber mill, each stage works on its own material and passes a finished product to the next stage. In the same way, the parser builds structure from grammatical categories alone. It does not check meaning or context. Meaning gets worked out afterward from the finished structure.","children":[]},{"text":"The parser has **its own operating characteristics**. It wants the simplest tree possible, which is **minimal attachment**. It gets there partly by leaving clauses open as long as possible, which is **late closure**. Both of these keep down how much structure it has to build and hold at once.","children":[]},{"text":"This is a very efficient way to handle ambiguity, because the parser **ignores ambiguity until it becomes a problem**. This is because it commits to one likely structure and builds only that one. Most of the time the first choice is right, so no extra work is needed. When a later word does not fit, the structure has to be fixed. Since that fix is the garden path, garden paths are the price of being efficient.","children":[]}],"sentence-09":[{"text":"Syntactically “difficult” sentences are **not always hard to read**, and that is a problem for a purely syntactic explanation. The difficult structure here is the **reduced relative clause** (a relative clause with “that was” or “that were” left out, as in the horse sentence).","children":[]},{"text":"Research articles contain many reduced relatives that are easy to read. The verb that starts each one is in capitals. Trueswell, Tanenhaus and Garnsey (1994) wrote “a noun phrase FOLLOWED by a MORPHOLOGICALLY AMBIGUOUS VERB … will be temporarily ambiguous”. Britt, Perfetti, Garrod and Rayner (1992) wrote “Referential information PROVIDED by the discourse is of no help”. Frazier and Rayner (1987) wrote “Recent research REPORTED by FERREIRA AND CLIFTON (1986) has demonstrated that syntactic processing is quite independent and that the initial syntactic analysis ASSIGNED to a sentence is little influenced by the semantic information ALREADY ANALYZED”. Altmann and Steedman (1988) wrote “The pattern of results OBTAINED with global reading times is reflected by differences LOCATED on the critical disambiguating phrase”.","children":[]},{"text":"These have the same structure as the horse sentence, but they are not impossibly hard. In “recent research reported by Ferreira and Clifton”, research is the thing that got reported.","children":[]},{"text":"**MacDonald, Perlmutter and Seidenberg (1994)** argue that two kinds of information affect parsing from very early on. These are **semantic factors** (meaning) and **statistical regularities** (how often words show up in certain structures). So familiar word uses can make the reduced relative reading easy from the start.","children":[]}],"sentence-10":[{"text":"The table of examples is **wonderfully passive-aggressive**. This is because many examples come from papers that use the difficulty of reduced relatives as evidence for an **autonomous parser**. That is a parser that builds structure without help from meaning or context. Those authors said reduced relatives are hard to process. Yet they wrote reduced relatives in their own papers, and readers understood them easily.","children":[]},{"text":"In each example, the capitalized verb describes the noun right before it. In “a noun phrase FOLLOWED by a morphologically ambiguous verb”, followed describes noun phrase. In “the initial syntactic analysis ASSIGNED to a sentence”, assigned describes analysis. In “differences LOCATED on the critical disambiguating phrase”, located describes differences.","children":[]},{"text":"Ferreira and Clifton (1986) wrote “A person PRESENTED with the evidence EXAMINED by the lawyer may interpret the evidence as a theme, not an agent, because examined requires an animate agent”. Presented describes person, and examined describes evidence.","children":[]},{"text":"These were **not the only reduced relatives** in those papers. They showed up about **once per journal page** in this sample. So reduced relatives are normal in scientific writing. Since readers handle them easily in context, a complex structure can be easy when the context supports it.","children":[]}],"sentence-11":[{"text":"“Recent research reported by…” is easy because several things besides syntax help from **very early on**.","children":[]},{"text":"The first is **semantics** (meaning). Research is **inanimate** (not a living thing). So it is unlikely to be the one doing the reporting. That means it gets read as the thing that was reported.","children":[]},{"text":"The second is **lexical statistics** (learned patterns of how often words show up in certain structures). “Reported” shows up a lot in reduced relatives.","children":[]},{"text":"The third is lexical statistics plus **world knowledge**. In scientific papers, “reported” shows up very often in reduced relatives, especially right after “research”. World knowledge here means knowing how people actually write in that setting. These and many other factors help decide which structure gets picked in the first place.","children":[]},{"text":"“The burglar arrested in the parking lot was charged today” is easy, but “The policeman arrested in the parking lot was charged today” causes a garden path. This is because burglars usually get arrested. So arrested is read as describing the burglar. Policemen usually do the arresting. So arrested is read as the main verb, and “was charged” then breaks that reading. Since the grammar is the same, the difference comes from what we know about burglars and policemen.","children":[]}],"sentence-12":[{"text":"Listeners briefly treat the empty towel as the place to put the apple. **Spivey and colleagues (1995)** found this when they tested whether **world knowledge** can come from the scene right in front of you.","children":[]},{"text":"People saw a pencil, a box, an empty towel and an apple sitting on a towel. They heard “Put the apple on the towel in the box” and did it while their eyes were tracked. The clear version was “Put the apple that’s on the towel in the box”.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because of **minimal attachment**. With it, “the towel” briefly seems to end the clause “put the apple on the towel”. So “on the towel” sounds like the **destination** (where the apple should go). In the real meaning, “on the towel” tells **which apple** to move, and “in the box” is the destination.","children":[]},{"text":"With the unclear instruction, people looked at the apple, then the empty towel, then the box. The look to the apple came near 1,000 milliseconds, and the look to the box came near 2,000 milliseconds. With the clear instruction, they looked from the apple straight to the box. Since every word was heard correctly, the mistake was only in how the location phrase got attached.","children":[]}],"sentence-13":[{"text":"With **two apples** in the display, people mostly stop looking at the empty towel. This display is the **two-referent context** (two possible things “the apple” could mean). One apple sits on a towel and the other sits somewhere else. The empty towel and the box are still there.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because “the apple” alone no longer tells which apple to move. So “on the towel” now has a job, which is picking out one apple. Since a phrase that picks out the apple is expected, listeners read “on the towel” that way from the start. The destination reading never takes hold.","children":[]},{"text":"People usually looked at the apple on the towel and then at the box. Looks to the empty towel were much less common. The **order and timing of eye movements**, measured against the nouns in the speech, **did not differ** between the unclear and the clear instruction.","children":[]},{"text":"So the unclear instruction was handled just like the clear one with “that’s on the towel”. Since only the scene changed, the scene changed how the same sentence got parsed.","children":[]}],"sentence-14":[{"text":"People look at the empty towel much less when there are **two apples**. This is because with two apples it becomes **felicitous** (natural and appropriate) to say which apple you mean. With one apple, there is no need to say which apple. So “on the towel” seems like extra information and gets heard as a destination. With two apples, “on the towel” has a clear job, which is picking the right apple.","children":[]},{"text":"The measure was how often people looked at the wrong destination, the empty towel. With one apple, the unclear instruction led to these looks on about **55%** of trials. The clear instruction led to almost none. With two apples, the unclear instruction led to these looks on only about **17%** of trials. That is close to the roughly 12% for the clear instruction.","children":[]},{"text":"The words were exactly the same in both displays. Since only the scene changed, the scene alone changed how often listeners were misled. This is an effect of **referential context** (the set of objects a phrase could be about). It affects which grammatical relationship listeners pick while they are still hearing the sentence.","children":[]}],"sentence-15":[{"text":"The apple results are **a big problem for purely syntactic accounts**. This is because the same sentence gets parsed differently under different **semantic** (meaning) and **pragmatic** (how language is used in a situation) conditions. One pragmatic condition is whether the speaker needs to say which object they mean.","children":[]},{"text":"The same unclear sentence misled people on about 55% of trials with one apple. It misled them on only about 17% of trials with two apples. The words never changed. Only the scene changed, and the scene changed the first parse.","children":[]},{"text":"The effect also happens **very early**. This is because it shows up in eye movements during the sentence, before the action starts. So the eye movements catch the parse while it is being built. If context only fixed the parse afterward, people in both displays would first look at the empty towel. They do not, because those looks drop with two apples. Since context shaped the first parse, a parser that always starts with the same structure cannot explain the results.","children":[]}],"sentence-16":[{"text":"Ambiguous instructions bend the **hand’s path** toward the wrong spot, not just the eyes. **Farmer and colleagues (2007)** found this by tracking **mouse movements** while people followed spoken instructions in an apple and towel display.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because mouse tracking records the whole path of the hand. So a competing reading can pull the hand toward another place before it reaches the target. The actual **arm movements** are affected by syntactic ambiguity and how it interacts with the **visual context**.","children":[]},{"text":"With one apple, the hand moved from the apple toward the box. On unclear instructions, its path **curved toward the empty towel**. The two paths split most in the middle of the movement. The hand still ended in the right place. With two apples, the unclear and clear paths were nearly the same. This is because the second apple gives the location phrase a job, so it is not heard as a destination.","children":[]},{"text":"They also measured the **Euclidean distance** (straight-line distance) between the unclear and clear paths at each moment. With one apple, it peaked at about 200 pixels around 40% of the way through. With two apples, it peaked lower and earlier, at about 140 pixels around 30%. Since the hand bends while moving, understanding and planning the action happen at the same time.","children":[]}],"sentence-17":[],"sentence-18":[{"text":"After “the”, the parser expects a noun to come. This is because **the** is a **determiner** (a word that starts a **noun phrase**). “The” does not name anything by itself. It limits what kinds of words can come next. An adjective or other describing word might come before the noun.","children":[]},{"text":"The expectation is about a **grammatical category** (a word type such as noun), not one particular word. Many nouns could follow, such as pizza, dog or book. An adjective could come first, as in “the hot pizza”. So the parser guesses the form of the phrase while its meaning is still open.","children":[]},{"text":"Since each word sets up an expectation for the next one, words that fit go through smoothly. Words that break the expectation cause trouble.","children":[]}],"sentence-19":[{"text":"After “the pizza”, the parser expects a verb. This is because **pizza** is a **noun** that finishes the noun phrase started by “the”. It is the **head of the noun phrase** (the main word the phrase is built around). “The pizza” can now be the subject of the sentence. So the parser expects a verb or verb phrase that says what the pizza did or what happened to it.","children":[]},{"text":"Recognizing the word gives two kinds of information. Its **category**, noun, tells the parser how it can combine with other words. Its **meaning**, a kind of food, makes certain events likely, such as being eaten or baked.","children":[]},{"text":"Since both kinds of information are available, grammar-based and meaning-based expectations both guide how the next words get handled.","children":[]}],"sentence-20":[{"text":"After “was”, the sentence can still go several ways. This is because **was** is a past-tense form of **be**. It can be an **auxiliary** (a helping verb that joins another verb to mark things like tense or voice).","children":[]},{"text":"In “the pizza was eaten”, was helps make a **passive** (a sentence about what was done to the subject) with eaten. In “the pizza was in the oven”, was links the pizza to a place.","children":[]},{"text":"So the sentence could say where the pizza was, what it was like, or what was done to it. A place would come next with a preposition. A passive would come next with a participle such as eaten. An adjective could also come next.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the role of was depends on what comes after it, the parser has to keep the growing structure ready while more words arrive.","children":[]}],"sentence-21":[{"text":"After “in”, the parser expects a noun phrase that names a place. This is because **in** is a **preposition** (a word for a relationship such as location or being inside something). It starts a **prepositional phrase**. “In” does not name the place by itself. So it needs a noun phrase to supply the place, as in “in the oven” or “in the box”.","children":[]},{"text":"After “the pizza was”, the word “in” narrows down what the sentence can be. Now it is heading toward saying where the pizza was.","children":[]},{"text":"The expectation is about grammar. So the next word has to be able to start a noun phrase, such as “the”, “an” or a noun. Since the phrase is only complete with that noun phrase, understanding it means putting the preposition and the noun phrase together.","children":[]}],"sentence-22":[{"text":"After “in the”, the parser strongly expects a **noun**. This is because this second **the** starts a new **noun phrase** inside “in the …”. That noun phrase will name the place. “The” does not give the place by itself. So a noun is still needed, maybe with a describing word in front, as in “in the hot oven”.","children":[]},{"text":"The expectation is about **word category**, not just a sensible topic. Knowing the sentence is about pizza makes words like oven or box likely. But the grammar rule holds no matter what the meaning is. Whatever comes next has to fit inside a noun phrase.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the grammatical frame is now very specific, any word that cannot be part of a noun phrase will clash with it.","children":[]}],"sentence-23":[{"text":"“The pizza was in the eaten” breaks the sentence’s structure. This is because **eaten** is the **past participle** (a verb form, as in “was eaten”) of eat, not a noun. “In the” needs a noun to finish its noun phrase. So eaten lands exactly where a noun is required. This is a **word-category violation** (a word of the wrong type in a spot that needs a different type).","children":[]},{"text":"This is different from a sentence that is grammatical but strange. “The pizza was in the ocean” is odd in meaning, but the grammar is fine, because ocean is a noun. “The pizza was in the eaten” cannot be fixed by imagining some unusual situation. This is because the problem is the type of word, not the meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"Since one is a grammar error and the other is a meaning error, these sentences are useful for brain research. Researchers can compare the brain’s responses to the two errors to see whether grammar and meaning are handled differently.","children":[]}],"sentence-24":[{"text":"The brain gives a fast response to a word of the wrong type, called the **early left anterior negativity** (**ELAN**). It is an **ERP** (event-related potential, the brain’s electrical activity recorded at the scalp). The activity is averaged over many trials and timed to something like a word appearing. It shows up for **word class violations** (a word of the wrong grammatical category, such as noun, verb or preposition).","children":[]},{"text":"Classic ELAN sentences have the wrong word type. “The pizza was in the eaten” has a participle where a noun should be. “The scientist criticized Max’s of proof the theorem” has the preposition **of** where a noun should come after “Max’s”.","children":[]},{"text":"The ELAN has three features. It is **early**, about **100 to 200 milliseconds** after the word. It is **left and anterior**, strongest over the left front of the scalp. That location raises the question of whether it is specific to language. It shows up **specifically for word category violations**, such as a noun where a verb is expected or a preposition in place of a noun.","children":[]},{"text":"Wrong sentences give this early negative response at left frontal sites, and correct sentences do not. Since it comes so fast, it was taken as evidence that the brain checks word categories before it works out meaning.","children":[]}],"sentence-25":[{"text":"With written words, the early response to a wrong word type comes from **visual cortex**. That is the part at the back of the brain, in the **occipital** lobe, that handles what we see. **Dikker, Rabagliati, Farmer and Pylkkänen (2010)** found this. Their paper is called “Early occipital sensitivity to syntactic category is based on form typicality”.","children":[]},{"text":"They compared “The beautiful princess …” with “The beautifully princess …”. Princess is a **bimorphemic noun** (a noun ending in a noun suffix). Beautiful is an adjective, so a noun is expected next. Beautifully is an adverb, so a verb or adjective is expected next. So princess is a surprise after beautifully. The visual response was **bigger** for that surprise at about 120 to 130 milliseconds.","children":[]},{"text":"This suggests the prediction **reaches down to the earliest parts of the visual system**. When you expect a verb, you also expect visual patterns that fit how verbs are written, such as typical endings. So the early response might come from a **mismatch** between that prediction and what actually shows up on the screen.","children":[]},{"text":"This raises a question. Does it work only for words **morphologically marked** (with a part, like a suffix, that signals the category) as nouns or verbs? Or does it work for **open-class** and **closed-class** words? Open-class words are content words that keep gaining members. Closed-class words are a small fixed set of function words such as prepositions. The effect shows up for nouns with a noun suffix and for typical-looking nouns, but not for neutral nouns like “infant”.","children":[]}],"sentence-26":[{"text":"The same word can be a **noun** or a **verb**, and the rest of the sentence decides which.","children":[]},{"text":"In “Chris and Ben are glad that the bird perches seem easy to install”, perches is a noun (the bars birds sit on), and seem is the verb. In “Chris and Ben are glad that the bird perches comfortably in the cage”, perches is a verb (what the bird does). In “The teacher told the principal that the student needs were not being met”, needs is a noun (what the student requires). In “The teacher told the principal that the student needs to be more focused”, needs is a verb.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the word form alone does not settle its role. The **continuation** (the words after the ambiguous word) settles it.","children":[]},{"text":"Words also differ in **phonological form typicality** (how much a word sounds like typical nouns or typical verbs). A corpus analysis measured this for **3,158 words**. Each word got a distance from all nouns and from all verbs in sound features. A small distance means it sounds like that group. Nouns mostly sound closer to nouns, and verbs closer to verbs. But some do not. Besides **noun-like nouns** and **verb-like verbs**, there are **verb-like nouns** and **noun-like verbs**. So a word can sound like one category while belonging to the other.","children":[]}],"sentence-27":[{"text":"**“Perches” sounds noun-like** and **“needs” sounds verb-like**, so each word has one use that fits its sound and one that clashes. These are **form typicality** tendencies (learned patterns in how words sound). Both words can still be used either way.","children":[]},{"text":"For perches, “the bird perches seem easy to install” uses it as a noun, which **fits** its noun-like sound. “The bird perches comfortably in the cage” makes it a verb, which **goes against** its sound.","children":[]},{"text":"For needs, it is flipped. “The student needs to be more focused” uses it as a verb, which **fits** its verb-like sound. “The student needs were not being met” makes it a noun, which **goes against** its sound.","children":[]},{"text":"These four sentences make a fair test. This is because each word shows up once in a fitting use and once in a clashing use. So the test separates what the word’s sound suggests from what the sentence’s grammar requires. If sound patterns guide parsing, the clashing uses should be harder to read. Since all four sentences are grammatical, any extra difficulty would come from the sound pattern.","children":[]}],"sentence-28":[{"text":"Reading slows down when the sentence needs the word type that does **not match** how the word sounds.","children":[]},{"text":"For **noun-like homonyms** such as perches, reading the word as a noun added only about **50 milliseconds**. Reading it as a verb added about **120 milliseconds**. So perches was harder to read as a verb. For **verb-like homonyms** such as needs, reading the word as a noun added about **110 milliseconds**. Reading it as a verb added almost nothing, under 10 milliseconds. So needs was harder to read as a noun.","children":[]},{"text":"The measure was reading time at **disambiguation** minus reading time at the **point of ambiguity**. Disambiguation is where the next words make the word type clear, such as “seem” or “comfortably”. The point of ambiguity is the ambiguous word itself. The times were adjusted for word length. A bigger increase means more difficulty.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the ambiguous word stays the same in each pair, and only the rest of the sentence changes. So the extra cost comes from the rest of the sentence demanding the type that clashes with the word’s sound. Since that clash slows people down, parsing uses learned patterns about how words sound, along with the structure the sentence finally needs.","children":[]}],"sentence-29":[{"text":"The early brain response to an unexpected noun only shows up when the word itself **physically looks like a noun**. This **ELAN effect** for nouns where a verb is expected needs one of two cues. One is **morphemes** (the smallest meaningful word parts, such as a noun suffix) that mark the word as a noun. The other is **“nouniness”** (how much the word’s form looks like typical nouns, even without a suffix).","children":[]},{"text":"The response measured was the **M100** (a brain response that peaks about 100 milliseconds after a word appears). Three kinds of nouns were tested. For **bimorphemic nouns**, “The beautifully princess …” gave a significantly bigger response than “The beautiful princess …”. Princess has a noun suffix. For **typical nouns**, “The tastelessly soda …” gave a significantly bigger response than “The tasteless soda …”. Soda has a typical noun form. For **neutral nouns**, “The cutely infant …” and “The cute infant …” did not differ.","children":[]},{"text":"The neutral nouns show **no effect** because infant has no strong cue. It has no noun suffix and no typical noun form. The brain predicts what the next word should look like. The early response only happens when the word carries physical evidence against that prediction. Since infant has no such evidence, a category mismatch alone is not enough.","children":[]}],"sentence-30":[{"text":"Sentence processing can be summed up in **four sentences**, each with one idea.","children":[]},{"text":"“Colorless green ideas sleep furiously” means **syntax is independent of semantics** to some extent. This is because a sentence can be grammatically correct but meaningless. The words fill a grammatical structure without describing a sensible event.","children":[]},{"text":"“The horse raced past the barn fell” means syntactic structure is often highly **ambiguous** (actually **vague**), but we only notice when things fail. Reading times for “preferred” and “unpreferred” structures suggest that parsing **takes shortcuts to save memory**. It does not build every possible parse. So when a shortcut picks the wrong structure, the reader goes down the garden path.","children":[]},{"text":"“Put the apple on the towel in the box” means **context, pragmatics and other factors** affect parsing from very early on. With two apples in view, listeners hear “on the towel” as telling which apple, not where to put it.","children":[]},{"text":"“The bird perches were comfortable” means the very earliest syntactic effects in the brain come partly from **form typicality**. They do not necessarily come from **morphosyntactic** structure (grammar signaled by word parts and word order). They seem tied to **predictions about how words physically look**. Perches has a noun-like form, so using it as a noun here fits what its form predicts.","children":[]}],"sentence-31":[],"aphasias-01":[],"aphasias-02":[{"text":"In the 1870s, German neurologists explained each kind of language loss as a broken link between two **centers**. A center is a brain region that stores one kind of language representation. This is because **Lichtheim** and **Wernicke** began making more systematic observations of **aphasia** (a loss or impairment of language caused by brain damage). They ascribed specific losses of function to **“disconnections”** (damage to the pathway between two centers). When the pathway is broken, information can no longer travel between the two centers. This was reasoning about aphasia before Chomsky.","children":[]},{"text":"**Lichtheim’s “House” model** draws the centers and pathways in the shape of a house. **A** stores **sound images** (the stored sound patterns of words). **M** stores **movement images** (the stored motor plans for saying words). **B** stores the **concepts** words stand for. Pathway **a** brings heard speech into A, and pathway **m** carries plans from M out to the speech muscles. Seven numbered sites mark different forms of aphasia, one for each damaged center or pathway.","children":[]},{"text":"The place of the break decides what the person loses.","children":["A break between **A and B** might take away **understanding spoken words**. The sound of the word arrives, but it cannot reach its concept.","A break between **A and M** might take away **verbatim repetition**. A heard word cannot go straight to the plan for saying it."]},{"text":"This **“box and arrow” model** is still foundational in **aphasiology** (the study of aphasia) and in modeling normal language. The **interactive activation model** of word recognition is built the same way, with levels of representation joined by pathways. This history comes from Eling and Whitaker (2009).","children":[]}],"aphasias-03":[{"text":"In the 1970s, **Norman Geschwind** brought back the box-and-arrow approach, and by then linguistics could split language into separate parts. The result was the **“Geschwind-Wernicke model”**, which ties language functions to particular brain regions and the pathways between them. **Broca’s area** is toward the front of the left hemisphere, in the frontal lobe. **Wernicke’s area** is farther back, in the temporal lobe. The **arcuate fasciculus** (a bundle of nerve fibers) connects Wernicke’s area to Broca’s area.","children":[]},{"text":"Two big grooves are used as landmarks.","children":["The **central sulcus**, also called the **fissure of Rolando**, separates the frontal lobe from the parietal lobe behind it.","The **lateral sulcus**, also called the **fissure of Sylvius**, separates the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes above it."]},{"text":"In the century between Wernicke and Geschwind, **linguistics changed dramatically**. Linguists built a model where **syntax was independent** of other parts of language. Syntax also had **its own computational principles**. That made it possible to think about **dissociations** and **double dissociations** of language functions in more sophisticated ways.","children":[]},{"text":"A **dissociation** means one ability is impaired while another is spared. A **double dissociation** means one patient loses the first ability and keeps the second, and another patient shows the reverse. A double dissociation is strong evidence that the two abilities use **separate systems**. Since syntax, words and meaning are now separate parts, a patient can be impaired in one part while the others still work.","children":[]}],"aphasias-04":[{"text":"Broca’s aphasia mostly breaks the grammar of speech, while Wernicke’s aphasia keeps speech fluent but fills it with wrong words. This is because of the classic profiles that **Fromkin (1997)** describes.","children":[]},{"text":"**Broca’s aphasia** speech has **word-finding pauses**, **loss of grammatical morphemes**, and often **disturbed word order**. Grammatical morphemes are small words or word parts that mark grammar, like “the,” “is” or past-tense “-ed.” Everyday conversation seems to be understood fairly well. But **controlled testing reveals considerable impairments** in comprehension.","children":[]},{"text":"**Agrammatism** (difficulty producing or understanding grammatical structure) is almost another name for Broca’s aphasia. But the match is not perfect.","children":["**Some patients with lesions in Broca’s area are not agrammatic**.","**Some agrammatic patients would not be classified neurologically as Broca’s aphasics**.","So Broca’s aphasia names a pattern of behavior, and that behavior does not always come with damage to Broca’s area."]},{"text":"**Wernicke’s aphasia** speech is **fluent**, with **good intonation and pronunciation**. It has **many word substitutions**, some related in meaning to the intended word and some not. It has **neologisms** (invented words) and **phonological errors** (mistakes in the sounds of words). These patients also have **comprehension difficulties**. Fluency only means the speech flows smoothly. It does not mean the speech carries a clear message.","children":[]},{"text":"So Broca’s patients keep the meaningful words but lose the small grammar pieces that connect them. Wernicke’s patients keep the grammar frame but fill it with wrong or invented words.","children":[]}],"aphasias-05":[{"text":"The two aphasias seem to split neatly in two, with fluent nonsense on one side and choppy speech made of content words on the other. This is an attractive **dichotomy** (a two-way split).","children":[]},{"text":"**Wernicke’s aphasia** is called **“word salad”** or **“Jabberwocky,”** often with **neologisms**, while **syntax is roughly intact**. “Jabberwocky” is Lewis Carroll’s poem of grammatical sentences full of nonsense words.","children":["Asked about his health, one patient said “I felt worse because I can no longer keep in mind from the mind of the minds to keep me from mind and up to the ear which can be to find among ourselves.” Kriendler and colleagues (1971) reported it.","Asked about his poor vision, another patient said “My wires don’t hire right.”","Asked if he was a doctor, an aphasic physician said “Me? Yes, sir, I’m a male demaploze on my own. I still know my tubaboys what for I have that’s gone hell and some of them go.” **Demaploze** and **tubaboys** are neologisms.","One patient called a fork “a need for a schedule” and a spoon “How many schemes on your throat.”"]},{"text":"**Broca’s aphasia** is called **“telegraphic” speech**. It has **difficulty with word order and grammatical particles**, but the **important content words** are mostly spared.","children":["Asked what brought him to the hospital, one patient said “Yes … ah … Monday … ah … Dad and P.H. (the patient’s name) and Dad … hospital. Two … ah doctors … and ah … thirty minutes … and yes … ah … hospital. And, er Wednesday … nine o’clock and eh Thursday, ten o’clock … doctors. Two doctors … and ah … teeth. Yeah … fine.” Badecker and Caramazza (1985) reported it.","Words like Dad, hospital and doctors carry the story. The small grammar words that would join them are missing."]}],"aphasias-06":[{"text":"Broca’s own patients would probably not count as Broca’s aphasics today. They likely had **apraxia of speech** (a disorder in planning and coordinating the movements of speaking). This is because the modern dichotomy is about language, not movement. Wernicke’s aphasia is **problems with activation, or possibly inhibition, in the lexicon** (the mental store of words). Broca’s aphasia is **problems with grammar**. **Activation** makes a candidate word available. **Inhibition** holds back competing words, so weak inhibition could let wrong words slip out.","children":[]},{"text":"That is **fairly distant from how Broca described his original cases**. Broca described **Leborgne**, known as **“Tan.”** Tan **could only produce one jargon syllable**, “tan.” Otherwise he **seemed to be in possession of his senses**. He even **seemed to understand what was being said**.","children":[]},{"text":"Broca’s description raises three questions.","children":["His other senses being intact matters because then the loss is only about speech, which makes it a **dissociation**.","A “capacity for articulate speech” sounds more believable as a job for a brain region than “criminality” or “philoprogenitiveness” (a phrenology term for love of one’s children).","Testing Tan today would mean measuring his deficits at the phonetic, phonological, lexical and syntactic **levels of description used in modern linguistics**."]},{"text":"This creates a real confusion. Broca’s actual patients were arguably **unable to produce speech at all**. That seems to come from **motor control problems**, not grammar. Apraxia of speech can cut speech down severely without any loss of grammatical knowledge.","children":[]}],"aphasias-07":[{"text":"The aphasia dichotomy is attractive because linguistics also draws a sharp line between **the grammar** and **the lexicon**. This is because late 20th-century formal linguistics separated these two parts. The **grammar** is the set of rules for combining words into sentences. The **lexicon** is the store of single words, which have to be memorized. So Wernicke’s aphasia would be a lexicon disorder, and Broca’s aphasia would be a grammar disorder.","children":[]},{"text":"The line starts with **Chomsky’s formulation of the computational problem of language learning**. We do not recognize a new sentence because it matches something familiar in any simple way. We recognize it because **it is generated by the grammar** each person has internalized. So a sentence never heard before can still be understood.","children":[]},{"text":"**Chomsky (1955)** also gave **compelling demonstrations of the independence of syntax and semantics**. Neither “colorless green ideas sleep furiously” nor “furiously sleep ideas green colorless” has ever occurred in an English speaker’s past experience.","children":["“colorless green ideas sleep furiously” makes no sense. Its word order still follows English grammar, so it is **grammatical**.","“furiously sleep ideas green colorless” uses the same words in an order grammar does not allow, so it is **not grammatical**.","Both are equally unfamiliar and equally meaningless. Since only the grammar differs, **grammar must be its own system**."]}],"aphasias-08":[{"text":"The dichotomy also fits the speech production model, where word choice happens at the **functional level** and grammar happens at the **positional level**. This is because the model has separate levels.","children":["At the **message level**, the speaker chooses a story angle. The speaker also calls up concepts of **referents** (the people, objects or events being talked about) and event structure.","At the **functional level**, the speaker calls up word meanings and **semantic roles** (the part someone plays in an event, like doer or receiver).","At the **positional level**, the speaker gets word forms into grammatically appropriate order.","The **phonological encoding level** supplies the sounds. The **speech gesture level** handles **articulation** (the physical movements of speaking)."]},{"text":"At the functional level, **lemmas** (a word’s lexicon entry, linking meaning to grammatical properties) have an **arbitrary** relationship to their referents. Nothing about a dog forces it to be called “dog.” So this is the lexical side of language.","children":[]},{"text":"At the positional level, syntactic trees and **affixes** (word parts added to a stem, like “-ed” or “-ly”) come from **abstract syntactic processes**. So this is the grammar side, built by rules.","children":[]},{"text":"Someone might object that **HOUSEBOAT** and **DOGHOUSE** are not arbitrary, because they are built from parts. The answer is **speech error evidence** that **derivational morphology** (building new words from parts) happens at the positional level. In the slip “easy enoughly,” the speaker meant “easily enough.” The “-ly” moved by itself and landed on the wrong word. Since the suffix moved as a separate piece, a grammar operation must have added it.","children":[]}],"aphasias-09":[{"text":"Broca’s patients might understand most of what is said even if their grammar is damaged. This is because everyday conversation gives many clues besides grammar. Word meanings, familiar events and context all point to the same message. A listener can follow along using these clues without fully working out the grammar.","children":[]},{"text":"Popular theories of **sentence processing** say **syntactic structure is computed independently of meaning** while we listen or read. A **parser** (the process that builds sentence structure) works out the structure first. Meaning gets worked out afterward.","children":[]},{"text":"To test these **“parsing” mechanisms**, the test sentences have to be **built carefully**.","children":["One such sentence is “The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite.”","In the **preferred** structure, “with the dynamite” attaches to the **verb phrase**, so the dynamite is what he used.","In the **unpreferred** structure, it attaches to the **noun phrase** “the safe,” so it says which safe.","The words stay the same and only the attachment changes. So this kind of sentence tests parsing without changing word meanings."]},{"text":"Since meaning can give away the answer, a real grammar test needs sentences where the roles cannot be guessed from the likeliest event. **Reversible sentences**, where either person could plausibly do the action, force the listener to use grammar.","children":[]}],"aphasias-10":[{"text":"To show that a patient’s problem is really with **syntax**, researchers test **comprehension**. This is because a comprehension test lets the patient answer without speaking. So motor problems cannot be the reason for mistakes. If the patient still misunderstands grammatical relationships, the problem **goes beyond articulation**.","children":[]},{"text":"Broca did not have any of this theory in the 1860s. For him, it was interesting enough to **dissociate “articulate speech” from comprehension**. Leborgne could say only one syllable, yet he seemed to understand what people said to him. That showed speaking and understanding can come apart, which was the key finding back then.","children":[]},{"text":"The modern question is different. It asks whether some patients have a difficulty that is **more generally with syntax**. A **syntactic impairment** (a problem with organizing grammatical relationships) should hurt both speaking and understanding sentences. Since speaking is already hurt by motor problems, understanding is the part that can show it.","children":[]}],"aphasias-11":[{"text":"It was interesting to find comprehension problems in Broca’s patients because their broken speech alone could just be a motor problem. This is because **Broca’s aphasia almost always comes with some motor dysfunction**. So agrammatic speech might **merely reflect an attempt to communicate despite motor limitations**. When speaking is very hard, a patient might drop the small grammar words and keep the most informative words.","children":[]},{"text":"**Hughlings Jackson** said **we are observing what the individual can do despite the impairment**. So agrammatic speech after an injury does not literally mean **the grammar itself is injured**. Caramazza and Zurif (1976) cite Lenneberg (1973) for a version of this. On that view, speaking is such a strain that the patient **speaks asyntactically to economize effort**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Chomsky’s distinction between competence and performance** helps here. **Competence** is a person’s underlying knowledge of language. **Performance** is actually using that knowledge, which memory limits, distraction or motor problems can disrupt. When motor problems clearly mess up performance, **comprehension may give a clearer window into competence**.","children":[]},{"text":"So **Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** tested comprehension in Broca’s patients. If the problem is only motor, understanding grammar should be normal. If the grammar is damaged, understanding should suffer too. Both explanations predict telegraphic speech, so only a comprehension test can tell them apart.","children":[]}],"aphasias-12":[{"text":"Special psycholinguistic tests are needed because ordinary sentences can be understood correctly without using grammar at all. This is because conversation has lots of **redundant cues** (separate clues that all point to the same meaning). “Man bites dog” is the newspaper example of a surprising event.","children":[]},{"text":"“The dog bit the man” gives many ways to get the right meaning.","children":["The listener can just put DOG, BITE and MAN into the **most likely event**, with or without syntax. Dogs bite people far more often than people bite dogs.","The sentence uses **canonical word order** (agent, verb, undergoer, which is subject, verb, object). The **agent** is the doer, and the **undergoer** is the one the action happens to.","In English the first noun is usually the agent.","The **discourse context** (the surrounding conversation) also clears up a lot."]},{"text":"Broca’s patients might understand by leaning on these **compensatory heuristics** (mental shortcuts that make up for a weakness somewhere else). A patient could guess the most plausible event. A patient could also guess that the first noun is the doer. Either shortcut gets “The dog bit the man” right with no grammar.","children":[]},{"text":"So a better test sentence is “The dog that the man is biting is angry.” Here the **man** is doing the biting. The dog comes first and dogs are likelier biters, so both shortcuts give the wrong answer. Only working out the grammar gets it right.","children":[]}],"aphasias-13":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif** wrote exactly this kind of test sentence, plus **control sentences**, in four groups of eight. This is because each group changes how much meaning helps. A **center-embedded** sentence has a relative clause in the middle of the main clause. In every one of them, the first noun is acted on and the doer is inside the clause.","children":[]},{"text":"**Control sentences**, group A, are simple and in normal order, like “The girl is kicking a green ball,” “The cat is chasing a blue bird” and “The boy is riding a new bicycle.”","children":[]},{"text":"**Center-embedded sentences with semantic constraints**, group B, let meaning give away the roles. Examples are “The apple that the boy is eating is red,” “The wagon that the horse is pulling is green” and “The balloon that the clown is holding is round.” Apples cannot eat boys.","children":[]},{"text":"**Reversible center-embedded sentences**, group C, have two participants who could each do the action. Examples are “The cow that the monkey is scaring is yellow,” “The horse that the bear is kicking is brown” and “The girl that the boy is tickling is happy.” Only grammar gives the roles.","children":[]},{"text":"**Improbable center-embedded sentences**, group D, describe unlikely events. Examples are “The bird that the worm is eating is blue,” “The man that the horse is riding is fat” and “The policeman that the robber is chasing is skinny.” Common sense points to the wrong answer.","children":[]},{"text":"A listener who relies on meaning should pass groups A and B but fail C and D. A listener who parses normally should pass all four.","children":[]}],"aphasias-14":[{"text":"Broca’s patients got sentences wrong only when grammar was the only way to tell who did what to whom. This is because **Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** used a **sentence-picture matching task** (hear a sentence, then choose which picture fits it). Accuracy shows whether the listener understood the roles, with no speaking needed. Healthy listeners and patients with Broca’s, conduction and Wernicke’s aphasia all heard the four sentence types.","children":[]},{"text":"**Healthy controls** were **more or less equally good** on every type, near the top score.","children":[]},{"text":"**Wernicke’s patients** were **poor overall**, even on simple control sentences. That is probably because of their **comprehension deficits**, which affect words as well as grammar.","children":[]},{"text":"**Broca’s patients** did **well** on control and semantically constrained sentences, where meaning gives away the answer. They did **poorly** on reversible and improbable sentences. In those, **syntax conflicted with common sense** or with the likely roles of the nouns.","children":[]},{"text":"This **specific comprehension deficit** was taken as evidence that **Broca’s aphasia affects syntax specifically**. Since the problem shows up in understanding as well as speaking, it seems to be in the grammar itself. The paper’s title is “Dissociation of algorithmic and heuristic processes.” Broca’s patients lose the **algorithmic** route (working out the grammar) but keep the **heuristic** shortcuts (guessing from meaning).","children":[]}],"aphasias-15":[{"text":"A detailed theory of the parts of language lets researchers separate grammar problems from word problems. This is because it opens new ways to study **structure-function relationships** (links between a brain structure and the job it does). Before, aphasics could only be sorted into good or poor comprehension, or more or less fluent. Now researchers can **isolate problems with particular aspects of language**.","children":[]},{"text":"Two patients can look the same on fluency and still differ.","children":["One patient might know word meanings well but assign the wrong roles in complex sentences.","Another patient might produce a fluent grammar frame filled with words that miss the message.","A single fluency score would hide that difference."]},{"text":"The key split is **the grammar versus the lexicon**. **Grammar** is the rules that relate words to each other in a sentence. The **lexicon** is the stored words and the ability to pull up the right one.","children":[]},{"text":"Each kind of damage predicts a different result. A **grammar deficit** predicts trouble with reversible sentences but not with word meanings. A **lexical deficit** predicts trouble with word meanings and naming but not with grammar. So controlled tasks can tie one damaged operation to one brain region.","children":[]}],"aphasias-16":[{"text":"**Dick and colleagues** found **problems with this dichotomy** by describing one event with four kinds of sentences. This is because only two of the four sentence types put the doer first. Participants heard each sentence and had to **indicate which animal is doing the action**. A picture of a dog and a picture of a cat appeared on a computer screen. Participants pressed a button on a response box to choose. The event was always the dog biting the cat.","children":[]},{"text":"Two types put the **doer first**.","children":["An **active** sentence is “The dog is biting the cat.”","**Subject cleft** is “It’s the dog that is biting the cat.” A **cleft** splits a simple sentence with “It’s … that” to focus on one part. Here the focused noun is the doer.","For both, picking the first animal mentioned gives the **right** answer."]},{"text":"Two types put the **receiver first**.","children":["**Object cleft** is “It’s the cat that the dog is biting.” Here the focused noun is the one being bitten.","**Passive** is “The cat is bitten by the dog.” The receiver becomes the grammatical subject.","For both, picking the first animal mentioned gives the **wrong** answer."]},{"text":"Since the first-animal shortcut fails on object clefts and passives, only a listener who **parses the grammar** gets all four types right.","children":[]}],"aphasias-17":[{"text":"Broca’s patients did worst on the hard sentences, object clefts and passives, but they were not great on any sentence type. We know this because aphasia patients and **healthy aging controls** did Dick’s task, and the pattern looked **much like what Caramazza and Zurif observed**. Patients were grouped by aphasia type with the Western Aphasia Battery.","children":[]},{"text":"Each group had its own profile.","children":["**Elderly controls** scored close to 100% on all four types.","**Anomic** patients (whose main problem is finding words) scored about 89% on actives and subject clefts. They scored about 69% on object clefts and about 73% on passives.","**Conduction** patients scored about 75% on actives and subject clefts but about 42% on object clefts.","**Wernicke’s** patients scored low on every type, around 43% to 50%."]},{"text":"**Broca’s** patients scored about 61% on actives and 65% on subject clefts. They dropped to about 42% on object clefts and about 54% on passives. Object clefts and passives are **noncanonical** (they break the usual doer-first order). So they are the **syntactically complex** sentences.","children":[]},{"text":"Broca’s patients had two problems at once. Even on simple actives, they were far below the elderly controls. On complex sentences, they lost even more. So their pattern is a **general drop** plus an **extra cost** for complex structures.","children":[]}],"aphasias-18":[{"text":"Healthy students under stress make the same kind of errors that Broca’s patients make, so the patients’ problem may be **performance** and not lost grammar. This is because researchers gave the task to **healthy undergraduates** under **conditions that stress the language system**.","children":["**Compressed** speech is speech played faster than normal.","A **low-pass filter** cuts the higher frequencies, so speech sounds muffled.","Noise was also added.","**Auditory digits** and **visual digits** were number lists to remember while listening, which uses up memory."]},{"text":"With **one stressor**, accuracy stayed high on actives and subject clefts. Object clefts suffered most. With noise, accuracy fell from about 97% on actives to about 86% on object clefts. Passives dropped to about 91%.","children":[]},{"text":"With **two stressors**, the drops got bigger. With muffled and sped-up speech together, accuracy was about 91% on actives and 90% on subject clefts. It fell to about 66% on object clefts and 68% on passives.","children":[]},{"text":"So **object clefts and passives are the most vulnerable** to every kind of stress. These students have no brain damage and no loss of grammar knowledge. Stress alone gave them an aphasia-like pattern. Complex sentences may need more processing resources, so any loss of resources hurts them first. Since that is true, Broca’s patients may still have the grammar but lack the resources to use it.","children":[]}],"aphasias-19":[{"text":"Broca’s aphasics’ comprehension deficits do not seem to be specific to language. This is because **Saygin and colleagues (2004)** asked patients to **match an action to an object**, with and without words.","children":["In the **non-linguistic** version, a drawing had a boy pantomiming an action, like licking. Participants picked the matching object from pictures like an ice cream cone, a cake, broccoli and a rooster.","In the **linguistic** version, the action was given in words instead."]},{"text":"**Broca’s aphasics had difficulty with this task whether the stimuli were linguistic or not**.","children":["Without words, controls scored **98.5%** and aphasic patients **93.4%**.","With words, controls scored **99.4%** and aphasic patients **87.5%**."]},{"text":"Accuracy also depended on the **distractor** (the wrong choice offered).","children":["With **unrelated** distractors, controls scored 99.8% and patients 93.3%.","With **semantic** distractors (related in meaning to the right object), controls scored 99.2% and patients dropped to **86.3%**.","With **affordance-based** distractors, controls scored 97.7% and patients 91.6%. An **affordance** is an action an object allows, like another object that can also be licked."]},{"text":"The pantomime version takes language out but keeps meaning in. The patient still has to understand the action and link it to the right object, just as a verb links to its object. A purely grammar deficit predicts no trouble here. Since the patients still struggled, the problem seems to include **action meanings** in general.","children":[]}],"aphasias-20":[{"text":"Doing badly on the pantomime version went with damage to **Broca’s area**, so Broca’s area does more than syntax. This is because **performance on the non-verbal version was significantly correlated with damage to Broca’s area**. Lesion maps marked the brain areas linked to trouble with **non-linguistic pantomime interpretation**. The maps show three horizontal slices of the brain. Red and yellow mark the strongest links between damage and poor performance. The strongest links are in the left frontal region in front of the **central sulcus**, which includes Broca’s area.","children":[]},{"text":"The task used no language at all.","children":["Participants only had to understand a pantomimed action and pick an object.","They did not have to say a sentence.","They did not have to work out the grammar of a spoken instruction."]},{"text":"Since no language was involved, patients with damage there struggled to connect an action with its meaning. So Broca’s area supports **understanding actions** as well as grammar. Any account of Broca’s area has to explain both the grammar findings and this trouble with actions.","children":[]}],"aphasias-21":[{"text":"Wernicke’s aphasics’ comprehension deficits do not seem to be specific to language either. This is because **Saygin and colleagues** asked patients to **match sounds to pictures**, with and without words.","children":["In the **nonverbal** version, a speaker played an environmental sound, like a cow mooing. Participants chose between pictures like a cow and a sheep.","In the **verbal** version, the speaker played a spoken phrase, like “cow mooing.” Participants chose between pictures like a cow and a violin."]},{"text":"**Wernicke’s patients were particularly bad at this task**.","children":["**Controls** scored 99.2% with words and 98.1% without.","Patients with **right hemisphere damage** scored 99.1% and 98.2%.","**Anomic** patients scored 98.6% and 96.7%.","**Broca’s** patients scored 96.3% and 95.2%.","**Wernicke’s** patients scored **90.0%** with words and **91.3%** without."]},{"text":"**No significant difference was observed between the verbal and nonverbal versions**. Wernicke’s patients had as much trouble with a cow’s moo as with the words “cow mooing.” So their problem goes beyond understanding spoken words. Since both kinds of sound gave trouble, the problem is **linking any meaningful sound to what it means**.","children":[]}],"aphasias-22":[{"text":"Trouble with sound-picture matching, with or without words, comes from damage in the same place, around **Wernicke’s area**. This is because lesion overlays marked where the brain was damaged in patients who failed each version. **Eight** patients were impaired on the **nonverbal** version. **Ten** patients were impaired on the **verbal** version. Red and yellow mark the areas damaged in the most patients.","children":[]},{"text":"For both groups, the most overlap is in the **posterior temporal area of the left hemisphere**, around Wernicke’s area. So the verbal and nonverbal problems come from damage to the **same general region**.","children":[]},{"text":"The two versions use different sounds but ask for the same thing.","children":["A spoken phrase gives the meaning through words.","An animal call gives the meaning without language.","Both require connecting a meaningful sound to what it stands for."]},{"text":"Since damage around Wernicke’s area breaks that connection in both cases, Wernicke’s area seems to handle **meaningful sounds in general**. Language is one important case of that.","children":[]}],"aphasias-23":[{"text":"Nobody fully agrees yet on how language-specific the deficits in aphasia are, so it is **still a controversy**. This is because the findings point two ways. Some patients have special trouble with complex grammar, which suggests some parts of language are handled in **specialized** ways. The same patients also struggle with action meanings or environmental sounds when no language is involved. That suggests language **shares brain systems** with other kinds of meaning. A full explanation has to cover both.","children":[]},{"text":"What is clear is **how the terms of the debate have evolved**. The field now has three kinds of tools.","children":["**Relatively sophisticated tests that tap specific aspects of language**, like reversible and improbable sentences, cleft sentences and nonverbal matching tasks.","**Quantitative analyses of behavior**, measuring accuracy across sentence types, patient groups and stress conditions.","**Quantitative analyses of structure-function relationships**, linking patterns of brain damage to performance across many patients."]},{"text":"So the field has come **a long way from the 19th century**. Wernicke and Lichtheim drew boxes and arrows from a few single cases. Modern research can test which specific operation is damaged. It can also map that damage onto brain tissue across large groups of patients.","children":[]}],"aphasias-24":[],"aphasias-25":[{"text":"Across many patients, damage toward the front goes with **fluency** problems and damage toward the back goes with **comprehension** problems. This is because **voxel-based lesion-symptom mapping** uses MRI data to formalize **structure-function relationships**. It comes from Bates, Wilson, Saygin, Dick, Sereno, Knight and Dronkers.","children":[]},{"text":"The method works one small spot at a time.","children":["A **voxel** is a tiny three-dimensional unit of an MRI brain image, like a 3D pixel.","At each voxel, patients with damage there are compared with patients without damage there.","If the damaged patients score much worse, that voxel is linked to the symptom.","Colors go from blue for a weaker link to red for a stronger link."]},{"text":"The **“symptoms”** here are **defined very broadly**. **Fluency deficits** link most strongly to the front part of the left hemisphere. **Comprehension deficits** link most strongly to the posterior temporal region, farther back.","children":[]},{"text":"So data from many patients fits a **rough double dissociation**. **Posterior regions** go with **perception**, or comprehension. **Anterior regions** go with **production**, or fluency. Since each patient adds a damage pattern and a score, many single cases become one statistical map.","children":[]}],"development1-01":[],"development1-02":[{"text":"**Nativists** say children are born with knowledge of what language is like. This view grew out of **Chomsky's critique of Skinner**. **Skinner** was a behaviorist who explained behavior, including language, with general learning such as reward and punishment. Chomsky said that kind of learning cannot explain how children get grammar.","children":[]},{"text":"Chomsky said the important thing about language is **generativity** (being able to make and understand an unlimited number of new sentences). This is because most sentences we hear or say are new. So learning a language means **figuring out the abstract grammar** that makes the sentences of that language.","children":[]},{"text":"Behaviorist learning is not up to that job, for two reasons.","children":["Grammatical and ungrammatical sentences are **not \"similar\"** in any simple way. One tiny change can make a sentence ungrammatical, and two grammatical sentences can look totally different.","Learning language does not look like **operant conditioning** (changing behavior with rewards and punishments that come after it). Children are not reliably rewarded for good sentences or punished for bad ones. So the reward and punishment links **do not hold**."]},{"text":"So nativists claim two things instead. Children have innate knowledge called **Universal Grammar** (built-in principles that limit which grammars a child will consider). Children also use **language-specific acquisition processes** (learning tools built just for language).","children":[]},{"text":"Chomsky backed this up by writing, \"The fact that all normal children acquire essentially comparable grammars of great complexity with remarkable rapidity suggests that human beings are somehow specially designed to do this, with data-handling or 'hypothesis-formulating' ability of unknown character and complexity\". Since every typical child gets a complex grammar fast, even with very different experiences, the ability looked built in.","children":[]}],"development1-03":[{"text":"At first, **categorical perception** (hearing speech sounds as categories) looked special to humans and to speech. This is because only humans had been tested, and only with speech sounds. So early theories called it **language-specific** (only for language) and **species-specific** (only in humans).","children":[]},{"text":"The classic evidence uses fourteen made-up consonant sounds. The upper formant transition changes in equal steps from one sound to the next, and the lower formant stays the same.","children":["When people **label** the sounds, they switch sharply from **/b/** to **/d/** around sounds 4 and 5. They switch from /d/ to **/g/** around sounds 9 and 10.","When people **tell pairs apart**, they do best right at those boundaries. They do worse for pairs inside one category. This holds for pairs one, two or three steps apart."]},{"text":"So whether two sounds seem alike depends on the **language categories**, not on the physical difference. This is because two sounds in the same category sound almost the same. Two sounds on opposite sides of a boundary sound clearly different. Both pairs can differ by the same acoustic amount. Since the pattern seemed tied to speech, it was taken as a sign of a special speech system.","children":[]}],"development1-04":[{"text":"Researchers find out what infants hear by watching **head turns** and **sucking**. This is because infants cannot tell us, but these behaviors change when an infant notices something new.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **conditioned head turn procedure** (used in Janet Werker's infant studies lab), the infant learns to turn toward a sound when it changes.","children":["The infant sits on a parent's lap, and an experimenter keeps the infant looking forward with toys.","When the sound changes and the infant turns, a lighted, moving toy rewards the turn.","Once the infant has learned this, a head turn right after a change means the infant **heard the change**."]},{"text":"In **high-amplitude sucking**, the infant sucks on a pacifier hooked to a pressure recorder. Strong sucks make a sound play.","children":["When the same sound keeps playing, the infant gets bored and sucks less. This is **habituation** (getting used to a repeated sound).","If a new sound makes sucking **go back up**, the infant **noticed the change**."]},{"text":"Neither method needs the infant to name or make a sound. The experimenter picks exactly which sound difference to play. Since only the sound difference changes, a head turn or renewed sucking tells us whether infants hear the same differences adults hear.","children":[]}],"development1-05":[{"text":"Four-month-olds noticed a change in **voice onset time** only when it crossed an adult category boundary. **Eimas and colleagues (1971)** found this using high-amplitude sucking. **Voice onset time**, or VOT, is the delay between releasing a stop consonant and the start of the vocal folds vibrating. With +100 milliseconds of VOT, voicing starts much later than with +10 milliseconds.","children":[]},{"text":"Infants heard one syllable over and over until their sucking dropped. Then the sound changed or stayed the same.","children":["In **20D**, the change was 20 milliseconds, from +20 to +40. The two sounds belong to **different** adult categories.","In **20S**, the change was also 20 milliseconds, from −20 to 0 or from +60 to +80. The two sounds belong to the **same** category.","In **0**, the **control** group, the sound never changed."]},{"text":"Only the 20D infants started sucking more again. In 20D, sucking jumped back up to around 50 per minute. In 20S, sucking barely recovered and kept dropping. In the control, sucking kept dropping to about 15 per minute.","children":[]},{"text":"This matters because 20D and 20S were the same size of change. So the only difference was **crossing the category boundary**. Since the control shows what repetition alone does, the jump in 20D must come from hearing a new category.","children":[]}],"development1-06":[{"text":"Eimas's result was a big deal because it made categorical perception look **innate**. This is because categorical perception was thought to be the **first step** in turning speech into a **symbolic format**. A symbolic format stores a sound as a category, such as /b/. Later language processes would use that format. So finding it in infants meant the starting point for language was already there.","children":[]},{"text":"Four-month-olds are really little.","children":["Their **gross motor milestones** (big body movements) are things like handling objects with two hands. They are also just holding their head and chest up while lying face down (prone).","Their color vision and **visual acuity** (sharpness of vision) are still \"in progress.\"","They are only starting to make **vowel-like sounds** instead of creaky whines."]},{"text":"So this is very, very early for a skill that seems to be **just for language**. This is because infants were sorting speech sounds into categories before they could make those sounds. Their other skills were still immature. Since speech categories showed up so far ahead of everything else, the ability seemed built in, not learned.","children":[]}],"development1-07":[{"text":"English-learning infants can hear non-English sound differences at six months, but they mostly lose them by twelve months. This is **perceptual narrowing** (losing sensitivity to sound differences your language does not use).","children":[]},{"text":"**Werker and Tees (1984)** tested English-learning infants on two non-English contrasts. One is a **Hindi** contrast between two \"ta\"-like sounds. The other is a **Salish** contrast between \"ki\" and \"qi\" from Nthlakampx, a Salish language. They counted how many infants reached a **discrimination criterion** at each age.","children":["At 6 to 8 months, about 95% passed for Hindi and about 80% for Salish.","At 8 to 10 months, about 67% passed for Hindi and about 52% for Salish.","At 10 to 12 months, only about 20% passed for Hindi and about 10% for Salish.","Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants passed for their own contrast close to 100% of the time."]},{"text":"The drop happens because of **language experience**. This is because infants who hear the contrast in their language keep it. English-learning infants never hear it, so they lose it by about their first birthday. This is **perceptual reorganization** in the first year of life.","children":[]},{"text":"Narrowing is related to **categorical perception** because infants are forming their native categories. Sounds their language puts in one category get harder to tell apart. So a non-English difference inside one English category ends up heard as a **within-category difference**.","children":[]}],"development1-08":[{"text":"The compelling story says babies are born able to hear every speech contrast, and learning just throws out the ones they do not need. This is because it puts the infant findings together in three steps.","children":["Speech perception depends on **categorical perception** abilities that are a special part of the language system.","These abilities show up very early in development.","Children start out as **\"citizens of the world\"** (able to hear all contrasts)."]},{"text":"Being citizens of the world means infants have innate, **language-general** knowledge about speech (knowledge that works for the sounds of every language). So a newborn is ready for any human language. Then children quickly **tune** their hearing to the one language around them.","children":[]},{"text":"In this story, learning only **selects**. This is because the contrasts are already there from birth. Learning narrows down the **inventory** (the full set of contrasts the child can hear). It keeps the contrasts that matter in the native language. It drops the ones the native language does not use. Since learning only removes things, the native language just decides which innate categories survive.","children":[]}],"development1-09":[{"text":"The citizens-of-the-world story has four problems. Three are about **categorical perception**, and one is about infants.","children":[]},{"text":"Categorical perception is not special to language or to humans.","children":["It is **not limited to language**, because it also happens for non-speech sounds.","It is **not limited to humans**, because animals without language also hear speech sounds as categories."]},{"text":"Categorical perception is also **task-dependent** (how categorical it looks depends on the task). This is because naming a sound and judging whether two sounds differ can give different results. So it is not always as categorical as the story assumes.","children":[]},{"text":"Infant speech perception is **not actually \"universal.\"** This is because infants do not start out hearing every contrast equally well. Some contrasts are hard for young infants to hear, even contrasts that are in their own language. So not every contrast is ready and waiting at birth.","children":[]},{"text":"Since some contrasts get better with experience, a full account has to explain gains as well as losses. That means learning must **build** categories, not only remove them.","children":[]}],"development1-10":[{"text":"People hear **non-speech** sounds as categories too, so categorical perception does not need a speech system. **Cutting and Rosner (1974)** showed this by changing **rise time** (how fast a sound gets to full loudness after it starts). A **short** rise time sounds like a **plucked** string. A **long** rise time sounds like a **bowed** string.","children":[]},{"text":"They used **sawtooth waves**, **speech syllables** and **sine waves**. Each one was made with a 10-millisecond rise and a 70-millisecond rise. A 10-millisecond rise hits full size almost right away. A 70-millisecond rise grows slowly.","children":[]},{"text":"For the sawtooth waves, labels switched sharply in the middle. Short rise times got called pluck almost 100% of the time. Long rise times got called bow almost 100% of the time. The switch happened between about 30 and 50 milliseconds.","children":[]},{"text":"Telling pairs apart was hardest inside a category and easiest at the switch. This is because pairs 10 milliseconds apart were told apart only about 55 to 60% of the time for most pairs. Near the switch, it **went up to about 75%**.","children":[]},{"text":"Pluck and bow are kinds of sound onset, not speech categories. Since the same label boundary and discrimination peak show up without any phonemes, categorical perception can come from **general hearing**.","children":[]}],"development1-11":[{"text":"Infants also hear **non-speech** sounds as categories. **Jusczyk and colleagues (1977)** found this with high-amplitude sucking. This means infants' early categorical perception is a **general hearing** ability, not a speech ability.","children":[]},{"text":"Infants heard one sound over and over until their sucking dropped. Then they got one of three conditions.","children":["With **no change**, sucking started near 20 per minute, dropped to about 12 and stayed there.","With a change **within a category**, sucking also stayed near 12.","With a change **between categories**, sucking jumped back up to about 18. Then it dropped again as the new sound got familiar."]},{"text":"So infants reacted only when the non-speech change **crossed a category boundary**. This is the same pattern Eimas and colleagues found for speech.","children":[]},{"text":"This ties to the adult results. Adults label and tell apart plucked and bowed sounds as categories. Infants tell apart non-speech changes as categories. Since neither group needs to know a language to do this, early categorical perception comes from general hearing.","children":[]}],"development1-12":[{"text":"Chinchillas hear **speech sounds** as categories, the same way humans do. **Ohlemiller and colleagues (1999)** showed this, following earlier work by **Kuhl and Miller (1975)**. This means categorical perception of speech is not just a human thing.","children":[]},{"text":"The chinchillas were trained with **operant conditioning** (a behavior gets more likely when a reward follows it). Each chinchilla learned that either a 0-millisecond lag sound or an 80-millisecond lag sound was an **SD** for food. **SD** means **discriminative stimulus** (a signal that a response will get rewarded). The lag is the **voice onset time**. Humans hear a 0-millisecond lag as /ga/ and an 80-millisecond lag as /ka/.","children":[]},{"text":"Then the chinchillas and some humans heard the in-between sounds along the /ga/-/ka/ continuum.","children":["At short VOTs, they called almost every sound /ga/.","At long VOTs, they almost never called it /ga/.","The switch was steep, mostly between about 30 and 70 milliseconds.","The chinchillas' curves had the **same steep shape** as the humans' curves."]},{"text":"Making the **burst** (the short noise when a stop is released) and **aspiration** (the breathy noise after a voiceless release) louder changed the results. The boundary moved to a **shorter VOT**, the switch got steeper, or both. This happened for one chinchilla and one human listener. Since both species reacted the same way, categorical perception of speech is shared with other animals.","children":[]}],"development1-13":[{"text":"Four-month-olds do not easily hear the difference between **/na/** and **/ŋa/**. This means some contrasts are **less salient** (harder to notice) than others for infants. **Narayan and colleagues (2010)** found this.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because both contrasts tested use **nasal consonants** in the **onset** (the start of a syllable), but the places differ. They differ in **place of articulation** (where in the mouth the sound is made). The sound /m/ is made with the lips. The sound /n/ is made with the tongue behind the teeth. The sound /ŋ/, the last sound in \"sing,\" is made with the back of the tongue against the soft palate.","children":[]},{"text":"English-hearing infants aged 4 to 5 months heard **same** trials and **change** trials. Looking longer on change trials means they noticed the change.","children":["For **[ma]-[na]**, they looked about 3.3 seconds on same trials and about 5.4 seconds on change trials. So they noticed it.","For **[na]-[ŋa]**, they looked about 4.5 seconds on same trials and about 4.2 seconds on change trials. So they did not notice it."]},{"text":"So /ma/-/na/ is **relatively salient**, but /na/-/ŋa/ is not readily heard. This is true **no matter what language** the infant hears. Since the difference comes from the sounds themselves, not every contrast starts out equally easy.","children":[]}],"development1-14":[{"text":"Hearing the /na/-/ŋa/ contrast in a **Filipino** home makes infants better at hearing it. **Narayan and colleagues (2010)** found this between 6 and 10 months. This means not all speech contrasts are available from birth.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because Filipino uses [na] and [ŋa] as different syllables. So Filipino-learning infants hear this contrast a lot.","children":["At 6 to 8 months, they looked about 3.8 seconds on both same and change trials. So they did **not yet notice** the switch.","At 10 to 12 months, they looked about 3.3 seconds on same trials and about 4.8 seconds on change trials. So they **noticed** it.","English-hearing infants at 4 to 5 months did not notice this contrast, though they did notice [ma]-[na]."]},{"text":"So it **cannot be that all speech contrasts are innate**. This is because the contrast starts out weak, even for infants whose language uses it. Hearing it over the first year makes it easier to detect.","children":[]},{"text":"So **exposure** to contrasts is **necessary** for learning them in some cases. Since learning can make a contrast stronger, development is not only losing unused contrasts. It also means building up hard ones.","children":[]}],"development1-15":[{"text":"The alternative to nativism is the **emergentist** approach. It says general **associative learning** picks up **statistical regularities** in the environment. Associative learning is learning that two things go together because they keep happening together. Statistical regularities are patterns in how often things happen and what happens with what. Language structure then grows out of these patterns in the input.","children":[]},{"text":"The simplest case is classical conditioning, with three neurons. One is for a **bell**, one detects **food** and one makes the animal **salivate**.","children":["The food neuron is already strongly connected to salivation, so food makes the animal salivate.","When the bell keeps happening along with food, the **synapse** (a connection between neurons) from the bell neuron to the salivation neuron gets **stronger**.","After enough pairings, the bell alone makes the animal salivate."]},{"text":"The same idea can go beyond conditioning to **pattern recognition** (spotting a repeating structure, such as a speech sound, across many different examples). This is because the same strengthening could connect sound features that tend to happen together. In conditioning, one event comes to predict another. In pattern recognition, many features come to predict a category.","children":[]},{"text":"Since connections grow from what keeps happening together, infants could learn speech categories by tracking which sounds go together and how often. They would not need a full set of built-in categories.","children":[]}],"development1-16":[{"text":"We might learn each vowel by keeping track of how its sounds are spread out, and the most typical vowel sits at the center. This is what **Kuhl and others** mean by learning the **statistics of distributions** in the environment. A **distribution** tells how often each value turns up across many examples.","children":[]},{"text":"The example is the **American vowels** in a **vowel space** made from the **first and second formants**.","children":["Each time someone says a vowel, that one production has a first formant value and a second formant value.","Many productions of the same vowel bunch together in one area.","The areas for different vowels **overlap** a lot. So one pair of formant values can belong to more than one vowel. That is why sorting speech sounds is hard."]},{"text":"So listeners have to learn where each vowel is centered and how far it spreads. This is because over many exposures, they can track where each vowel's productions bunch up. Their categories then match the shape of what they hear.","children":[]},{"text":"A **\"prototypical\" /a/** is then just the **mean** (the average) or maybe the **mode** (the most common value) of all the /a/ sounds we hear. Since the prototype comes from experience, it can be different across languages and listeners.","children":[]}],"development1-17":[{"text":"Yes, infants really do learn categories from how often sounds occur. **Maye and colleagues (2002)** had infants **listen passively** (just listen, with no task or reward) to an eight-step **[da]-[ta] continuum**. The continuum changes in **voice onset time**.","children":[]},{"text":"Two groups heard the same sounds, but how often each sound played was different.","children":["The **bimodal** group heard sounds 2 and 7 the most, 16 times each, and the middle sounds rarely. Two peaks suggest **two categories**.","The **unimodal** group heard sounds 4 and 5 in the middle the most, and the ends rarely. One peak suggests **one category**."]},{"text":"At test, every infant heard **sounds 3 and 6**. In **alternating** trials, 3 and 6 switched back and forth. In **non-alternating** trials, one sound repeated. If infants hear 3 and 6 as different, the two kinds of trials should hold their attention differently.","children":[]},{"text":"Only the **bimodal** infants treated the two kinds of trials differently.","children":["Bimodal six-month-olds looked 5.66 seconds on alternating trials and 6.41 seconds on non-alternating trials. Bimodal eight-month-olds looked 5.45 and 6.15 seconds.","Unimodal six-month-olds looked 4.85 and 4.53 seconds. Unimodal eight-month-olds looked 4.98 and 5.20 seconds, about the same."]},{"text":"This is because the unimodal infants seem to have learned to treat 3 and 6 as the **same category**. Since a few minutes of listening changed how they grouped sounds, infants do use the statistics of what they hear.","children":[]}],"development1-18":[{"text":"Infants learn speech sounds better from a **live, responsive person** than from a recording. Two experiments show **social interaction** matters for learning.","children":[]},{"text":"In the first, nine-month-old American infants heard **Mandarin Chinese** in twelve 25-minute sessions.","children":["The **live** group had a Mandarin speaker playing and reading with them in person.","Other groups got the same kind of Mandarin from audio or TV.","A control group heard English."]},{"text":"Only the live group learned the Mandarin contrast. We know this because when tested with the **head-turn technique**, live infants got about 65% correct. English controls got about 56%. TV and audio infants scored about the same as controls, near chance.","children":[]},{"text":"In the second, eight-month-olds got responses from their mothers when they made sounds.","children":["In the **contingent** condition, mothers answered right after the infant vocalized (**contingent** means the response depends on the infant's behavior).","In the **yoked-control** condition, mothers answered on another infant's schedule, so the answers were **noncontingent**.","Familiarization lasted 30 minutes, then baseline, social response and **extinction** (answers stopped) lasted 10 minutes each."]},{"text":"Contingent answers made infants produce **more syllables**. At baseline, both groups produced few. With social response, the contingent group went up to about 0.32, but the noncontingent group stayed near 0.05. In extinction, the contingent group stayed at about 0.2.","children":[]},{"text":"So social responses that depend on the infant's sounds act like a **reinforcing stimulus** (something that makes a behavior more likely). One idea is that humans find social interaction **innately reinforcing**. Another is that we are innately good at using **social cues**.","children":[]}],"development1-19":[{"text":"Statistical learning is limited by **constraints**, and constraints can mean three different things. **Statistical learning with constraints** is learning from patterns in the input that is guided and limited.","children":[]},{"text":"The first is about which sound properties can be **contrastive** (able to tell words apart, such as voicing in /b/ versus /p/). **Speaker** voice and **speech rate** change the sound a lot, but they do not change which word is said. So they are **not contrastive**. This matters because a learner tracking every pattern would make categories for different voices and speeds.","children":[]},{"text":"The second comes from **earlier learning**. This is because what a learner already knows changes what is easy to learn next. Once native categories are set up, new differences that cut across them are harder to notice. This kind of constraint is central to **sensitive periods** (times when earlier experience limits later learning).","children":[]},{"text":"The third comes from **social and interactive** settings. This is because sounds are learned better in live, responsive interaction than from a recording. Caregivers' contingent responses also shape the sounds infants make.","children":[]},{"text":"Learning depends on the input, on what the learner already knows and on the social setting. Since all three guide it, learning can be fast and stick to the differences that matter.","children":[]}],"development2-01":[],"development2-02":[{"text":"Speech has no physical boundaries between words, but we are still really good at finding the words. Finding where one word stops and the next one starts in continuous speech is called **word segmentation**.","children":[]},{"text":"Speech is **messy** in this way. This is because the sound energy of each word flows straight into the next word. In “I hope it’s in my desk,” no silent gap separates the words from one another.","children":[]},{"text":"The pauses in speech do not mark the words either. This is because the pauses come from **closures** (moments when the vocal tract is fully blocked, as for /p/, /t/, /d/ and /k/). Very little sound gets out during a closure, so a short silence appears. Closures are **as likely to happen within words as between words**. In “hope” and “it’s,” the silences fall inside words or at their edges. Since the silences do not line up with word boundaries, a listener who used them would split some words and merge others.","children":[]},{"text":"Adults still hear separate words in this “sea of sounds” with no effort. They can lean on the words they already know. The **harder question** is **how we learn to do this**. This is because infants do not know the words yet. They have to find the words in the stream before they can learn what the words mean.","children":[]}],"development2-03":[{"text":"There are **several cues to word boundaries**, but no single cue works for every word.","children":[]},{"text":"The first cue is that some words **appear in isolation**, as in “Hello,” “No,” “Please,” “Go” and “Stop.” A word said alone has clear edges. Not all words are said alone, so this cue only covers part of the vocabulary.","children":[]},{"text":"The second cue is **phonotactics** (the patterns for which sound sequences a language allows). This helps because **some sequences of phonemes are very unlikely to occur within words**. In “wordboundary,” the sequence /db/ almost never occurs inside an English word, so it suggests a break between word and boundary. In “speechcode,” the sequence /tʃk/ suggests a break between speech and code. “Movement” needs care, because its /vm/ sequence might suggest a break even though movement is one word.","children":[]},{"text":"The third cue is **stress** (one syllable being louder, longer or higher in pitch). This helps because **in English, most words are stressed on the first syllable**. **Jusczyk and colleagues (1999)** let **7.5-month-old infants** get familiar with one word, and then played them passages. One passage kept using “kingdom,” as in “Your kingdom is in a faraway place”. The other passage kept using “device,” as in “Your device can do a lot”. Infants who had heard “kingdom” **preferred the kingdom passage**. Infants who had heard “device” **did not show the same kind of preference**. This is because kingdom is stressed on its first syllable and device is stressed on its second syllable. An infant who starts a new word at every stressed syllable finds kingdom easily but cuts device in the wrong place.","children":[]},{"text":"So stress **may help signal the beginnings of words** in connected speech. Since languages put stress in different places, this cue will not work in every language or for every word in English.","children":[]}],"development2-04":[{"text":"Two syllables inside one word go together much more often than two syllables from two different words. A learner can use this to find word boundaries, and the cue is called **transitional probabilities**. Using it is a kind of **statistical learning** (picking up patterns in how often things occur together).","children":[]},{"text":"Take the phrase “pretty baby.” In English words, the syllable **/ti/** is **very strongly predicted by the syllable /prɪ/**. This is because pretty is a common word, so after “pre” the next syllable is very often “ty.” The pair “ty” and “ba” crosses from one word into the next. This pair is **less predictable**. This is because many different words can come after “ty,” so “ba” only follows it when pretty happens to be followed by baby.","children":[]},{"text":"The comparison can be written with frequencies (how often something occurs). The frequency of “pretty” divided by the frequency of “pre” is the chance that “ty” comes after “pre.” The frequency of “tyba” divided by the frequency of “ty” is the chance that “ba” comes after “ty.” The first number is **much bigger** than the second number.","children":[]},{"text":"Pairs inside a word keep coming up together, and pairs across a word boundary do not. Since this is true, a learner who counts which syllable follows which can find where the words are.","children":[]}],"development2-05":[{"text":"Syllable B is more likely to come after syllable A **within a word than between words**. This likelihood is the **transitional probability** from A to B. This is because inside a word the same syllables always come in the same order, so the probability is high. At a word boundary almost any word can come next, so the probability is lower. Since the probability goes down at a boundary, a drop in transitional probability is a good guess for where a word ends.","children":[]},{"text":"This cue is **probabilistic**, which means it works most of the time but not every time. “What’s that in the road a/head?” can be heard as “a head” or as “ahead.” The sounds are the same both ways, so the listener uses the meaning of the whole sentence to decide. “I scream you scream we all scream for ice cream” plays on “I scream” and “ice cream” having almost the same sounds. The same run of sounds can be split into words in two ways.","children":[]},{"text":"Transitional probabilities make one way of splitting the stream more likely than another. They work together with stress, phonotactics and words heard alone. When the cues agree, finding the words is easy. When the cues disagree or are missing, as with “a head” and “ahead,” listeners use context.","children":[]}],"development2-06":[{"text":"**Saffran and colleagues (1996)** took away every cue to word boundaries except **transitional probabilities**. This is because they wanted to find out if transitional probabilities alone are enough to identify words. So the stimuli were **tightly controlled**.","children":[]},{"text":"The speech was **monotone** (no changes in pitch, duration or amplitude, where amplitude is loudness). This took away stress, so stress could not mark where words begin.","children":[]},{"text":"The syllables were **simple CV syllables** (one consonant followed by one vowel). This took away **phonotactic cues**, because no unusual consonant clusters ever came up.","children":[]},{"text":"The syllables were **presented continuously** (with no pauses). This took away silence, so silence could not mark a boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"The researchers made an **artificial language** out of a set of **three-syllable “words,”** such as tokibu, gikoba and gopila. The words were joined in random order into one long stream. The only thing that told where a word began and ended was the transitional probability between syllables. Since every other cue was gone, infants who found the words must have used transitional probabilities alone.","children":[]}],"development2-07":[{"text":"The stream that the infant hears has no breaks in it at all. It sounds like “tokibugikobagopilatipolutokibu” and “gopilatipolutokibugikobagopila” and “gikobatokibugopilatipolugikoba.” There are no spaces and no pauses.","children":[]},{"text":"There are **four words** hidden in the stream, which are tokibu, gikoba, gopila and tipolu. Each word comes up many times in random order.","children":[]},{"text":"The words can still be found. This is because the syllables follow **regular patterns**. Inside **tokibu**, “to” is always followed by “ki,” and “ki” is always followed by “bu.” After “bu,” the stream can go on with “gi,” “go” or “ti.” Which one comes next depends on which word comes next. So some syllable pairs are **fully predictable** and other pairs are not.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the order of the syllables is the only signal, a learner has to keep track of which syllables follow which. A learner who does this can find the words from the statistics of the stream.","children":[]}],"development2-08":[{"text":"The four words become easy to see when each word is marked in its own color. Tokibu is red, gikoba is green, gopila is pink and tipolu is purple. The infant does not get any marking like this. The infant only hears one unbroken run of syllables.","children":[]},{"text":"The marked words make one pattern clear. Each word keeps the **same inner order** every time, but the words next to it keep changing. “To-ki-bu” always comes in that order. After tokibu, the next word can be gikoba, gopila or tipolu.","children":[]},{"text":"**Transitional probabilities** (how likely one syllable is to follow another) are **high inside each word** and **lower across word edges**. This is because the syllables inside a word always follow each other. At the end of a word, the next syllable could start any of the other words.","children":[]},{"text":"The word edges are exactly the places where the transitional probability drops. The signal has no spaces in it. Since predictability drops at each word edge, a learner who notices the drops can find where one word ends and the next begins.","children":[]}],"development2-09":[{"text":"To test for an effect of **transitional probabilities**, the infants had to compare items that they had heard **equally often**. This is because after a long stream of syllables, infants are more familiar with sequences they heard than with ones they did not hear. That is trivial to find. An infant could prefer the heard sequences only because they are familiar, without using transitional probabilities at all.","children":[]},{"text":"Two extra details fix this problem. The first is that **half of the words were repeated more frequently** than the other half. The second is that the test compared words with **part-words** (three-syllable sequences that cross a word boundary). A part-word is made from the end of one word and the start of the next. The part-words were **matched for frequency of occurrence** with real words.","children":[]},{"text":"Take the stream “tokibu gikoba gopila gikoba tokibu gopila tokibu gikoba gikoba tokibu.” The word **GOPILA** appears twice and the part-word **BU-GIKO** also appears twice. The probability of GI given BU is **2 out of 4**. This is because BU comes up four times, at the end of each tokibu, and GI comes after it only two of those times. The transitional probabilities inside GOPILA are both **2 out of 2**. This is because PI comes after GO both times, and LA comes after PI both times.","children":[]},{"text":"Since gopila and bu-giko are heard equally often, familiarity cannot explain a difference between them. A difference has to come from how predictable the syllables are.","children":[]}],"development2-10":[{"text":"Infants **looked longer in the direction of the part-words** than the words. This is called a **novelty effect**. Words got about 6.8 seconds of looking and part-words got about 7.6 seconds. The difference was statistically significant.","children":[]},{"text":"Looking longer at the part-words is a **novelty preference** (infants look longer at whatever seems new). This is because the words had become familiar during listening, so they were less interesting. The part-words seemed less familiar to the infants. This can be confusing, because the infants had heard the part-words just as often as the words.","children":[]},{"text":"The only thing that was different about the part-words was their transitional probabilities. The syllables in a part-word do not reliably go together. Since frequency was the same and only the transitional probabilities were different, the infants must have noticed the transitional probabilities.","children":[]},{"text":"So the infants **had learned the statistical regularities of the speech stream**. They were **not merely picking up on simple frequency of occurrence**. After a short exposure, they pulled units out of a continuous stream using only how predictable the syllables were.","children":[]}],"development2-11":[{"text":"Finding a word in speech does not tell a child what the word means. Children can now “find” the words, but they still have to figure out each word’s **referent** (whatever the word refers to). This problem is called **identifying word referents**.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because **segmentation** only gives the child a chunk of sound that keeps coming back. The chunk of sound has no information about meaning in it. A child may hear “cat” while looking at a cat. Recognizing the sound pattern “cat” is one achievement. Knowing that “cat” names the furry animal is a different achievement.","children":[]},{"text":"Figuring out the meaning is hard because any scene has many objects, parts, properties and actions in it. The child needs more evidence to tell which of these the speaker has in mind.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the sound does not give the meaning, the child has to link the word’s form to what the speaker means. The child gets that evidence from the situation in which the word is used.","children":[]}],"development2-12":[{"text":"A child who hears a new word once cannot tell what it means, because one experience fits many possible meanings. This is called the **inductive problem of early word learning**. **Induction** is reasoning from particular examples to a general rule, and here the rule is the meaning of a word.","children":[]},{"text":"Say a child hears a new word while watching a cat. The word might mean **CAT**, **ANIMAL** or **PET**, which are categories at different levels. It might be **FELIX**, the name of this one cat. It might name a property, such as **FURRY** or **ALIVE**. It might name an action, such as **WALKING**. It might name a part, such as **WHISKERS** or **LEG**.","children":[]},{"text":"All of these possible meanings together are the **hypothesis space**. The child cannot pick one from a single scene. This is because every possible meaning fits what the child sees, and nothing in the scene singles out the one the speaker means.","children":[]},{"text":"This is a form of **Quine’s “gavagai” problem**. A linguist studying an unknown language hears a speaker say “gavagai” as a rabbit runs by. “Gavagai” might mean rabbit, but it might also mean undetached rabbit parts, a stage of a rabbit or something else.","children":[]},{"text":"Children have this problem with every new word, and they still learn words fast. Since they learn fast, something must cut down the possibilities. Joint attention, cues to what the speaker intends and default strategies do this.","children":[]}],"development2-13":[{"text":"Children learn what words mean partly through **joint attention** (sharing an attentional focus with another person). In joint attention, two people look at the same object. Each one knows that the other is paying attention to it too.","children":[]},{"text":"Joint attention helps with word meaning. This is because when the adult says a word, the object they are both looking at is the most likely referent. So the word gets tied to what the speaker is paying attention to. This makes the **hypothesis space** (the set of possible meanings) smaller.","children":[]},{"text":"Infants can join an adult’s focus once they can follow gaze. **Gaze following** (looking where someone else is looking) **emerges around 9 months of age**. When an adult looks over at an object, the infant follows the adult’s eyes to it.","children":[]},{"text":"Gaze following **signals an understanding of reference**. This is because an infant who follows gaze understands that the adult’s attention is aimed at something specific. Since the infant knows what the adult is attending to, the infant can link the adult’s word to that object.","children":[]}],"development2-14":[{"text":"**Eighteen-month-olds learned a new word** when an adult was interacting with them, but **not** when the word came from a voice behind a screen. **Baldwin and colleagues** found this, and it means that **joint attention optimizes word learning**.","children":[]},{"text":"There were two groups of infants. Both groups heard a novel word while playing with an object. In the **joint attention group**, an adult interacting with the baby labeled the object the baby was playing with. In the **no joint attention group**, a voice from behind a screen labeled the object the baby was playing with.","children":[]},{"text":"The two groups were the same in most ways. In both groups, the word came while the infant was focused on the same object. The only difference was whether a visible person was engaged with the infant and the object.","children":[]},{"text":"The 18-month-olds learned the novel word in the joint attention group but not in the no joint attention group. This is because hearing a word while seeing an object is not enough. Infants need evidence that a speaker means the word to refer to that object. Since the hidden voice gave the same word at the same moment, the difference in learning came from the visible, engaged speaker.","children":[]}],"development2-15":[{"text":"Children narrow the **hypothesis space** (the set of possible meanings) for new words by using **intentional cues from the speaker**. An **intentional cue** is a signal of what the speaker means to refer to. One intentional cue is **eye gaze direction**. This works because when a speaker looks toward one toy, the eyes mark which object the speaker is paying attention to.","children":[]},{"text":"**Baldwin** taught new words to infants of 16 to 17 months and infants of 18 to 19 months. There were two conditions. In **follow-in labeling**, the adult looked at the toy that the infant was focused on while saying the new word. In **discrepant labeling**, the adult looked at a toy in a bucket while saying the new word. The toy in the bucket was a different toy from the one the infant was focused on.","children":[]},{"text":"The two conditions are needed for this reason. In follow-in labeling, the infant and the adult look at the same toy, so both cues point to one toy. In discrepant labeling, the infant’s focus and the speaker’s focus point to different toys. Infants who just attach a new word to whatever they are looking at would link it to their own toy. Infants who follow the speaker’s gaze would link it to the toy in the bucket. Since the two answers are different, comparing the conditions tells whether infants treat the speaker’s gaze as evidence of what the word means.","children":[]}],"development2-16":[{"text":"**Gaze direction** helps a child figure out which object a speaker is naming when there are several objects. This is because a speaker who looks at one object while saying a new word ties the word to that object. The child can follow the speaker’s eyes to the **intended referent** (the thing the speaker means).","children":[]},{"text":"The speaker’s focus can be different from the child’s own focus. Following the speaker’s focus means using information about someone else’s attention. Since the child uses the speaker’s focus, the child can attach a new word to an object. This works even if the child was not looking at that object when the word was said.","children":[]},{"text":"Gaze makes the possible meanings fewer even though the sound of the word gives no clue. This is because the child puts the new word together with evidence of what the speaker intends. To do this, the child has to understand that a speaker’s eyes point to what the speaker means. Since the eyes pick out one object, the child links the word to that object and not to everything in view.","children":[]}],"development2-17":[{"text":"Infants can use an adult’s **facial expression** to figure out which object a new word names. **Akhtar and colleagues** found that infants **used adults’ facial expressions to identify the intended referent of a novel word**.","children":[]},{"text":"This works because the adult’s emotional reaction to each object tells whether it is the one the adult meant. A satisfied expression means the speaker found the object the speaker wanted. A disappointed expression means the object is not the one the speaker meant. Since the expression picks out one object, infants linked the new word to that object.","children":[]},{"text":"These are intentional cues from the speaker, and there are two kinds here. **Affective cues** are emotional expressions. An expression can tell whether an object is the one the speaker was searching for. **Behavioral cues** are the speaker’s actions. An action, such as reaching for something, can tell what the speaker wants.","children":[]},{"text":"Several objects may be there when a word is spoken. The speaker’s reaction can single out one of them. Since the child has to read the reaction, learning a word involves understanding the goal behind the interaction.","children":[]}],"development2-18":[{"text":"A speaker’s **emotional reaction** can pick out which object a new word is for, when there are several objects. The reaction is an **intentional cue** (a signal of what the speaker means to refer to).","children":[]},{"text":"This is because satisfaction or excitement ties one object to what the speaker was trying to find or do. The learner can use that tie to understand a new label. Infants who do this are following the speaker’s goals. They understand that the speaker is searching for something. They understand that the word names the thing the speaker wants. Since the word names the wanted thing, the word gets attached to the object that meets the goal. Objects that disappoint the speaker are ruled out.","children":[]},{"text":"**Intention is conveyed through more than gaze direction**. Facial expressions and actions also tell what a speaker means. A word may be said near several objects, and they are not all equally likely to be the referent.","children":[]},{"text":"The useful information is in how the adult responds to each object. Connecting the label to that response makes the word’s **hypothesis space** (the set of meanings it might have) smaller.","children":[]}],"development2-19":[{"text":"Children expect a new word to name **a whole object**. This is the **whole object assumption**. It is one of the **word learning strategies**, which are **default strategies that children bring to the word learning situation**. A **default** is a starting assumption that holds until evidence says otherwise.","children":[]},{"text":"These strategies are there to **narrow the hypothesis space** (the set of possible meanings) for new words. They give children a place to start when other cues are weak.","children":[]},{"text":"With the whole object assumption, the child does not expect a new word to name a part of the object. The child also does not expect it to name the object’s color, its texture or something it is doing. A child who hears a new word while looking at a cat will pick “cat” over “whiskers,” “furry” or “walking.”","children":[]},{"text":"This makes word learning faster. This is because the assumption gets rid of many unlikely meanings at once. It also fits the way adults usually name objects for children.","children":[]},{"text":"The assumption is only a starting preference. Later evidence can override it. Since it can be overridden, children do learn words for parts and properties once the evidence points that way.","children":[]}],"development2-20":[{"text":"When a child hears a new word, the child gives it to an object that does not have a name yet. This is **mutual exclusivity**, from **Markman (1989)**.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because of a default that **objects can have only one name**. An object that already has a name is not expected to get a second one. So the implication is that **new words map to nameless objects**. Mutual exclusivity **emerges during the second half of the second year**, which is roughly between 18 and 24 months.","children":[]},{"text":"Say a child knows the word “dog” but has no word for a parrot, and both animals are in view. The child hears a new word. The dog already has a name, so the child decides the new word means the parrot. The child does not need a gaze cue or an emotional cue to make this choice.","children":[]},{"text":"Mutual exclusivity works together with the whole object assumption. The whole object assumption says a new word names an object. Mutual exclusivity says it names an object that has no name yet.","children":[]},{"text":"Mutual exclusivity is a default, so later learning can override it. This happens when an object turns out to have two names.","children":[]}],"development2-21":[{"text":"With **mutual exclusivity**, a child who hears an unfamiliar word gives it to an object that has no known name yet. This is because an object the child can already name is not likely to be the target of a new word. The child makes the choice by comparing the objects in view.","children":[]},{"text":"Which object gets the word depends on the child’s **prior vocabulary** (the words the child already knows). This is because prior vocabulary decides which objects count as already named. Two children in the same scene can make different guesses if they know different words. So the strategy builds on what the child learned earlier.","children":[]},{"text":"This makes vocabulary growth feed itself. A child who knows many names can rule out many objects. The objects without names that are left over become the most likely referents for new words. Since known names rule out objects, every word the child knows helps narrow the meaning of the next new word.","children":[]}],"development2-22":[{"text":"It is hard to tell how children start putting words together. This is because in production children produce only one, two or three words at a time. Very different processes can make the same short utterance.","children":[]},{"text":"**Menn** distinguishes among four kinds of early utterances. The first is **“just a bunch of relevant words.”** Here the child strings together words that fit the situation, and no grammar links them. The second is **formulas**. A formula is a phrase the child memorized as a whole unit, without knowing it has separate parts. The third is **word combinations**. Here the child joins separate words productively to make new combinations. The fourth is **stable constructions**. These are consistent patterns the child uses to build utterances, and they are **not necessarily adult-like**.","children":[]},{"text":"A child might say “Up!” or “Pick you up!” or “Mommy up!” “Pick you up!” sounds more advanced, but it may be a formula copied whole from what adults say. “Mommy up!” is shorter, but it may be a real combination of two separate words. So the length of an utterance cannot tell which process made it.","children":[]},{"text":"To tell these apart, a **full description of the context** in which the utterance is produced is **necessary, but often not sufficient**. Context is necessary because it tells what the child intends and how the utterance is used across situations. It is not always sufficient because the source of an utterance can stay uncertain even with complete context.","children":[]}],"development2-23":[{"text":"Children can understand **basic transitive word order** even when they can only say one word at a time. So **comprehension** is ahead of **production**. The **one-word stage** is when a child speaks only in single words. A **transitive** sentence has a verb with a subject and an object. In English, the actor comes first and the one acted on comes after the verb.","children":[]},{"text":"**Gertner and Fisher** explain this with the **structure-mapping account** of early **syntactic bootstrapping** (using sentence structure to help learn meaning).","children":[]},{"text":"The child hears the input sentence “The boy is tickling the girl!” and makes a **partial sentence representation** of it. This has the verb tickle, with boy as the first noun and girl as the second noun.","children":[]},{"text":"The child also sees the scene, which is a boy tickling a girl. The child makes **conceptual representations** of the events that could be going on. One is giggle with the girl as agent. One is tickle with the boy as agent and the girl as patient. Two more are play with the boy as agent and play with the girl as agent. An **agent** does an action and a **patient** is affected by it.","children":[]},{"text":"Then the child uses a **one-to-one mapping between nouns and participant roles**. This is because the sentence has two nouns, so it fits an event with two participants. The two nouns get lined up with the two participants in the tickling event. Since the nouns are matched to the participants, the child can use noun order to tell who did what. This matching also helps the child figure out what the verb means.","children":[]}],"development2-24":[{"text":"Two-year-olds can understand a transitive sentence even when the verb is made up. **Gertner and colleagues (2006)** found this with a **generalization test**.","children":[]},{"text":"The test works this way. If children have really learned the **transitive structure**, they will **generalize it to new verbs**. A child who only memorized particular sentences will be lost with an unfamiliar verb. A child who knows the structure can use word order to understand any verb.","children":[]},{"text":"The sentence was “The duck is gorping the bunny.” The child has to pick which of two novel actions the sentence could depict. “Gorp” is a made-up verb, so its meaning gives no help. The child has to know that the first noun is the doer and the second noun is the one acted on.","children":[]},{"text":"Children first heard “Hey look! The duck’s gonna gorp the bunny!” before the scenes. Then two scenes came up side by side. In one scene, a person dressed as a duck pushed a person dressed as a bunny in a wagon. In the other scene, the bunny acted on the duck. Children heard “The duck is gorping the bunny! See?” with the scenes. After a short break they heard “The duck gorped the bunny! Find gorping!” and the scenes came up again.","children":[]},{"text":"The two-year-olds **looked significantly longer at the duck acting on the rabbit** than at the reverse. This is because they used the order of the nouns to figure out who was doing the action.","children":[]},{"text":"Children at this age typically have a **mean length of utterance (MLU)** of **around 2**. MLU is the average number of morphemes (meaningful words or word parts) in each utterance. Their three-morpheme utterances might be like “Duck gorp bunny.”","children":[]},{"text":"Their own speech is this short. So their understanding of the transitive structure is ahead of what they can say.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-01":[],"l1-critical-02":[{"text":"A **critical period** and a **sensitive period** are different because of how strict the time limit on learning is. This is because a critical period has a **discrete, “programmed” onset and offset** (the window opens and closes at fixed points in development). Learning (change in response to the environment) is **strictly limited to this period**. There is **no sensitivity to the environment before or after**, so experience outside the window does nothing.","children":[]},{"text":"A **sensitive or “optimal” period** is looser. **Learning is possible throughout the life span**. Learning is just **optimal during particular windows**, when it is easiest or works best. These windows can have a **gradual onset and offset**, and experience can itself change when they start and end.","children":[]},{"text":"The two ideas predict different things for people who learn late.","children":["A critical period says late learning cannot happen. So if a late learner gets better at all, that goes against a critical period.","A sensitive period says late learning is harder but still possible. So if early learners keep a lasting advantage, that supports a sensitive period."]},{"text":"Since even the classic critical period in vision fits “sensitive period” better, the same question comes up for language.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-03":[{"text":"**Amblyopia** is **poor vision in one eye** even though the eye itself is healthy. The definition says despite normal **peripheral function** (the working of the eye itself). This is because the problem is in how the brain handles the input from that eye.","children":[]},{"text":"Three conditions can lead to amblyopia.","children":["**Strabismus** is when the eyes are misaligned. The two eyes point in different directions, so the brain gets two conflicting images.","A **cataract** is a clouding of the lens of the eye. It blurs or blocks the light that reaches the back of the eye.","**Anisometropia** is a large difference in focusing power between the two eyes. One eye's image is much blurrier than the other's."]},{"text":"Poor vision can last **long after the conditions themselves have been remediated** (corrected). This is because the conditions cause **periods of deprivation from normal visual input**. Fixing the eye brings the input back. The brain may still not use that input well. Since a temporary early problem can leave a lasting effect, when the problem happens matters as much as what it is.","children":["A cataract taken out early may leave little lasting damage.","A cataract taken out late may leave poor vision even after the lens is clear."]}],"l1-critical-04":[{"text":"**Amblyopia** happens because the two eyes compete in the brain and the weaker eye loses. This is because both eyes send input to **visual cortex**, and the two inputs **compete** to control the neurons there. These are the **competitive dynamics between cortical inputs**. When both eyes work normally, they share the neurons evenly. When one eye has abnormal vision, the other eye can **“colonize”** its cortical representation. The stronger eye takes over neurons that would have served the weaker eye.","children":[]},{"text":"**Monocular deprivation** (blocking or degrading the input to one eye) in mice reveals how the takeover happens over time.","children":["Within **2 to 3 days**, there is **increased spine motility**. **Dendritic spines** (small projections where neurons receive connections) become unstable and move around.","Within **3 to 4 days**, there is **spine loss and Wallerian degeneration** (the breakdown of nerve fibers cut off from their source). The deprived eye's connections disappear.","After **more than a week**, there is **axon sprouting and new spine birth**. The open eye's fibers branch out and form connections where the deprived eye lost them."]},{"text":"Fixing the eye later does not fix the vision. This is because the restored eye **remains at a competitive disadvantage**. The other eye now holds the connections. Ordinary viewing with both eyes open keeps favoring the stronger eye. Since the stronger eye keeps winning, **normal cortical responses cannot be restored by normal experience**.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-05":[{"text":"The “good” eye gets patched because the patch gives the weak eye a chance to compete again. This is because treatments for amblyopia are **based on competition**. One of the most effective treatments is **intermittently patching the “good” eye**. The patch blocks the stronger eye's input for part of the day. The brain then has to rely on the weaker eye. So the weaker eye's input can win back connections in cortex.","children":[]},{"text":"Patching **restores the competitive balance between the eyes**. Then the brain's own **plasticity** (its ability to change its connections in response to experience) can restore normal function.","children":["The patch does not repair the eye itself.","The patch changes the experience the brain gets, and plasticity does the rest."]},{"text":"Patching is **most effective early in development** and **may be ineffective by late childhood** (10 years). This is because a young brain is highly plastic, so rebalancing the input leads to fast reorganization. As the visual system matures, the same treatment helps less and less. Since the treatment works through plasticity, when it is given matters as much as the treatment itself.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-06":[{"text":"Adults with amblyopia can still get better, even after the critical period. **Levi and Polat (1996)** found that **plasticity is not abolished entirely**, even well into adulthood. Some of the patients were **in their 50s**, an age normally considered past the point where treatment works.","children":[]},{"text":"The adults did **long-term, intensive training** on specific **psychophysical tasks** (tasks that measure the link between a physical stimulus and what a person perceives). They used the affected eye. They practiced visual judgments for many **kilotrials** (one kilotrial is a thousand trials).","children":[]},{"text":"Getting better was measured with a **threshold** (the smallest stimulus a person can reliably detect). A lower threshold means better vision.","children":["Over the first **19 kilotrials**, the threshold **went down steadily**, and that change was highly significant.","From about **20 to 35 kilotrials**, the threshold **stayed flat**.","Around **36 kilotrials**, the threshold **went down again** to a new lower level and stayed there."]},{"text":"The training made the adults better at the task and also improved their **general visual function**. This is because the adult visual system keeps some **experience-dependent plasticity**. Since the right kind and amount of training can still change the adult visual system, late improvement is possible.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-07":[{"text":"Playing video games made vision better in adults with amblyopia. **Li, Ngo, Nguyen, Lam and Levi (2008)** had adults play an action video game with their amblyopic eye. The game was a first-person shooter set in a battle scene. Playing it **enhanced visual acuity and visual attention**.","children":["**Visual acuity** (sharpness of vision, the ability to see fine detail) got better, so vision through the weak eye was clearer.","**Visual attention** (the ability to pick out and focus on the important parts of a scene) got better. In a busy battle, the important parts are things like targets."]},{"text":"This is because action games demand fast visual decisions and constant attention. Those demands force the visual system to use the amblyopic eye's input. So the weak eye gets intense, varied practice.","children":[]},{"text":"The gains came from the training and not from normal development. This is because these adults were long past childhood visual development. They were also well past the age when patching usually works best. Since a demanding, engaging visual task still changed a mature visual system, adult amblyopia has **plasticity**. An early optimal window can close without removing every chance of improvement.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-08":[{"text":"**Amblyopia** is a classic example of a **sensitive period**, not a strict critical period. This is because it has a strong early window, but adults can still change later. A strict critical period would rule out any late improvement.","children":[]},{"text":"Three things make the early window strong.","children":["A **peripheral disturbance in early experience** (an early problem with the eye itself) can lead to **life-long disruption in visual function**.","The long-term loss of function is the **sequela** (a lasting result of an earlier condition) of a temporary deficit. The deficit happened during **experience-dependent organization of the brain**. The brain organized itself around abnormal input, and that organization stays.","**Treatment is most effective during a limited developmental window**. Patching works best early and stops working well by late childhood."]},{"text":"However, new evidence suggests that the **right kind of experience can reverse deficits late into adulthood**. Intensive perceptual training made adults' vision better. Video game play did too. Late learning is harder and needs special conditions, but it does happen. Since amblyopia has both a strong early window and some later change, it fits a sensitive period.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-09":[{"text":"The vision findings relate to language through two lines of work.","children":[]},{"text":"The first is **Lenneberg's** early attempts to synthesize two things. One is **developmental neurology** (the study of how the nervous system develops). The other is a **“language acquisition device”** (a proposed innate capacity for learning language). Lenneberg asked whether changes in the growing brain could open and close a window for language. He linked language milestones to stages of brain development. If there is such a window, language learning depends on the state of the brain and not only on the input.","children":[]},{"text":"The second is **studies of early deprivation**. The data come from **neglected children** and **Deaf children with limited input to sign**. These studies ask what happens when language input comes late. This is because these people first met a language they could access unusually late. Neglected children were cut off from ordinary language input. Some Deaf children have no access to sign language in early childhood. Comparing them with typical learners tests whether the age of first exposure changes the final outcome.","children":[]},{"text":"Since vision has a window when learning works best, the question is whether language has one too.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-10":[{"text":"**Lenneberg (1967)** thought language grows on a biological schedule. In **Biological Foundations of Language**, he tried to synthesize contemporary linguistics with what was known about brain development. This is because studies were starting to find a fairly robust and **stage-like pattern** of language development.","children":[]},{"text":"Children reach the stages in the same order. Data from Austrian, British and American children between 8 and 36 months agree.","children":["**Words** come first, mostly between about 10 and 18 months.","**Two-word phrases** come next, mostly between about 18 and 26 months.","**Sentences of five words or more** come last, between about 28 and 36 months."]},{"text":"Lenneberg matched language milestones with motor milestones at each age.","children":["At **8 months**, infants stand holding on and pick up small objects. **Reduplicated** babbling (repeated syllables) becomes frequent, with intonation that can signal emphasis and emotion.","At **10 months**, infants creep efficiently and pull to standing. Their vocalizations mix with sound play such as gurgling and bubble-blowing. They try to imitate sounds and start to tell words apart.","At **12 months**, infants walk with one hand held. Words such as **mama** or **dada** come out, and they understand some words and simple commands.","At **18 months**, children have more than three but fewer than fifty words. Much babbling continues, and comprehension grows quickly."]},{"text":"Since language milestones line up with motor milestones, both seem to run on one biological timetable. That is why **maturational milestones** (developmental achievements tied to physical growth) were seen as consistent with the **unfolding of a biological program**. Lenneberg proposed that brain maturation decides the pace for both.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-11":[{"text":"Lenneberg thought the critical period for language **opens after the fast brain growth of the first two years**. This is because he observed abrupt changes in **neuron size** and **packing density** (how closely neurons are crowded together) before age two. He proposed that these changes might be **permissive** for the beginning of language acquisition. Permissive means they make language acquisition possible without directly causing it. So the brain first has a burst of physical growth. Once that growth settles, the window for learning language opens.","children":[]},{"text":"The data behind this were **sparse**. This is because they came almost entirely from **post-mortem** (after death) analyses of tissue. Researchers could not follow the same children's brains as they grew. They had to piece development together from children who had died at different ages.","children":[]},{"text":"The neuron size data are an example of how sparse the data were. Neuron size was the cell body volume of neurons in cortical layers III, IV and V.","children":["Neuron size grows fast in the first two years and then levels off.","**No data were available between ages 2 and 18**.","That gap covers much of the period of language acquisition."]},{"text":"Since the measurements had this gap, Lenneberg filled it with a reasonable guess about how the brain matures. Later research would need to test that guess directly.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-12":[{"text":"Children can lose the left hemisphere and still develop language, but adults cannot. Lenneberg explained this with **cerebral dominance** (one hemisphere, usually the left, being specialized for language). This is because in young children language is not yet fixed in the left hemisphere. So the right hemisphere can take over. In adults, dominance is established, so the right hemisphere can no longer take over. Since nothing can take over in adults, removing the left hemisphere leaves permanent aphasia. That is why Lenneberg hypothesized that adult-like cerebral dominance is a key factor in **closing the critical period**.","children":[]},{"text":"The evidence came from studies of recovery from stroke. **Aphasia is surprisingly rare in children with brain lesions** (areas of damaged brain tissue). In extreme cases a **hemispherectomy** was done. This is surgery that removes or disconnects one whole cerebral hemisphere, typically to relieve seizures in people with epilepsy.","children":[]},{"text":"Lenneberg's summary of outcomes from **Basser (1962)** sorts the cases by age.","children":["For lesions before the teens with the **left** hemisphere removed, **49** had speech not affected or improved. Only 3 had permanent aphasia, and all 3 had aphasia before the operation.","For lesions before the teens with the right hemisphere removed, 38 had speech not affected or improved. Another 5 had permanent aphasia, and all 5 had aphasia before the operation.","For one lesion during puberty with left removal, improvement was slow. Some aphasia stayed until the end of life, 27 months after surgery.","For **adults** with the **left** hemisphere removed, **none** had speech unaffected or improved, and **6 had permanent aphasia**.","For adults with the right hemisphere removed, 25 had speech unaffected or improved, and none had permanent aphasia."]}],"l1-critical-13":[{"text":"Children with **perinatal strokes** (strokes between the prenatal period and 28 days after birth) do much better than adults. But they do not fully catch up. Systematic studies found that their outcomes are **clearly much better than for adults with similar injuries**. The children still have some effects of the injury.","children":[]},{"text":"The first effect is **early language deficits in both right-hemisphere and left-hemisphere patients**. This is different from adults. In adults, language problems mostly come after left-hemisphere damage. In young children, damage to either side causes early language delays. **Some deficits are worse for children with right-hemisphere damage**, which is the opposite of the adult pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"The second effect is that language outcomes are generally in the **low-normal range**. That suggests a **persistent delay** rather than a catching up. The children recover far better than adults. They often do not reach the level of children without injury.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because a young brain can reorganize to support language, but the reorganized system may not work as well as the original would have. Since recovery is strong but incomplete, early plasticity is **real but limited**.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-14":[{"text":"**Genie** is a case of what happens when first-language learning starts in adolescence. **Curtiss and colleagues (1974)** studied her to learn about the **effects of early deprivation**. Genie was born in **April 1957**. When researchers first met her, she was **13 years and 7 months old**. She was so thin that she looked six or seven.","children":[]},{"text":"Her early life was extreme isolation and abuse.","children":["**From the age of 20 months**, she was kept in a small room under more and more physical restraint.","Most of the time she was harnessed to an infant's potty chair. Otherwise she was kept in a homemade sleeping bag in an infant's crib covered with wire mesh.","Her mother was rapidly losing her sight and gave her minimal care. Genie was fed only infant food.","Her father **physically punished her if she made any sounds**.","Her father and older brother did not speak to her, but at times they barked at her like dogs.","There was no television or radio, and she had very little contact with other people."]},{"text":"When she was hospitalized for malnutrition, she **could not stand up straight or chew food**. She was **not toilet trained**. She **did not speak, cry or produce any vocal sounds**.","children":[]},{"text":"Her case cannot say that missing language alone caused her problems. This is because her lack of language came together with severe physical, social and emotional deprivation. Since all of these happened together, the language deprivation **cannot be separated** from her other hardships.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-15":[{"text":"Genie learned quite a bit in the **four years** that her language was observed.","children":["She learned to **comprehend hundreds of words**.","She learned to comprehend some aspects of **morphology** (the system of meaningful word parts, such as the plural ending “-s”). She was **particularly good with plurals**, so she could use word endings to tell one from many.","She learned to **comprehend some prepositions** (words for spatial relationships between objects)."]},{"text":"The prepositions were tested with **buttons and plastic boxes** of different colors and sizes. Genie had to move the buttons and boxes the way the spoken instruction said. A sample item was **“Put the red box behind the blue box.”** The test covered **under, over, in, on, behind, in front of, in back of** and **next to**.","children":[]},{"text":"She got **more right than wrong for every preposition**, between October 1972 and August 1973.","children":["**In** had 37 correct and 4 incorrect. **On** had 36 correct and 9 incorrect.","Over had 11 correct and 2 incorrect. Under had 17 correct and 7 incorrect.","In front of had 20 correct and 6 incorrect. In back of had 7 correct and 3 incorrect.","Next to had 7 correct and 1 incorrect. Behind had 12 correct and 5 incorrect."]},{"text":"So **some language can be learned late**. This is because Genie learned all of this after puberty, long after the window Lenneberg proposed. Her learning was uneven across different parts of language.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-16":[{"text":"Genie did not learn to speak as well as she understood, and she did not learn much grammar.","children":[]},{"text":"Her **production** (what she said) **lagged severely behind her comprehension** (what she understood). This is because of two things. **Some lag is typical of development**, since all children understand more than they can say. Genie was also **punished for making sounds** as a child, which could limit how much she spoke. So her speech revealed less than she actually knew.","children":[]},{"text":"Her **grammatical abilities developed much more slowly than her vocabulary**. A test of **conjunctions** put **five familiar objects in a row** in front of her. She was asked to point to one or more of them. The items included **“Show me the fork and pencil”**, **“Show me the crayon or the knife”** and **“Show me either the spoon or the crayon”**.","children":["For **and**, she had to point to every named object.","For **or**, she had to point to just one. This is harder because it depends on understanding a logical relationship."]},{"text":"She got and right but or wrong. The testing ran from October 1971 to December 1973.","children":["In **early responses**, she treated or sentences the same as and sentences, so she pointed to both objects.","In **later responses**, she could tell and from or, but she still did not understand **either/or**.","And sentences had **100 correct**, 3 incorrect and 0 unanalyzable responses.","Or sentences had only **7 correct**, **51 incorrect** and 6 unanalyzable responses."]},{"text":"Since she knew hundreds of words but still failed or, her case fits the idea that **grammar depends more on early exposure than vocabulary does**.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-17":[{"text":"Genie's case cannot prove a sensitive period for grammar, but it is widely interpreted as suggesting one.","children":[]},{"text":"The interpretation comes from the **disparity between her vocabulary development and her grammatical development**. She learned hundreds of words but made little progress with grammar. So the case is taken as suggesting that **grammar learning in a first language is specifically impaired after a sensitive period**. On this interpretation, words can be learned at any age, while grammar depends on early exposure.","children":[]},{"text":"The case cannot prove it. This is because there are **lots of questions** about other causes.","children":["One question is whether Genie was **learning disabled**. She may have had difficulties from birth that would have hurt her language even without deprivation.","Another question is what the **residual impacts of neglect and abuse** were on her social development. Severe trauma could affect learning in many ways besides the missing language input.","Disability, abuse and late exposure could each have affected her grammar, and one case cannot separate them."]},{"text":"There is also a time limit on the evidence. **After four years, funding to study her was pulled**. So nobody can know what she might have learned after a longer time.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the other causes cannot be ruled out, her case stays suggestive. Her pattern still became an influential piece of evidence for a sensitive period in first-language learning.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-18":[{"text":"Deaf children who learn **American Sign Language (ASL)** late have much more difficulty if ASL is their first language. **Mayberry, Lock and Kazmi (2002)** compared two groups who both learned ASL late.","children":["The first group were children aged **9 to 15** who were learning a language for the **first time**. They had **difficulty** learning ASL.","The comparison group were children who **went deaf after first learning a spoken language**. They did **much better** at learning ASL.","**These differences persist into adulthood**."]},{"text":"This is because the second group already had a language early and the first group did not. Both groups learned ASL late, so lateness alone cannot explain the difference.","children":[]},{"text":"These children are a cleaner test than Genie. Many deaf children **do not get sign language input** but have an **otherwise typical upbringing**. They grow up in ordinary, caring homes. So the only unusual thing is the lack of accessible language early in life.","children":[]},{"text":"Two tasks measured the adults.","children":["One task was **recall of complex ASL sentences**. Deaf adults with **no early language** scored about **36%**. Deaf adults with an **early spoken language** scored about **78%**.","The other task was judging whether **complex English sentences were grammatically correct**, where chance is 50%. Deaf adults with no early language scored about **52%**, which is close to chance. Deaf adults who had ASL in infancy scored about **80%**. Hearing adults with an early spoken language other than English also scored about 80%."]},{"text":"Since early ASL helped later English as much as an early spoken language did, what matters is **having any language early**. Early accessible language, signed or spoken, is the foundation for learning languages later.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-19":[{"text":"Age clearly matters for learning a first language (**L1**), but the evidence cannot firmly say whether there is a **“critical period”** or a **“sensitive period”**.","children":[]},{"text":"Age matters because **recovery from stroke**, and certainly from **hemispherectomy**, looks **very different in childhood and adulthood**. Children can develop language after damage that would leave an adult with permanent aphasia. So age strongly affects how well the brain can reorganize for language.","children":[]},{"text":"**Deprivation of input does not appear to extend the critical period**. One might expect the window to stay open until language input arrives. It does not stay open. Genie and the deaf adults who learned sign late have lasting difficulty, especially with grammar. Since they still had difficulty, the window seems to follow a maturational schedule, whether or not language has been experienced.","children":[]},{"text":"It is **difficult to reach firm conclusions**. This is because all of these data come from **highly unusual cases with lots of other things going on**.","children":["Stroke, brain surgery, abuse and neglect each bring complications besides the timing of language input.","There is a **lack of animal models**, because animals do not learn human language.","**Gene knockout experiments** (turning off specific genes to test what they do) cannot be done with people."]}],"l1-critical-20":[],"l1-critical-21":[],"l2-learning-01":[],"l2-learning-02":[{"text":"Babies are overrated as language learners, so the idea of **sensitive periods** for a second language (**L2**) should not be taken too far. This is because toddlers actually learn words slowly. By **18 months**, a typical child uses at least **20 words**. An adult who knew only about 20 words after 18 months of classes would be a very poor learner.","children":[]},{"text":"A typical 18-month-old also does several other things.","children":["The child understands \"in and out\" and \"off and on\" and points to body parts when asked.","The child answers \"Where's teddy?\" and \"What's that?\" with words or gestures and pretends to give teddy a drink.","The child makes at least four consonant sounds, such as b, n, d, g, w and h. The child enjoys being read to and points to pictures with one finger."]},{"text":"**Adults learn faster at the start** than children do. This is because adults have two advantages. The first is **transfer from L1** (using what they already know from their first language). The second is **better general cognitive skills**, such as memory, attention and reasoning.","children":[]},{"text":"Adults do worse on **ultimate attainment** (the final level of skill a learner ends up with). This is because adults rarely end up sounding native-like. That may also partly come from transfer. Since early speed and final level are different things, children and adults can each come out ahead on one of them.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-03":[{"text":"Adult learners already have many of the ideas that toddlers are still figuring out. This is because some toddler **language milestones** are really **general conceptual abilities**. Understanding \"in and out\" and \"off and on\" depends on understanding **spatial relations between objects** (where things are relative to each other). **Pretend play**, like giving teddy a drink, depends on **event representations** (mental models of who does what in an event).","children":[]},{"text":"So a later learner has a smaller job. This is because an adult learning Spanish already has the ideas behind \"in\" and \"on\". The adult only has to learn how the **target language** (the language being learned) expresses those ideas. A toddler has to build the ideas and the words at the same time.","children":[]},{"text":"Later learners also already know another language, and that changes how they learn the new one. This is because the first language gives ready-made ways to say things. Some of those ways fit the new language, so they help. Other ways clash with it, so they lead to mistakes.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-04":[{"text":"**Transfer** is using your first language (**L1**) processes and representations for a second language (**L2**). This is because a learner already has a full working language system. So the learner carries old habits for hearing sounds, building sentences and finding words into the L2.","children":[]},{"text":"Transfer can help or hurt.","children":["**Positive transfer** happens when an L1 pattern matches the L2. Then the learner gets a head start.","**Negative transfer** happens when an L1 pattern clashes with the L2. Then the learner makes mistakes where the two languages differ."]},{"text":"A cartoon gives an example of negative transfer in grammar. A man in a pub asks \"Do you not be happy with me as the translator of the books of you?\" This is because he is translating word by word. The words are English, but the word order and phrasing come from another language. Since every first-language habit comes along, it helps where languages match and hurts where they differ.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-05":[{"text":"English speakers have trouble learning a Hindi sound difference because English lumps the two sounds together. This is because of **categorical perception**, which leads to **acquired similarity** (sounds in one category come to sound more alike with experience).","children":[]},{"text":"**Minimal pairs in Hindi** (two words that differ in only one sound) make this clear.","children":["Two words both sound like \"tap\" to an English speaker. One means hot, and the other means a kind of beat.","Two words both sound like \"dab\". One means pressure, and the other means a kind of fruit.","Two words both sound like \"ari\". One means saw (the tool), and the other means crooked.","In each pair, one word has a **dental** consonant (tongue against the teeth). The other has a **retroflex** consonant (tongue curled back toward the roof of the mouth)."]},{"text":"Native English speakers use their **alveolar** category for both sounds. An alveolar consonant, like English /t/ or /d/, is made with the tongue on the ridge behind the teeth. This is because English only has the alveolar category. So both Hindi sounds fall into it and seem the same.","children":[]},{"text":"So here **transfer makes learning harder**. The English category works fine for English. But it hides a difference that Hindi uses to tell words apart. Since the first language taught learners to treat these sounds as one, they must now learn to pull them apart.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-06":[{"text":"English speakers learning Chinese have a hard time with **classifiers**. This is because English never makes them pay attention to what kind of thing a noun names. A **classifier** is a word that goes between a number or demonstrative and a noun. Which classifier you use depends on the kind of thing the noun names. Classifiers are a somewhat unusual feature across languages.","children":[]},{"text":"The order is demonstrative pronoun, then classifier, then noun. In **yí gè rén** (\"one person\"), gè is the classifier. Each classifier goes with one kind of noun.","children":["**Gè** is the general classifier. **Bǎ** is for objects with a handle. **Běn** is for books and similar objects.","**Kuài** is for objects divided into pieces. **Liàng** is for wheeled vehicles. **Tái** is for devices and gadgets.","**Xiē** is for an indeterminate amount, or for the plural of pronouns and demonstratives.","**Zhāng** is for flat objects. **Zhī** is for long, thin objects."]},{"text":"In English, saying \"a chair\", \"that dog\", \"the car\" or \"one book\" needs no thought about noun meaning. In Chinese, different nouns need different classifiers. So English speakers have learned to ignore the cues that classifiers depend on. Since they learned to ignore shape and function, they now have to start noticing them again.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-07":[{"text":"**Grammatical gender** causes transfer problems for English speakers. This is because English nouns have no gender, so English speakers have no habit of tracking it. Grammatical gender sorts nouns into classes such as masculine and feminine. It is different from social or cultural gender.","children":[]},{"text":"There is nothing \"male\" about a book or \"female\" about liberty. Still, French **le livre** (the book) is masculine, and **la liberté** (liberty) is feminine.","children":["Other masculine nouns are le cheval (the horse), le chien (the dog) and le bruit (the noise).","Other feminine nouns are la colombe (the dove), la chemise (the shirt) and la maison (the house)."]},{"text":"Gender matters because of **agreement** (related words have to match the noun). Pronouns, adjectives and some verb forms all have to match the noun's gender.","children":[]},{"text":"Gender has to be learned word by word. This is because French has **synonyms with different genders**.","children":["**Auto** and **voiture** both mean car, and one is masculine while the other is feminine.","**Rivière** (feminine) and **fleuve** (masculine) both mean river.","**Vélo** (masculine) and **bicyclette** (feminine) both mean bicycle."]},{"text":"Word endings give hints.","children":["Masculine nouns often end in -age (le fromage), -r (le professeur), -t (le chat) or -isme (le capitalisme).","Feminine nouns often end in -ie (la boulangerie), -ion (la nation), -ité or -tié (la fraternité) or -ance (la balance).","Feminine nouns also often end in -nne, -mme or -lle (la fille, l’indienne)."]}],"l2-learning-08":[{"text":"Transfer is not all bad, because similar words make vocabulary easier to learn. This is because the learner already knows a word that looks and means about the same.","children":["French **auto** looks like English \"auto\" and means a car.","French **rivière** looks like \"river\", and French **bicyclette** looks like \"bicycle\".","Japanese **コロッケ (korokke)** means croquette, and **モノレール (monoreru)** means monorail."]},{"text":"Words can be similar for two reasons.","children":["One reason is **shared etymology** (a shared word history). Words with a shared origin are called **cognates**, like rivière and river.","The other reason is more recent **borrowing**. A **loan word** is a word taken from another language. Japanese korokke and monoreru came from European languages, with their sounds changed to fit Japanese."]},{"text":"Both kinds give **positive transfer** for vocabulary. Someone who knows river can link it to rivière right away. Since the new word hooks onto a familiar one, it is easier to store and remember.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-09":[{"text":"Not all similar-sounding words are cognates. This is because some words sound alike but mean different things. Those are called **false friends** (words that sound like a word in another language but mean something else).","children":[]},{"text":"\"Arnold está embarazado\" does not mean Arnold is embarrassed. Spanish **embarazado** means **pregnant**. An English speaker hears embarrassed in it. So the speaker gets the meaning badly wrong, and the result is embarrassing.","children":[]},{"text":"The mistake happens because the pull to link L2 words to similar L1 words is very strong. The link forms before the learner checks the meaning.","children":["When the meanings match, as with cognates, the link speeds up learning.","When the meanings differ, the same link causes mistakes."]},{"text":"Since the same link drives both, a helpful cognate and a false friend differ only in whether the meanings really match.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-10":[{"text":"**De Groot and Keijzer (2000)** tested in the lab whether cognates help people learn new second-language words. They used **paired-associate learning** (studying pairs, then recalling one when given the other) with made-up words.","children":[]},{"text":"Participants were Dutch speakers. So Dutch played the first language, and the made-up words played a new second language. Participants were simply shown each Dutch word with its made-up partner and asked to learn the pairs. Then they were tested **a week later** on **retention** (how well they still remembered).","children":[]},{"text":"The made-up words differed in two ways.","children":["Some were similar to their Dutch partner (cognate) and some were not (non-cognate).","Some were **concrete** (something you can see or touch) and some were **abstract** (an idea)."]},{"text":"Mixing the two gave four kinds of pairs.","children":["Gitaar and gettar (\"guitar\") is concrete and cognate.","Tijger and duunze (\"tiger\") is concrete and non-cognate.","Domein and domaan (\"domain\") is abstract and cognate.","Herstel and groop (\"repair\") is abstract and non-cognate."]},{"text":"Since every pair was one of each kind, the effect of each factor could be measured on its own.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-11":[{"text":"Yes, people use cognates when learning a second language. In De Groot and Keijzer (2000), **cognates were learned faster and remembered better**. We know this because recall started higher for cognates and dropped less after a week. Recall was tested in three training sessions, then in session 4 after a week with no training.","children":[]},{"text":"Recall rose from session 1 to session 3 for every kind of word.","children":["Concrete cognates went from 69.7% to 93.4%.","Concrete non-cognates went from 43.7% to 92.0%.","Abstract cognates went from 42.7% to 86.0%.","Abstract non-cognates went from 19.0% to 72.7%."]},{"text":"Recall dropped after the week off, most of all for abstract non-cognates.","children":["Concrete cognates dropped to 78.4%, and concrete non-cognates dropped to 69.0%.","Abstract cognates dropped to 68.4%, and abstract non-cognates fell hard to **36.0%**.","Responses were also faster for cognates in every session."]},{"text":"So **cognate status helps from the very first stage** of word learning. The cognate advantage was biggest in session 1 and showed up again after the week.","children":[]},{"text":"**Concreteness** also mattered a lot, and the two factors add up. Abstract non-cognates had neither kind of help, so they were hardest. One explanation for concreteness effects is **context availability** (concrete words are easier to link to a helpful context).","children":[]}],"l2-learning-12":[{"text":"Yes, it is a good experiment, because it tested people in both directions. This is because a receptive test alone would have been too easy. **Receptive** testing gives the made-up word and asks for the Dutch word. A cognate made-up word gives the answer away.","children":[]},{"text":"The meanings of gitaar, tijger and domein could be guessed without any Dutch. This is because they look like English guitar, tiger and domain. The concrete cognates are very close to their Dutch partners.","children":["Broer and breur, brood and brodde, gitaar and gettar, jongen and jange, lepel and lippel.","Lichaam and lechem, naald and nadel, paraplu and parredu, rivier and riffer, schaar and skare.","Stier and stire, trein and trinen, varken and vark, viool and vialo, vrouw and vrauk.","Each made-up word is a slightly changed Dutch word."]},{"text":"The study also tested **expressive** vocabulary, which the authors call **productive** testing. Productive testing gives the Dutch word and asks for the made-up word. This direction matters because the learner then has to recall the exact new form, like gettar. Since there is no shortcut, a cognate advantage here means real learning.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-13":[{"text":"The participants really learned the new words, and cognates and concrete words were forgotten less. This is because they dropped less over the week in **both** test directions. Recall was measured right after learning (session 3) and a week later (session 4).","children":[]},{"text":"In **productive** testing, learners had to produce the made-up word.","children":["Concrete words went from 92.7% to 73.7%, a drop of 19.0 points. Abstract words went from 79.3% to 52.2%, a drop of 27.1.","Cognates went from 89.7% to 73.3%, a drop of 16.4. Non-cognates went from 82.3% to 52.5%, a drop of 29.8."]},{"text":"In **receptive** testing, learners had to give the Dutch word.","children":["Concrete words went from 97.7% to 88.9%, a drop of 8.8. Abstract words went from 91.5% to 64.9%, a drop of 26.6.","Cognates went from 98.5% to 90.7%, a drop of 7.8. Non-cognates went from 90.7% to 63.0%, a drop of 27.7."]},{"text":"Receptive scores were higher overall. This is because recognizing a familiar word is easier than producing a new one.","children":[]},{"text":"The cognate advantage held up in productive testing too. Since the learner had to recall the new form itself, similarity to a known word must have helped build a lasting memory.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-14":[{"text":"The question is whether similar first-language words always become active when someone processes a second language. There have been many, many studies of this. This is because a bilingual hearing an L2 word might also activate L1 words that sound like it. That could happen on its own, even when the person is trying to use only the L2. If it does, the two languages are never fully separate.","children":[]},{"text":"**Marian and Spivey (2003)** gave one clear example with a **visual world experiment**. In this kind of experiment, people hear spoken instructions while looking at objects. Their eye movements are recorded.","children":[]},{"text":"Eye movements can answer the question. This is because a look toward an object whose L1 name sounds like the spoken L2 word means the L1 word was active. The person never has to report it. The person still picks the right object in the end.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-15":[{"text":"Eye movements show which words a person is considering, even when the final choice is right. The same method was used to study categorical perception. **McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)** used it to study how within-category **VOT** (voice onset time) affects **lexical access** (finding the word in memory).","children":[]},{"text":"Participants saw a lamp, a ship, a bear and a pear. They heard a word from a continuum between \"bear\" and \"pear\". Bear and pear differ only in the first consonant. The lamp and ship were unrelated fillers.","children":[]},{"text":"A participant could click the bear after glancing at the pear. This is because \"pear\" was active as a competitor. Those glances measure **competition**.","children":[]},{"text":"The amount of competition between BEAR and PEAR depended on **distance from the category boundary**.","children":["Sounds near the /b/ and /p/ boundary got more looks to the competitor.","Sounds far from the boundary got fewer."]},{"text":"Since eye tracking measures how active another word is, it can also test whether a word in one language competes with one in another.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-16":[{"text":"**Allopenna and colleagues (1994)** found the **cohort effect**. Words that start like the target compete early in recognition. A **cohort** is the set of words that share the same beginning sounds.","children":[]},{"text":"Participants saw a grid of pictures and shapes and followed spoken instructions.","children":["The **beaker** was the target.","The **beetle** starts like beaker.","The **speaker** rhymes with beaker.","The **carriage** is unrelated."]},{"text":"Looks were tracked for the first 1,000 milliseconds after the word began.","children":["Looks to the beaker and the beetle went up together early. This is because both fit the first sounds.","Looks to the beetle peaked near 0.2 around 500 milliseconds. They dropped once the sounds that tell the words apart came in.","Looks to the beaker kept going up toward 1 by about 1,000 milliseconds.","Looks to the speaker rose later and stayed small, near 0.1. This is because its overlap is at the end.","Looks to the carriage stayed near zero."]},{"text":"So words compete as soon as they match the incoming sounds. They drop out once the sounds stop matching. Since eyes follow this, eye movements track competition moment by moment.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-17":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey** checked whether a word in the other language competes just because it starts with the same sounds. They called these **cross-language competitors** (objects whose name in the other language starts like the target).","children":[]},{"text":"Their hypothesis was that if L1 words are always active, Russian speakers hearing English \"speaker\" should look at matches. This is because English **speaker** starts like Russian **спички (spichki)**, which means matches.","children":[]},{"text":"Each English target had an English and a Russian competitor.","children":["Speaker had spear and spichki (matches). Boot had book and buben (tambourine). Shark had shovel and sharik (balloon).","Chair had chess set and cherepaha (turtle). Marker had marbles and marka (stamp). Barbed wire had bark and barhat (velvet).","Plug had plum and platye (dress). Gun had gum and gaika (nut). Lock had lobster and lak dlea nogtei (nail polish)."]},{"text":"For Russian targets, it was flipped.","children":["Spichki (matches) had English spear and Russian spitsi (knitting needles).","Marka (stamp) had English marker and Russian marlya (cheese cloth).","Platye (dress) had English plum and Russian plashch (raincoat)."]},{"text":"The target and the competitor share only **sound**. Speakers and matches have nothing to do with each other in meaning. Since the overlap only exists if you know Russian, looks to the matches mean spichki was active.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-18":[{"text":"Yes, the first language stays active while bilinguals listen in their second language. We know this from Russian and English bilinguals who followed instructions in **English**. They looked at objects with similar Russian names far more than at unrelated objects.","children":[]},{"text":"The **between-language competitor** had a similar Russian name. The **within-language competitor** had a similar English name. Looks were counted as the percent of trials with a look at each object.","children":["With only the Russian-name competitor, looks were **18%**.","With only the English-name competitor, looks were also **18%**.","With both, the Russian-name one got 13% and the English-name one got 19%.","With no competitor, an unrelated control object got only **7%**."]},{"text":"With one competitor, both kinds drew looks **to the same extent**. So the Russian name pulled the eyes as strongly as a similar English word.","children":[]},{"text":"With both, the English-name competitor got more looks. The Russian-name competitor still got about **twice as many as the control**. Since bilinguals still picked the right object, the L1 words competed along the way without causing mistakes.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-19":[{"text":"No, English words did not reliably become active when the same bilinguals listened in **Russian**. We know this because looks to objects with similar English names were barely above the unrelated control.","children":[]},{"text":"Looks were counted as the percent of trials with a look at each object.","children":["With only the English-name competitor, looks were **8%**.","With only the Russian-name competitor, looks were 14%.","With both, the English-name one got 8% and the Russian-name one got 13%.","With no competitor, the control object got **5%**."]},{"text":"The difference between 8% and 5% is **not statistically significant** (it is small enough to be chance). The Russian-name competitor still drew extra looks, so the cohort effect showed up in Russian.","children":[]},{"text":"So the result goes one way only, which is called **asymmetric**.","children":["Russian words became active during English tasks.","English words did not reliably become active during Russian tasks."]},{"text":"Since the first language interferes with the second but not the reverse, the first language seems more firmly set up and easier to activate.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-20":[{"text":"In the **Revised Hierarchical Model** of **Kroll and Stewart**, activation flows more easily from an L2 word to an L1 word than the other way. This is because learners usually learn each L2 word by tying it to its L1 translation.","children":[]},{"text":"The model works by **spreading activation** (turning on one representation partly turns on the ones linked to it). It has three parts.","children":["One part is the L1 vocabulary.","Another part is the L2 vocabulary.","The third part is a store of **concepts** (meanings)."]},{"text":"**Lexical links** connect L1 and L2 words directly. **Conceptual links** connect each vocabulary to the concepts.","children":[]},{"text":"The L1 vocabulary is assumed to be **larger**. It is also **more strongly linked to concepts**. L2 words are linked to concepts more weakly.","children":[]},{"text":"The L2 vocabulary has strong forward links to the L1 vocabulary. The link from L2 to L1 is strong, and the link from L1 to L2 is weak. Since learning practices the L2 to L1 direction, that path gets strong. The direct path from L2 words to concepts stays weaker, at least early on.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-21":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model of Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994)** explains why the Russian competitor drew looks in the English task. This is because L1 words are strongly linked to their concepts.","children":[]},{"text":"In the L2 task with the target \"speaker\", the model works in four steps.","children":["The concept for \"matches\" partly activates the Russian word for matches, spichki.","The sounds of speaker also partly activate spichki, because the two words start the same way.","This activity feeds back and boosts the concept for \"matches\".","The boosted concept for \"matches\" makes the eyes move toward the L1 competitor, the matches."]},{"text":"Speaker and spichki share only **sound overlap**. Speakers and matches share no meaning. So the looks come from sound alone.","children":[]},{"text":"The effect does not happen when the task is done in L1. This is because the conceptual links for L2 words might be too weak. An English competitor would need a strong link from its English word to its concept. L2 words only have weak links. Since those links are weak, English words cannot reliably pull the eyes during a Russian task.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-22":[{"text":"The model predicts that **balanced bilinguals** (people about equally good at both languages) are not \"two monolinguals in one\". This prediction is counter-intuitive. You might expect each language to run on its own.","children":[]},{"text":"The two languages compete all the time instead. This is because a balanced bilingual has similarly strong **conceptual links** for both languages. Each concept strongly activates its word in both languages. So both vocabularies light up whenever either is used.","children":[]},{"text":"Two processes are engaged all the time.","children":["**Competitive dynamics** means the words of the two languages keep competing to be picked.","**Inhibition** means holding back the word from the language not in use."]},{"text":"This prediction led to a lot of work on bilinguals' advantages in non-linguistic **cognitive control** tasks. Cognitive control is managing attention and holding back responses that do not fit. Since bilinguals constantly practice picking one language and holding back the other, they might get better at controlling attention in general.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-23":[{"text":"The question is what happens when someone **stops using their first language** after learning a second. This is because the usual sequence keeps both languages in use.","children":["The person begins learning L1.","Later the person learns L2.","The person keeps learning and using L1 the whole time."]},{"text":"In the other sequence, the person begins learning L1 and later learns L2. But the person stops using L1. So L1 is no longer heard or spoken, and L2 takes over.","children":[]},{"text":"This second sequence pulls apart two things that usually go together. One is **learning a language early**. The other is **continuing to use it**.","children":["If early learning leaves a permanent foundation, traces of L1 should still be there without use.","If ongoing use is needed, L1 might fade or disappear."]}],"l2-learning-24":[{"text":"**Pallier, Dehaene, Poline, LeBihan, Argenti, Dupoux and Mehler** asked whether a second language can replace the first. They used brain imaging of language plasticity in adopted adults.","children":[]},{"text":"They tested adults aged **20 to 32**. These adults were adopted into French homes from Korean orphanages between ages **3 and 8**. They learned Korean as young children and stopped using it completely after adoption. This is because they then heard only French at home. So they are a real case of L1 use stopping.","children":[]},{"text":"The researchers looked for **residual knowledge** of Korean (any leftover trace of it). Monolingual French speakers who never knew Korean were the comparison group. If the adoptees did better than them on Korean tasks, some Korean must have survived. Since any surviving Korean would come only from early childhood, it would show what early learning leaves behind.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-25":[{"text":"Korean has a **three-way stop contrast**, so it makes a good test of whether adoptees kept any Korean. A **stop** is a consonant made by fully blocking the air and then letting it go, like /p/, /t/ or /k/.","children":[]},{"text":"English and French have two kinds of stop at each place in the mouth, like /b/ and /p/. Korean has three kinds at each place.","children":["An **aspirated** stop is let out with a strong puff of air.","A **plain** stop has a lighter release.","A **tense** stop is made with a stiffened, tight throat."]},{"text":"The UCLA Phonetics Archive has recordings of three series.","children":["The **bilabial** series (made with the lips) is pʰul (\"grass\"), pul (\"fire\") and p’ul (\"horn\").","The **dental** series (tongue at the teeth) is tʰal (\"mask\"), tal (\"moon\") and t’al (\"daughter\").","The **velar** series (back of the tongue) is kʰo (\"nose\"), kok (\"music\") and k’ok (\"exactly\")."]},{"text":"In each series, the three words differ only in the kind of stop. French speakers tend to hear the three sounds as two. This is because French has no such contrast. Korean speakers hear three. Since French speakers struggle, adoptees who kept Korean sound knowledge should do better than them.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-26":[{"text":"No, the Korean adoptees heard the Korean stop differences no better than French speakers. **Ventureyra and colleagues** found this. Listeners heard pairs of sounds and said whether they were different.","children":[]},{"text":"Each set of numbers below gives how often Koreans, adoptees and French speakers said \"different\", in that order.","children":["For plain and tense p and k, it was about 96%, 8% and 3%.","For plain and aspirated p and k, it was about 90%, 19% and 26%.","For tense and aspirated p and k, it was about 95%, 23% and 20%.","For plain and tense s, it was about 79%, 26% and 23%."]},{"text":"The adoptees could do the task. We know this because two control conditions came out fine for everyone.","children":["On a **vowel** difference, all three groups said \"different\" about 97 to 99% of the time.","On **identical** pairs, all groups said \"different\" only about 3 to 13% of the time."]},{"text":"So the problem was only with the Korean stops. Since the adoptees scored like people who never heard Korean, early Korean left no measurable advantage for these sounds.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-27":[{"text":"No, the adoptees showed no trace of Korean on any of three unusual tasks. In a more extensive study, **Pallier and colleagues** found adoptees were no better than monolingual French speakers on all three. This is because the adoptees scored like French speakers even on tasks built to catch weak knowledge. Pallier and colleagues were looking for any remaining trace at all, so they used tasks that are not standard psycholinguistic tasks.","children":[]},{"text":"The first task was telling Korean apart from mostly unrelated languages. People rated from 1 to 7 how Korean each sentence sounded.","children":["Both groups rated Korean around 4 and Japanese around 4 to 5.","Polish got about 2, and Swedish and Wolof got about 2.5 to 3.","Adoptees were no better at picking out Korean."]},{"text":"The second task was picking the correct Korean translation of French words. Adoptees got about 55% and French speakers about 50%. Both are close to chance.","children":[]},{"text":"The third task was deciding whether a short piece came from a recorded sentence they just heard.","children":["Both groups got about 95% on French.","Both got about 80% on Korean, Japanese and Polish.","Adoptees had no edge on Korean."]},{"text":"Since every measure matched French speakers, the adoptees looked like people who never learned Korean.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-28":[{"text":"The adoptees' brains treated Korean like a foreign language, just like French speakers' brains did. **Pallier and colleagues** found this with brain imaging.","children":[]},{"text":"Listening to French turned on the **classical language network** in both groups.","children":["This includes **superior temporal** regions (the upper temporal lobe, which handles speech sounds).","It also includes **inferior frontal** regions (the lower frontal lobe, including Broca's area)."]},{"text":"The researchers subtracted the activity for another language from the activity for French.","children":["They did French minus Polish, French minus Japanese and French minus Korean.","They did each one for the French group and for the Korean group (the adoptees).","French always turned on the language network more than the foreign language."]},{"text":"French minus Korean could have come out different for the adoptees. This is because adoptees who still processed Korean would show more language activity for Korean. Then the French minus Korean difference would be smaller for them. But it was not different. Since the brain results match the behavior results, no Korean processing seems to be left.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-29":[{"text":"An unused first language could have its brain connections taken over by the second language. This is the idea of **competitive dynamics**. Imagine L1 as a set of connections within and between brain regions. Using a language keeps those connections in place.","children":[]},{"text":"The same thing happens in vision with **monocular deprivation** (one eye gets no input) in mice.","children":["After 2 to 3 days, the deprived eye's connections show **increased spine motility** (they become unstable).","After 3 to 4 days, there is **spine loss and Wallerian degeneration** (cut-off nerve fibers break down).","After more than a week, the other eye shows **axon sprouting and new spine birth**. It takes over the space."]},{"text":"So the eye that keeps getting input wins the connections the other eye loses.","children":[]},{"text":"Language could work the same way. If L1 connections are never used again, L2 could take them over. This is because L2 is used all the time, so its connections get stronger and spread. L1 is not used, so its connections weaken. Since both compete for the same brain resources, L2 could end up replacing L1.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-30":[{"text":"Apparently yes, a second language can replace the first. This is because the adoptees showed no sign of their childhood Korean in behavior or in the brain. As far as these tests could tell, French had fully replaced Korean.","children":[]},{"text":"The next question is whether traces of L1 are left that can be **\"salvaged\"**. Salvaging a trace means getting back or using some leftover effect of the old language. A trace might not show up on a test of what you can do now. It could still make relearning easier.","children":[]},{"text":"These are two different kinds of test.","children":["A recognition test asks what the person can do with the language now.","A relearning test asks whether new learning gets help from something left over."]},{"text":"Since a hidden trace might only help new learning, **relearning** is the measure that could reveal it.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-31":[{"text":"Yes, some traces can be salvaged. Students who overheard Spanish as kids later pronounced it much more like native speakers. **Terry Au and colleagues** at UCLA (Au, Knightly, Jun and Oh) found this.","children":[]},{"text":"They studied undergraduates who **overheard Spanish** often as children but never learned to speak it. All started Spanish classes around age 14. All were tested after a second-year college Spanish course. They were compared with native speakers and typical late learners.","children":[]},{"text":"Pronunciation was much better for overhearers. Each set of numbers below is in the order natives, overhearers, then late learners.","children":["**Voice onset time** (the delay before voicing starts) for word-initial voiceless stops was 19.3, 19.6 and 36.2 milliseconds. Word-medial values were 22.4, 23.4 and 31.2.","**Lenition** (softening voiced stops between vowels) was 43%, 24% and 1% word-initially, and 72%, 63% and 23% word-medially.","Accent ratings out of 5 were 4.3, 3.5 and 2.2 for voiceless stops, and 4.4, 3.3 and 2.7 for voiced.","Childhood slang production was 79%, 17% and 3%, and comprehension was 88%, 22% and 3%."]},{"text":"Grammar was not better.","children":["Gender agreement was 93%, 70% and 68%, and number agreement was 92%, 88% and 85%.","Grammaticality judgment accuracy was 94%, 64% and 63%. Reaction times were 1,042, 2,475 and 2,209 milliseconds."]},{"text":"Since sounds improved but grammar did not, early listening left traces in the sound system that later classes unmasked.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-32":[{"text":"Losing all contact with a first language can make it look fully replaced, but some knowledge may still be there. This is because the two groups gave different results.","children":["The Korean adoptees had no Korean exposure at all. L2 seemed to completely replace L1, since they showed nothing on any test of current ability.","The Spanish overhearers took Spanish classes later. Their pronunciation showed leftover knowledge that got \"unmasked\" by the new learning."]},{"text":"So the replacement is **not necessarily complete**. There may be some **residual knowledge** (a leftover trace) that comes back with later learning.","children":[]},{"text":"The two groups also differ in ways that could matter.","children":["The adoptees were tested on what they knew, with no new Korean classes.","The overhearers were tested at the end of a second-year college Spanish course. That gave hidden knowledge a chance to show up."]}],"l2-learning-33":[{"text":"The adoptees and the overhearers differ in many ways, so it is hard to say why only the overhearers kept traces. L1 can seem fully replaced, but residual knowledge may be unmasked later. This is because several other differences could explain the results.","children":["Korean is rarely heard in France, but Spanish is common in Los Angeles. So overhearers may have kept hearing Spanish all their lives.","A **heritage language** (spoken by family members) is different from a language nobody you know speaks. Overhearers often heard Spanish from relatives, while adoptees lost all contact with Korean speakers.","**Cognates** are common between Spanish and English but rare between Korean and French. Shared words could help overhearers relearn.","A **shared writing system** also helps. Spanish and English use the same alphabet, while Korean uses a different script from French."]},{"text":"This is a general problem in research on second language acquisition and bilingualism. Languages and learning situations differ in many, many ways. Those ways are hard, maybe impossible, to control in experiments. This is because researchers cannot assign people to grow up with certain languages. Since real groups never differ in just one way, these factors are hard to pull apart.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-01":[],"l2-sensitive-02":[{"text":"Lenneberg (1967) said the **sensitive period** closes because language settles into one side of the brain. This is because his theory ties the sensitive period to the development of **cerebral dominance** (one hemisphere, usually the left, becoming specialized for language). He also called this developmental changes in **language laterality** (how much language depends on one side of the brain instead of both). Once language is fixed in the left hemisphere, the window for learning language closes.","children":[]},{"text":"Two kinds of evidence led him to the theory.","children":["The first was that brain development roughly lined up with language milestones. Cortical neurons grow quickly for about two years and then level off. There were no measurements between ages 2 and 18. Lenneberg linked the end of fast brain growth to the start of language learning.","The second was a review of **resilience after brain injury or surgery**. Children who lost the left hemisphere often went on to develop language. Adults did not get language back."]},{"text":"The theory is about the brain's **maturational state** (its stage of physical development). A young brain is less specialized, so it can move language to other tissue. A mature brain is specialized, so it has fewer ways to do that. Since a mature brain has fewer options, language learning gets harder.","children":[]},{"text":"**Second-language (L2) learning** is used as a **model system** for research on the sensitive period. A model system is a common, easily studied situation used to answer questions about a process that is hard to study directly.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-03":[{"text":"L2 learning gives a bigger comparison than first-language cases do. This is because most of what is known about **first-language (L1) sensitive periods** comes from **idiosyncratic** (particular to one individual) cases or relatively rare phenomena. The cases include children recovering from surgery that removed a hemisphere. They also include Genie, who was cut off from language until age 13. Each case comes with other unusual circumstances, such as injury, illness or abuse. So it is hard to figure out which factor caused the outcome.","children":[]},{"text":"In contrast, many people learn **second languages** at different times in their lives, often with somewhat **limited success**. L2 learners are common, so researchers can study large groups. They start at every age from early childhood to late adulthood. Their final success can be measured. So researchers can compare early starters with late starters.","children":[]},{"text":"Those limits on L2 success are used to figure out the **timing** of the sensitive or critical period. This is because later starters might reliably end up at lower levels. If they do, the age where the drop appears could mark when the sensitive period closes.","children":[]},{"text":"There is one complication, which is the **first language**. Every L2 learner already knows a language. That knowledge shapes how the new language is learned. Since L2 learners are common and start at every age, they still give a large comparison on timing.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-04":[{"text":"People who arrived in the United States younger ended up with better English grammar. **Johnson and Newport (1989)** found this when they tested for **critical period effects** in second-language learning. Their paper is about the influence of **maturational state** on learning English as a second language.","children":[]},{"text":"They ran the study because of **Lenneberg (1967)**. He hypothesized that language could be acquired only within a **critical period** (from early infancy until puberty). In its basic form, that idea is only about first-language acquisition. If it also applies to a second language, young children should learn a second language better than adults. They should also reach higher final proficiency.","children":[]},{"text":"To test this, they compared the English proficiency of **46 native Korean or Chinese speakers**. These people had arrived in the United States between the ages of 3 and 39. They had lived there between 3 and 26 years when they were tested. They did a **grammaticality judgment task** on a wide variety of structures of English grammar.","children":[]},{"text":"Earlier arrivals had a **clear and strong advantage** over later arrivals. Up to puberty, test performance was **linearly related** to age of arrival, so each extra year of age at arrival meant a lower score. After puberty, performance was **low but highly variable** and was unrelated to age of arrival.","children":[]},{"text":"The age effect was not an accident. This is because it did not come from differences in amount of experience with English, motivation, self-consciousness or American identification. It also appeared on every grammatical structure tested, even though later learners mastered some structures far better than others. Since the age effect held up, the authors concluded that a critical period for language acquisition **extends its effects to second-language acquisition**.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-05":[{"text":"Each example set tests one rule of English grammar. This is because every item on the **grammaticality judgment test (GJT)** had a grammatical version and an ungrammatical version. **Johnson and Newport (1989)** devised the test. Participants listened to recorded sentences and said whether each sentence was grammatical or not. So each answer was a **grammaticality judgment** (a decision about whether a sentence follows the rules of the language). The test was **untimed**, so they could take as long as they needed.","children":[]},{"text":"Two sets test the form of a noun or a verb.","children":["**(1a) “The farmer bought two pigs at the market”** and **(1b) “The farmer bought two pig at the market”** test **plural marking** on nouns.","**(2a) “The little boy is speaking to a policeman”** and **(2b) “The little boy is speak to a policeman”** test the **progressive -ing** form of the verb."]},{"text":"Three sets test articles and other **determiners**.","children":["**(6a) “Tom is reading a book in the bathtub”** and **(6b) “Tom is reading book in the bathtub”** test whether a needed article is there.","**(7a) “The boys are going to the zoo this Saturday”** and **(7b) “A boys are going to the zoo this Saturday”** test whether the determiner matches the noun in number.","**(8a) “Larry went home after the party”** and **(8b) “Larry went the home after the party”** test a place where English does not allow an article."]},{"text":"Two sets test questions.","children":["**(23a) “When will Sam fix his car?”** and **(23b) “When Sam will fix his car?”** test **wh-question** word order, where the helping verb must come before the subject.","**(24a) “What do they sell at the corner store?”** and **(24b) “What they sell at the corner store?”** test the need for the helping verb “do” in a question."]},{"text":"Since each pair tests one rule, the whole test covers many different rules of English grammar.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-06":[{"text":"Two patterns are in these data. For people who arrived as children, a later arrival goes with a lower score. For people who arrived as adults, age of arrival says very little about the score.","children":[]},{"text":"The data set each person's score on the grammar test, out of 276 items, against their **age of arrival (AOA)**. The people are split at puberty, because puberty is the end of the critical period in Lenneberg's proposal. Splitting there tests whether the age effect changes after puberty.","children":[]},{"text":"The strength of each pattern is given by a **correlation** (written r, a number from −1 to +1 for how closely two things are related). A value near −1 means one thing goes down steadily as the other goes up. A value near 0 means there is little relationship.","children":[]},{"text":"For **early arrivals** (aged 3 to 15), the correlation was **−0.87**. This is because children who arrived between about 3 and 7 scored near the maximum, around 270. Scores then went down steadily, to around 250 near age 10 and around 210 to 230 near age 15. Since −0.87 is close to −1, age of arrival predicts much of the score in this group.","children":[]},{"text":"For **late arrivals** (aged 17 to 39), the correlation was only **−0.16**. This is because scores were lower overall and widely scattered, from about 160 to 250. Some adults scored around 250, while others of a similar age scored below 180. Since −0.16 is close to 0, age of arrival predicts little about which adults do well.","children":[]},{"text":"So there is a **strong age effect in childhood** and **lower, highly variable performance in adulthood**.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-07":[{"text":"The **Johnson and Newport (1989)** data have three main features.","children":[]},{"text":"The first is a **strong relationship between AOA and accuracy**. AOA is age of arrival. People who arrived earlier got higher scores.","children":[]},{"text":"The second is that **early learners**, up to about age 8, are often **near-perfect** (native-like, with scores close to native English speakers). Children who arrived after that, between about 8 and 15, scored progressively lower. Since the scores are already going down by then, the decline starts well before puberty.","children":[]},{"text":"The third is a **nearly random distribution of accuracy** for late arrivals. This does not mean the late arrivals were guessing. This is because late arrivals scored well above chance. “Nearly random” means that among late arrivals, age of arrival and score are only weakly related. Scores varied greatly from person to person. Two adults who arrived at the same age could end up with very different scores. Since age of arrival barely predicts an adult's score, the adult scores look nearly random.","children":[]},{"text":"Near-perfect scores for the youngest arrivals are the clearest sign of an early advantage. Adult arrivals scored lower still, with wide differences between individuals.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-08":[{"text":"Johnson and Newport (1989) say their data are consistent with a **sensitive period** for L2 learning. They actually call it a **“critical period.”** They give two reasons.","children":[]},{"text":"The first reason is that **ultimate attainment** (the final level a learner reaches) depends on AOA. This is because scores go down steadily as the age of arrival in childhood goes up. A steady drop like that is consistent with a **loss of language-learning ability over development**.","children":[]},{"text":"The timing is not entirely consistent with Lenneberg's hypothesis. This is because Lenneberg put the end of the critical period at puberty, but the decline in these data starts around age 7 or 8. Lenneberg was working with a large gap between early childhood and adolescence. He had no brain measurements between ages 2 and 18. Since he had that gap, his timing was only a rough estimate.","children":[]},{"text":"The second reason is that attainment in late acquisition is **all over the place**. Adults who arrived at the same age reached very different levels. This is because, after the sensitive period closes, attainment depends on **idiosyncratic learning abilities** (individual differences in things such as memory, motivation or analytical skill). It no longer depends on the usual language acquisition processes. Children all learn through the same natural acquisition process, so they reach similar levels at similar ages. Adults no longer have that process, so they have to rely on their own abilities. Since those abilities differ a lot between people, adult scores are spread out.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-09":[{"text":"Johnson and Newport (1989) see the **maturational state hypothesis** as more consistent with their data. This is because all of their participants had learned a first language normally. They had already used their capacity for acquiring languages. Later starters still learned the L2 less well. Since using the capacity did not protect it, the data fit the maturational state hypothesis, which is also named in the title of their paper.","children":[]},{"text":"The two versions of the **“critical period” hypothesis** start the same way. Both say that early in life, humans have a superior capacity for acquiring languages.","children":["The **exercise hypothesis** says that if the capacity is not exercised (used) during this time, it will disappear or decline with maturation. If the capacity is exercised, further language-learning abilities will remain intact throughout life. So an adult who learned an L1 normally should learn an L2 as well as a child does.","The **maturational state hypothesis** says the capacity disappears or declines with maturation, whether or not it has been used. So even a person with a normal L1 should learn an L2 less well as an adult."]},{"text":"Yes, this changes the interpretation of the **L1 critical period data**. This is because in cases like Genie, the capacity was never exercised. Both versions predict poor learning when the capacity was never exercised. Since both predict the same thing, the L1 cases cannot tell the two versions apart. Only second-language data can tell them apart, because only L2 learners exercised the capacity before learning another language.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-10":[{"text":"The **grammaticality judgment task** may not measure the same knowledge that people use in fluent language. This is because the task is very different from fluent language use. In fluent use, people speak and understand quickly without stopping to think about rules. In the task, people consider each sentence and decide whether it is correct. So people can stop and use **“metalinguistic”** or **“explicit” knowledge** (conscious knowledge about language, such as knowing that English plurals add “-s”).","children":[]},{"text":"**Ellis (2005)** compares **implicit knowledge** and **explicit knowledge** on several characteristics.","children":["For **awareness**, implicit knowledge is an intuitive awareness of linguistic norms, and explicit knowledge is a conscious awareness.","For **type of knowledge**, implicit knowledge is **procedural** (knowing how to do something) and explicit knowledge is **declarative** (knowing facts that can be stated).","For **systematicity**, implicit knowledge is variable but systematic, and explicit knowledge is often anomalous and inconsistent.","For **accessibility**, implicit knowledge comes through **automatic processing**, and explicit knowledge comes through **controlled processing**, which takes effort.","For **use of L2 knowledge**, implicit knowledge is used during fluent performance, and explicit knowledge is used when a speaker is planning and having difficulty.","For **self-report**, implicit knowledge is **nonverbalizable** (people cannot explain it) and explicit knowledge is **verbalizable**.","For **learnability**, implicit knowledge is potentially only learnable within the **critical period**, and explicit knowledge is learnable at **any age**."]},{"text":"This is a problem of test design for an untimed GJT. One learner can get the right answer by consciously applying a taught rule. Another learner can get the same answer from automatic knowledge. The score is the same either way. Since the score cannot tell the two apart, the untimed test may miss the implicit knowledge that the critical period is supposed to affect.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-11":[{"text":"The **untimed GJT** may be a better measure of explicitly learned **“metalinguistic” knowledge** than of grammar acquisition. This is because of what the task asks people to do. **Ellis (2005)** compared the design features of five tests.","children":["**Imitation** and **oral narrative** rely on feel, have time pressure and focus on meaning. They require no metalinguistic knowledge.","The **timed GJT** relies on feel and has time pressure, but it focuses on form. It requires no metalinguistic knowledge.","The **untimed GJT** relies on rules, has no time pressure and focuses on form. It does allow metalinguistic knowledge.","The **metalanguage test** asks people to state rules. It relies on rules, has no time pressure, focuses on form and requires metalinguistic knowledge."]},{"text":"Untimed GJT performance goes with **years of formal education in L2**. Ellis related each test to the age people started learning and to their years of formal instruction. Only two relationships were significant.","children":["The age people started was related to the **timed GJT**, with a correlation of **−0.23**.","Years of formal instruction were related to the **untimed GJT with ungrammatical items**, with a correlation of **0.27**.","The other tests had only weak relationships with either measure."]},{"text":"This is because formal classroom instruction teaches explicit rules, and explicit rules help on the untimed test. The timed test limits the use of explicit rules, so starting age matters there instead. Taught rules help on the untimed test. So an age effect found with an untimed GJT may partly come from explicit, taught knowledge and not from natural grammar acquisition.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-12":[{"text":"If the languages were more similar, the learners would get more help from **transfer**, and the age pattern might look different. This is because transfer (using first-language knowledge while learning a second language) helps more when the two languages are alike. Languages that are **typologically more similar** (more similar in structure, such as word order and word forms) can benefit more from transfer. A learner whose first language shares grammar and vocabulary with the second language can reuse much of what they already know.","children":[]},{"text":"Familiar words are one kind of help. Cognates and loan words, such as French **auto**, **rivière** and **bicyclette**, and Japanese **korokke** and **monoreru**, give learners familiar words to build on.","children":[]},{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** tested people with **English as L2** and **Chinese or Korean as L1**. Chinese and Korean are very different from English in grammar and vocabulary. So these learners got little help from transfer.","children":[]},{"text":"Speakers of a language close to English would get much more help. Their scores might relate to age in a different way. Transfer depends on the language pair. So the age pattern in Johnson and Newport's data would depend on the particular languages involved as well as on age. That is why transfer is both a methodological and a theoretical issue.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-13":[{"text":"Not all aspects of the grammar are equally difficult for late learners. **Determiners** and **plurals** are the hardest, and **word order** and **progressive -ing** are the easiest.","children":[]},{"text":"Johnson and Newport (1989) compared the errors on **12 types of English rules**. They compared native speakers with five arrival groups. The groups arrived at ages 3 to 7, 8 to 10, 11 to 15, 17 to 24 and 25 to 39.","children":["The first rule types are determiners, plurals and **verb subcategorization** (the rules for which kinds of words or phrases can follow a verb). Then come past tense, pronouns, particles and auxiliaries. The rest are third-person singular, wh-questions, yes/no questions, word order and progressive -ing.","Native speakers and the youngest arrivals made very few errors on any rule.","The latest arrivals (aged 17 to 24 and 25 to 39) made the most errors on determiners and plurals. Their errors were around 65 to 68% on determiners and around 45 to 55% on plurals.","Errors on word order and progressive -ing were low for every group, under about 10%."]},{"text":"Some of this looks like the **order in which children learn language**. This is because progressive -ing and word order are among the first structures children master. They are also the easiest for late learners.","children":[]},{"text":"Some of it could be **transfer** (using first-language knowledge in the second language).","children":["Determiners are hard because **Chinese has no determiners**. Chinese speakers have no L1 category for “a” and “the.” Since they have nothing to carry over, English articles are especially hard for them.","Questions do not match the child order. Children learn questions very late, but questions are among the **easiest structures for late learners**. This may be because adults can use general cognitive skills or knowledge from their L1 when they learn question forms."]},{"text":"Since both things matter, how hard a structure is depends on the structure itself and on what the learner already knows from the first language.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-14":[{"text":"The consequence is that AOA effects on GJT performance do not reflect only **maturational constraints** (limits on learning caused by the brain's development). They also depend on the language pairing. We know this because **Birdsong and Molis (2001)** repeated the Johnson and Newport study with **Spanish speakers learning English** and got almost the reverse pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"For **Chinese and Korean speakers learning English**, age mattered most in childhood. Early arrivals (aged 3 to 15) had a strong correlation between age of arrival and score, **−0.87**. Late arrivals (aged 17 to 39) had a weak one, **−0.16**.","children":[]},{"text":"For **Spanish speakers learning English**, age mattered most in adulthood. Early arrivals (aged 3 to 16) had only a weak correlation, **−0.23**. They scored near the maximum regardless of age, with only a slight drop for those arriving around 16. Late arrivals (aged 17 to 44) had a strong correlation, **−0.69**. Their scores went down from around 260 for those arriving near 18 to around 160 for those arriving after 40.","children":[]},{"text":"If a fixed maturational schedule controlled learning, the pattern should look the same for every first language. Instead, the pattern changed with the learners' first language. This may be because Spanish is much closer to English than Chinese or Korean is. Spanish speakers can get more help from **transfer**, so they can reach native-like grammar even when they arrive late in childhood. Since the pattern changes with the first language, the age effect depends on the language pairing as well as on age.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-15":[{"text":"The one thing that holds across studies is that **L2 attainment is lower for older learners** overall. Even so, these findings may not really count as “conclusions.” This is because how much lower depends on several things, and several factors are hard to separate.","children":[]},{"text":"How much lower depends on three things.","children":["It depends on **what constructions are tested**. Some structures, like determiners, are much harder for late learners than others, like word order.","It depends on **how they are tested**. A timed test and an untimed test can give different results, because the untimed test lets people use explicit knowledge.","It depends on **language pairings**. Spanish speakers and Chinese or Korean speakers have different age patterns when they learn English."]},{"text":"Three pairs of things are **hard to dissociate** (hard to separate, because they usually go together).","children":["**Age of acquisition** is hard to separate from the **amount and quality of experience**. This is because people who arrive younger usually have more years of exposure and more immersion, such as schooling in the language.","**Transfer effects** are hard to separate from **L2 acquisition**. This is because L2 performance reflects what was learned and also what carries over from the L1.","**Performance** (how well knowledge is used on a particular task) is hard to separate from **competence** (the underlying knowledge of the language). This is because a test score measures performance, so it may not reveal competence directly."]},{"text":"Since so many factors go together, the age effect is real, but it depends on many factors that are hard to separate.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-16":[],"review-54":[{"text":"Bilinguals listening in English look at the matches because the Russian word for matches gets partly activated and boosts its meaning. This is explained by the **Revised Hierarchical Model of Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994)**. The model works by **spreading activation** (turning on one representation partly turns on the ones linked to it).","children":[]},{"text":"The model has three parts.","children":["One part is the **L1** vocabulary (the first language).","Another part is the **L2** vocabulary (the second language).","The third part is a shared store of concepts (meanings).","**Lexical links** connect words across the two languages. **Conceptual links** connect each vocabulary to meanings.","L1 words are strongly linked to concepts, and L2 words are weakly linked."]},{"text":"In the **Marian and Spivey** task in L2, the model works step by step. Russian and English bilinguals hear an English instruction to pick up the speaker.","children":["The concept for \"speaker\" partly activates the Russian word for \"matches\", which is spichki.","This is because speaker and spichki start with the same sounds. They share **sound overlap** but no meaning.","The activity of spichki feeds back and boosts the concept for \"matches\".","The boosted concept for \"matches\" makes the eyes move toward the L1 competitor. The listener still picks up the speaker."]},{"text":"This does not happen when the task is done in L1. This is because the conceptual links for L2 words might be too weak. An English competitor would need a strong link from its English name to its concept. Since L2 words only have weak links, interference goes from the first language into the second but not back.","children":[]}],"review-1":[],"review-2":[{"text":"The speech continuum changes one thing in equal steps, so every neighboring pair of sounds differs by the same physical amount. This is because a **speech continuum** (a series of sounds that change step by step along one property) was built by **keeping F1 constant**. Only the **F2 onset frequency** (the frequency where the second formant starts its glide) was changed. It was **varied linearly** from stimulus 1 to stimulus 13, meaning it moved by the same step every time. So **adjacent stimuli differ by the same amount across the entire continuum**.","children":[]},{"text":"Along the series, the F2 glide slowly changes. At the start, F2 begins well below the vowel frequency, so it rises. As the starting point goes up, the rise gets smaller until the glide is nearly flat. Later, F2 starts above the vowel frequency, so it falls.","children":[]},{"text":"Equal steps make a fair test of whether hearing follows the sound or the categories. Some neighboring pairs fall inside one consonant category. Other pairs cross a boundary between two categories. A listener who only tracked the sound would hear every step as the same size. A listener with categorical perception would hear steps inside a category as alike and steps across a boundary as different. Since all steps are physically equal, any advantage for boundary pairs has to come from the boundary.","children":[]}],"review-3":[{"text":"If perception were not categorical, the labels would change slowly and smoothly from /b/ to /d/ to /g/. This is because a simple **ideal observer model** assumes perception is **non-categorical**. An ideal observer model is a prediction of how a listener would respond under one idea of how perception works. That means hearing follows the sound gradually, with no sharp category boundaries. The model is a baseline, so real data can be compared with it.","children":[]},{"text":"The first prediction is that **categorization gets “blurry” as you move away from category prototypes**. A **prototype** is the clearest example of a category. The ends of the continuum are /b/ and /g/, with /d/ in the middle. Between these points, **stimulus ambiguity should scale with the distance from the prototype**. So the farther a sound is from the /b/ prototype, the less it sounds like /b/ and the more it sounds like /d/.","children":[]},{"text":"**Stimulus 2** would be labeled **75% /b/ and 25% /d/**. This is because it sits a quarter of the way from the /b/ prototype toward the /d/ prototype.","children":[]},{"text":"Under this idea, **labeling** changes smoothly. The /b/ labels go down in a straight slope to 0 at stimulus 6, where /d/ reaches its peak. The /d/ labels then go down to 0 at stimulus 10, where /g/ reaches the top. Halfway between two prototypes, the two labels are equally likely. No label ever jumps suddenly.","children":[]}],"review-4":[{"text":"If perception were linear, people would be equally good at telling apart every pair, no matter how they label the sounds. This is because a **linear** ideal observer hears equal sound differences as equally big. So it predicts that **discrimination performance should be independent of labeling**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Labeling** asks a listener to name a sound as /b/, /d/ or /g/. **Discrimination** only asks whether two sounds are the **same or different**. These are two separate tasks, even when they use the same sounds.","children":[]},{"text":"Labeling **has to have a “peaked” function**. This is because there are three categories and listeners have to pick between labels. So each category’s labels go up toward its prototype and go down away from it. These peaks come from how the labeling task is set up.","children":[]},{"text":"Discrimination works in a different way. The pairs are two steps apart, from **1 and 3** through **9 and 11**, so every pair has the same physical gap. If people heard F2 onset differences equally well across the whole continuum, accuracy would be the same for every pair. So the prediction is a flat line, at about 0.8.","children":[]},{"text":"A **flat** result would mean category boundaries do not affect hearing differences. A **peaked** result would mean the boundaries make some differences easier to hear.","children":[]}],"review-5":[{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957)** found that labels switch suddenly and that people are best at telling sounds apart right at the switch. This pattern is **categorical perception**, and it is very different from the linear prediction. Listeners labeled each sound on the /b/, /d/ and /g/ continuum. They also judged pairs that were **one step**, **two steps** or **three steps** apart.","children":[]},{"text":"The labels switched **sharply**. Stimuli 1 through about 3 were labeled /b/ almost 100% of the time. Around stimuli 4 and 5, /b/ dropped steeply and /d/ climbed to nearly 100%. /d/ stayed on top through about stimulus 8. Around stimuli 9 and 10, /d/ dropped steeply and /g/ rose to near 100%. The narrow spot where the label flips is the **category boundary**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Listeners were best at discriminating sounds near the category boundaries**. Accuracy peaked at the /b/ to /d/ boundary and again at the /d/ to /g/ boundary. Pairs inside one category were close to **chance** (the score you get by guessing). Bigger steps raised accuracy overall, but the peaks stayed at the boundaries.","children":[]},{"text":"Liberman and colleagues also predicted discrimination from the labels, assuming people only hear a difference when they give two different labels. This prediction matched much of the real pattern. Real accuracy was often a little higher, especially inside categories. So listeners still hear some sound differences beyond the labels.","children":[]}],"review-6":[{"text":"A **thresholded model** has a hard time explaining the bear and pear eye-tracking result, so the result is evidence for **cascaded processing**. A **minimal pair** is two words that differ in one sound, such as bear and pear. This is because looks to the other member of the minimal pair go up gradually toward the boundary. That happens even when the final click is the same, so it comes from graded activation inside one category.","children":[]},{"text":"In a thresholded model, graded feature activity never gets to the phoneme level.","children":["Feature activation goes back and forth between +voiced and −voiced.","Phoneme activation stays flat until /b/ suddenly turns on.","So every sound in a category sends the **same finished phoneme** to the word level.","Then word-level competition would be the same for every /b/, and nothing could make competitor looks change gradually."]},{"text":"In a cascaded model, graded information does get to the word level. More support for the competing phoneme passes on more support for its word. Since that support grows near the boundary, competitor looks grow near the boundary too.","children":[]}],"review-7":[{"text":"The **TRACE** model also has **feedback from the lexical layer to the phoneme layer**. This means activity goes up from phonemes to words and also back down from words to phonemes. **Lexical feedback** (an active word sends support back to its own phonemes) works one step at a time. An unclear sound partly activates a word. Then that word strengthens the reading of the sound that fits it.","children":[]},{"text":"So influence goes both ways.","children":["The sound evidence changes which words are active.","The active words change the balance between possible phonemes while the phoneme decision is still forming.","This helps listeners deal with noisy or ambiguous input."]},{"text":"**Feedback is not the same as cascading**.","children":["Cascading sends incomplete evidence forward, from features to phonemes and from phonemes to words.","Feedback sends influence from words back down to phonemes.","One interactive model can have both."]},{"text":"The reason to add feedback comes from experiments where knowing words changes how sounds are heard, such as the **Ganong effect**.","children":[]}],"review-8":[{"text":"One prediction is a **late “correction” process**, where the listener categorizes the sound as usual but then leans toward reporting a word. This is because in this account **categorical perception works as usual**, and the word knowledge only comes in at the response stage. The result is a **bias to report having heard a word**.","children":[]},{"text":"Three kinds of continua are compared.","children":["In **word-nonword** continua, the voiced end makes a word.","In **nonword-word** continua, the voiceless end makes a word.","In **neutral** continua, neither end has a word advantage."]},{"text":"A bias added after perception pushes answers toward the word by the same amount everywhere.","children":["For word-nonword, voiced answers stay near 0.25 at the long VOT end and never fall to 0.","For nonword-word, voiced answers start near 0.75 at the short VOT end, not at 1.","So even a clear sound would sometimes be reported as the word."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** (the difference in voiced answers between the two word conditions) would be **the same size at every step**, near 0.25. Since the bias only nudges the reported answer, perception and word knowledge stay separate.","children":[]}],"review-9":[{"text":"A second prediction is that **word knowledge shifts the category boundary**. Then the word effect should be **strongest for the items closest to the boundary**. This is because those are the sounds where knowing the word can help clear up which first phoneme it was.","children":[]},{"text":"In this account, word knowledge changes where the listener switches from one phoneme to the other. Near an unclear start, the reading that makes a real word gets extra support. So the categorization itself changes, not just the reported answer.","children":[]},{"text":"The conditions should only differ in the middle.","children":["At both ends, the sound is clear, so voiced answers start near 1 and end near 0 in every condition.","Near the boundary, the word-nonword condition switches later, and the nonword-word condition switches earlier."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** makes a **peak**. It is near 0 at the ends and goes up to about 0.35 for the items next to the boundary. At the ends, the strong sound evidence already settles the consonant, so word knowledge has little room to help.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the two accounts differ in shape, a **flat effect** points to a reporting bias, and a **peaked effect** points to a boundary shift.","children":[]}],"review-10":[{"text":"**Ganong’s results** match the **second model**, the boundary shift, very closely. This is because the word effect was biggest right at the boundary.","children":["At the short and long ends of the VOT continuum, the word-nonword and nonword-word conditions give almost the same answers.","Near the boundary, the two conditions pull apart the most.","The **lexical effect** goes from about 0.1 at the ends up to a peak near **0.3** at the boundary."]},{"text":"A peak at the boundary rules out a simple reporting bias. A reporting bias would be the same size everywhere. Here word knowledge matters most where the sound evidence is weakest. At the boundary, the word that fits tips the balance. At the ends, the sound decides the answer by itself.","children":[]},{"text":"Ganong raised several ideas for how this could happen. He focused on different kinds of **guessing strategies**, where listeners use word knowledge only when they are unsure of the sound.","children":[]},{"text":"Later models put more weight on **feedback**. In **interactive models**, an active word sends support back to its own phonemes. So the whole word can change how an unclear part of it is heard during recognition.","children":[]}],"review-11":[{"text":"A **TRACE simulation of the Ganong effect** shows the activation of words and phonemes changing as an unclear start turns into the word **plug**. This is because the model records **phoneme-level and word-level activations** at four moments, called frames, one as each new sound comes in. The input starts with a sound **ambiguous between /b/ and /p/**, then /l/, the vowel /ʌ/ and /g/.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **first frame**, only the unclear first sound has arrived. Both /b/ and /p/ are a little active, at about the same level, near 0.15 to 0.2. Word activation is still very low.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **second and third frames**, /l/ and the vowel arrive.","children":["Partial /b/ evidence supports words starting with bl, and partial /p/ evidence supports words starting with pl.","**Plug**, **blood** and **blush** all go up together, because all three fit what has been heard so far.","/p/ starts to pull ahead of /b/."]},{"text":"In the **fourth frame**, the final **/g/** arrives. The /g/ fits plug but does not fit blood or blush. So **plug** becomes the most active word, at about 0.45, and the others fall away. Since plug wins, **/p/ ends up more active than /b/**.","children":[]}],"review-12":[{"text":"In TRACE, the final /g/ makes **“plug”** win, and plug then pushes the unclear first sound toward /p/. This is because activity builds up for plug as the input comes in, most of all when the **final /g/** arrives. The /g/ does not fit “blood” or “blush”, which were also active in Frame 3. Blood and blush fit the early part, so they were possible for a while. Once /g/ arrives, their endings do not match, so they lose support.","children":[]},{"text":"The model explains **word effects on phoneme category boundaries** with **top-down processing** (higher-level knowledge changing lower-level analysis).","children":["Activity at the lexical level sends **feedback to the phoneme level**.","The feedback gives **more activation to the phoneme that fits a word**.","Plug is the winning word, so /p/ gets the extra support."]},{"text":"Since /p/ belongs to the word the whole sequence supports, the unclear first sound ends up heard as /p/. So information at the end of a word can change how an earlier sound is heard.","children":[]}],"review-13":[{"text":"Syntactic explanations of the garden path start with a **parser** (the mental process that builds a **syntactic tree** for each sentence we hear or read). A syntactic tree records how words group into phrases and how the phrases fit together.","children":[]},{"text":"The correct tree for the horse sentence is big. The subject is a large **NP** (noun phrase). It holds “the horse” plus a shortened clause meaning “that was raced past the barn”. The words “that” and “was” are silent, and the clause has an empty subject spot. Inside it, raced takes the **PP** (prepositional phrase) “past the barn”. The main **VP** (verb phrase) is just **fell**.","children":[]},{"text":"The failed parse is small. “The horse” is a plain NP, and “raced past the barn” is the main VP. This is why fell has nowhere to attach, so the parse breaks down.","children":[]},{"text":"The parser has to work with **limited memory** and **time constraints**. This is because words come in fast, so the parser cannot wait for the end of the sentence. It also can only keep a few structures going at once. Since it works under these limits, it needs some way to deal with ambiguous sentences.","children":[]}],"review-14":[{"text":"The parser first prefers the smaller tree, and this strategy is called **minimal attachment**. It means fitting each new word in with the fewest new **nodes**. Nodes are points in a tree that stand for a grouping, such as an NP, a VP or a sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"This strategy saves space. Fewer nodes means less structure to build. It also means less to hold in memory at that moment.","children":[]},{"text":"The correct version of the horse sentence is much more complex. This is because it has many more nodes. It needs extra nodes for the embedded clause. It also needs nodes for the silent “that” and “was”. The failed parse just makes “raced past the barn” the main VP, which takes far fewer nodes.","children":[]},{"text":"So minimal attachment picks **raced as the main verb**. This is because that reading needs the fewest nodes. The reading where “raced past the barn” describes the horse needs an embedded relative clause. So the parser passes it over at first. When fell arrives, it needs the bigger structure. Since the parser chose the small tree, the reader has to rebuild the tree, and that is the garden path.","children":[]}],"review-15":[{"text":"**Late closure** is a second parsing strategy. It is a bias to keep the current clause **open** as long as possible. Keeping a clause open means adding new words to it instead of closing it and starting something new.","children":[]},{"text":"In “The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite”, the phrase “with the dynamite” joins the big verb phrase starting with “blew open”. **Frazier (1987)** gives two trees for this sentence. In the **preferred** tree, the PP attaches to the VP. This is **verb-phrase attachment**, so the dynamite is the **instrument** (the tool) used to blow open the safe. In the **unpreferred** tree, the PP attaches to the NP “the safe”. This is **noun-phrase attachment**, so the phrase tells which safe, the one that had the dynamite.","children":[]},{"text":"Both are grammatical, but they mean different things. The parser picks the VP attachment. This is because late closure keeps the verb phrase open, so the new phrase joins it. Minimal attachment also likes the VP attachment, because it needs fewer nodes than an extra NP around “the safe with the dynamite”. Since the preferred parse is also the sensible one here, readers get the right meaning easily.","children":[]}],"review-16":[{"text":"People read “The burglar blew open the safe with the jewels” more slowly. This is because the sensible meaning needs the **unpreferred parse**.","children":[]},{"text":"Jewels are not a tool for blowing open a safe. The sensible meaning is that the burglar blew open **the safe that had the jewels**. That means “with the jewels” has to attach to the noun phrase “the safe”. That is the unpreferred NP attachment.","children":[]},{"text":"Careful tests of **reading time** and **eye movements** find the slowdown. Reading time is how long readers spend on each part of a sentence. Eye movements show where readers look and when they jump back to reread. The extra time shows up right where the parse has to change.","children":[]},{"text":"Readers **briefly think** the jewels were used to blow open the safe. This is because late closure makes the parser attach the phrase to the verb phrase first, just like with the dynamite. Then the meaning of jewels clashes with the tool reading. So the parser moves the attachment to the noun phrase, and readers **retrace their steps**. Since that repair takes time, reading is slower even though the final meaning is easy.","children":[]}],"review-17":[{"text":"The syntactic explanation assumes a parser that works **only on abstract syntactic structure**. It is like the **“lumber mill”** example from speech perception. In a lumber mill, each stage works on its own material and passes a finished product to the next stage. In the same way, the parser builds structure from grammatical categories alone. It does not check meaning or context. Meaning gets worked out afterward from the finished structure.","children":[]},{"text":"The parser has **its own operating characteristics**. It wants the simplest tree possible, which is **minimal attachment**. It gets there partly by leaving clauses open as long as possible, which is **late closure**. Both of these keep down how much structure it has to build and hold at once.","children":[]},{"text":"This is a very efficient way to handle ambiguity, because the parser **ignores ambiguity until it becomes a problem**. This is because it commits to one likely structure and builds only that one. Most of the time the first choice is right, so no extra work is needed. When a later word does not fit, the structure has to be fixed. Since that fix is the garden path, garden paths are the price of being efficient.","children":[]}],"review-18":[{"text":"Listeners briefly treat the empty towel as the place to put the apple. **Spivey and colleagues (1995)** found this when they tested whether **world knowledge** can come from the scene right in front of you.","children":[]},{"text":"People saw a pencil, a box, an empty towel and an apple sitting on a towel. They heard “Put the apple on the towel in the box” and did it while their eyes were tracked. The clear version was “Put the apple that’s on the towel in the box”.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because of **minimal attachment**. With it, “the towel” briefly seems to end the clause “put the apple on the towel”. So “on the towel” sounds like the **destination** (where the apple should go). In the real meaning, “on the towel” tells **which apple** to move, and “in the box” is the destination.","children":[]},{"text":"With the unclear instruction, people looked at the apple, then the empty towel, then the box. The look to the apple came near 1,000 milliseconds, and the look to the box came near 2,000 milliseconds. With the clear instruction, they looked from the apple straight to the box. Since every word was heard correctly, the mistake was only in how the location phrase got attached.","children":[]}],"review-19":[{"text":"With **two apples** in the display, people mostly stop looking at the empty towel. This display is the **two-referent context** (two possible things “the apple” could mean). One apple sits on a towel and the other sits somewhere else. The empty towel and the box are still there.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because “the apple” alone no longer tells which apple to move. So “on the towel” now has a job, which is picking out one apple. Since a phrase that picks out the apple is expected, listeners read “on the towel” that way from the start. The destination reading never takes hold.","children":[]},{"text":"People usually looked at the apple on the towel and then at the box. Looks to the empty towel were much less common. The **order and timing of eye movements**, measured against the nouns in the speech, **did not differ** between the unclear and the clear instruction.","children":[]},{"text":"So the unclear instruction was handled just like the clear one with “that’s on the towel”. Since only the scene changed, the scene changed how the same sentence got parsed.","children":[]}],"review-20":[{"text":"People look at the empty towel much less when there are **two apples**. This is because with two apples it becomes **felicitous** (natural and appropriate) to say which apple you mean. With one apple, there is no need to say which apple. So “on the towel” seems like extra information and gets heard as a destination. With two apples, “on the towel” has a clear job, which is picking the right apple.","children":[]},{"text":"The measure was how often people looked at the wrong destination, the empty towel. With one apple, the unclear instruction led to these looks on about **55%** of trials. The clear instruction led to almost none. With two apples, the unclear instruction led to these looks on only about **17%** of trials. That is close to the roughly 12% for the clear instruction.","children":[]},{"text":"The words were exactly the same in both displays. Since only the scene changed, the scene alone changed how often listeners were misled. This is an effect of **referential context** (the set of objects a phrase could be about). It affects which grammatical relationship listeners pick while they are still hearing the sentence.","children":[]}],"review-21":[{"text":"The two aphasias seem to split neatly in two, with fluent nonsense on one side and choppy speech made of content words on the other. This is an attractive **dichotomy** (a two-way split).","children":[]},{"text":"**Wernicke’s aphasia** is called **“word salad”** or **“Jabberwocky,”** often with **neologisms**, while **syntax is roughly intact**. “Jabberwocky” is Lewis Carroll’s poem of grammatical sentences full of nonsense words.","children":["Asked about his health, one patient said “I felt worse because I can no longer keep in mind from the mind of the minds to keep me from mind and up to the ear which can be to find among ourselves.” Kriendler and colleagues (1971) reported it.","Asked about his poor vision, another patient said “My wires don’t hire right.”","Asked if he was a doctor, an aphasic physician said “Me? Yes, sir, I’m a male demaploze on my own. I still know my tubaboys what for I have that’s gone hell and some of them go.” **Demaploze** and **tubaboys** are neologisms.","One patient called a fork “a need for a schedule” and a spoon “How many schemes on your throat.”"]},{"text":"**Broca’s aphasia** is called **“telegraphic” speech**. It has **difficulty with word order and grammatical particles**, but the **important content words** are mostly spared.","children":["Asked what brought him to the hospital, one patient said “Yes … ah … Monday … ah … Dad and P.H. (the patient’s name) and Dad … hospital. Two … ah doctors … and ah … thirty minutes … and yes … ah … hospital. And, er Wednesday … nine o’clock and eh Thursday, ten o’clock … doctors. Two doctors … and ah … teeth. Yeah … fine.” Badecker and Caramazza (1985) reported it.","Words like Dad, hospital and doctors carry the story. The small grammar words that would join them are missing."]}],"review-22":[{"text":"Broca’s own patients would probably not count as Broca’s aphasics today. They likely had **apraxia of speech** (a disorder in planning and coordinating the movements of speaking). This is because the modern dichotomy is about language, not movement. Wernicke’s aphasia is **problems with activation, or possibly inhibition, in the lexicon** (the mental store of words). Broca’s aphasia is **problems with grammar**. **Activation** makes a candidate word available. **Inhibition** holds back competing words, so weak inhibition could let wrong words slip out.","children":[]},{"text":"That is **fairly distant from how Broca described his original cases**. Broca described **Leborgne**, known as **“Tan.”** Tan **could only produce one jargon syllable**, “tan.” Otherwise he **seemed to be in possession of his senses**. He even **seemed to understand what was being said**.","children":[]},{"text":"Broca’s description raises three questions.","children":["His other senses being intact matters because then the loss is only about speech, which makes it a **dissociation**.","A “capacity for articulate speech” sounds more believable as a job for a brain region than “criminality” or “philoprogenitiveness” (a phrenology term for love of one’s children).","Testing Tan today would mean measuring his deficits at the phonetic, phonological, lexical and syntactic **levels of description used in modern linguistics**."]},{"text":"This creates a real confusion. Broca’s actual patients were arguably **unable to produce speech at all**. That seems to come from **motor control problems**, not grammar. Apraxia of speech can cut speech down severely without any loss of grammatical knowledge.","children":[]}],"review-23":[{"text":"The aphasia dichotomy is attractive because linguistics also draws a sharp line between **the grammar** and **the lexicon**. This is because late 20th-century formal linguistics separated these two parts. The **grammar** is the set of rules for combining words into sentences. The **lexicon** is the store of single words, which have to be memorized. So Wernicke’s aphasia would be a lexicon disorder, and Broca’s aphasia would be a grammar disorder.","children":[]},{"text":"The line starts with **Chomsky’s formulation of the computational problem of language learning**. We do not recognize a new sentence because it matches something familiar in any simple way. We recognize it because **it is generated by the grammar** each person has internalized. So a sentence never heard before can still be understood.","children":[]},{"text":"**Chomsky (1955)** also gave **compelling demonstrations of the independence of syntax and semantics**. Neither “colorless green ideas sleep furiously” nor “furiously sleep ideas green colorless” has ever occurred in an English speaker’s past experience.","children":["“colorless green ideas sleep furiously” makes no sense. Its word order still follows English grammar, so it is **grammatical**.","“furiously sleep ideas green colorless” uses the same words in an order grammar does not allow, so it is **not grammatical**.","Both are equally unfamiliar and equally meaningless. Since only the grammar differs, **grammar must be its own system**."]}],"review-24":[{"text":"Special psycholinguistic tests are needed because ordinary sentences can be understood correctly without using grammar at all. This is because conversation has lots of **redundant cues** (separate clues that all point to the same meaning). “Man bites dog” is the newspaper example of a surprising event.","children":[]},{"text":"“The dog bit the man” gives many ways to get the right meaning.","children":["The listener can just put DOG, BITE and MAN into the **most likely event**, with or without syntax. Dogs bite people far more often than people bite dogs.","The sentence uses **canonical word order** (agent, verb, undergoer, which is subject, verb, object). The **agent** is the doer, and the **undergoer** is the one the action happens to.","In English the first noun is usually the agent.","The **discourse context** (the surrounding conversation) also clears up a lot."]},{"text":"Broca’s patients might understand by leaning on these **compensatory heuristics** (mental shortcuts that make up for a weakness somewhere else). A patient could guess the most plausible event. A patient could also guess that the first noun is the doer. Either shortcut gets “The dog bit the man” right with no grammar.","children":[]},{"text":"So a better test sentence is “The dog that the man is biting is angry.” Here the **man** is doing the biting. The dog comes first and dogs are likelier biters, so both shortcuts give the wrong answer. Only working out the grammar gets it right.","children":[]}],"review-25":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif** wrote exactly this kind of test sentence, plus **control sentences**, in four groups of eight. This is because each group changes how much meaning helps. A **center-embedded** sentence has a relative clause in the middle of the main clause. In every one of them, the first noun is acted on and the doer is inside the clause.","children":[]},{"text":"**Control sentences**, group A, are simple and in normal order, like “The girl is kicking a green ball,” “The cat is chasing a blue bird” and “The boy is riding a new bicycle.”","children":[]},{"text":"**Center-embedded sentences with semantic constraints**, group B, let meaning give away the roles. Examples are “The apple that the boy is eating is red,” “The wagon that the horse is pulling is green” and “The balloon that the clown is holding is round.” Apples cannot eat boys.","children":[]},{"text":"**Reversible center-embedded sentences**, group C, have two participants who could each do the action. Examples are “The cow that the monkey is scaring is yellow,” “The horse that the bear is kicking is brown” and “The girl that the boy is tickling is happy.” Only grammar gives the roles.","children":[]},{"text":"**Improbable center-embedded sentences**, group D, describe unlikely events. Examples are “The bird that the worm is eating is blue,” “The man that the horse is riding is fat” and “The policeman that the robber is chasing is skinny.” Common sense points to the wrong answer.","children":[]},{"text":"A listener who relies on meaning should pass groups A and B but fail C and D. A listener who parses normally should pass all four.","children":[]}],"review-26":[{"text":"Broca’s patients got sentences wrong only when grammar was the only way to tell who did what to whom. This is because **Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** used a **sentence-picture matching task** (hear a sentence, then choose which picture fits it). Accuracy shows whether the listener understood the roles, with no speaking needed. Healthy listeners and patients with Broca’s, conduction and Wernicke’s aphasia all heard the four sentence types.","children":[]},{"text":"**Healthy controls** were **more or less equally good** on every type, near the top score.","children":[]},{"text":"**Wernicke’s patients** were **poor overall**, even on simple control sentences. That is probably because of their **comprehension deficits**, which affect words as well as grammar.","children":[]},{"text":"**Broca’s patients** did **well** on control and semantically constrained sentences, where meaning gives away the answer. They did **poorly** on reversible and improbable sentences. In those, **syntax conflicted with common sense** or with the likely roles of the nouns.","children":[]},{"text":"This **specific comprehension deficit** was taken as evidence that **Broca’s aphasia affects syntax specifically**. Since the problem shows up in understanding as well as speaking, it seems to be in the grammar itself. The paper’s title is “Dissociation of algorithmic and heuristic processes.” Broca’s patients lose the **algorithmic** route (working out the grammar) but keep the **heuristic** shortcuts (guessing from meaning).","children":[]}],"review-27":[{"text":"**Dick and colleagues** found **problems with this dichotomy** by describing one event with four kinds of sentences. This is because only two of the four sentence types put the doer first. Participants heard each sentence and had to **indicate which animal is doing the action**. A picture of a dog and a picture of a cat appeared on a computer screen. Participants pressed a button on a response box to choose. The event was always the dog biting the cat.","children":[]},{"text":"Two types put the **doer first**.","children":["An **active** sentence is “The dog is biting the cat.”","**Subject cleft** is “It’s the dog that is biting the cat.” A **cleft** splits a simple sentence with “It’s … that” to focus on one part. Here the focused noun is the doer.","For both, picking the first animal mentioned gives the **right** answer."]},{"text":"Two types put the **receiver first**.","children":["**Object cleft** is “It’s the cat that the dog is biting.” Here the focused noun is the one being bitten.","**Passive** is “The cat is bitten by the dog.” The receiver becomes the grammatical subject.","For both, picking the first animal mentioned gives the **wrong** answer."]},{"text":"Since the first-animal shortcut fails on object clefts and passives, only a listener who **parses the grammar** gets all four types right.","children":[]}],"review-28":[{"text":"Broca’s patients did worst on the hard sentences, object clefts and passives, but they were not great on any sentence type. We know this because aphasia patients and **healthy aging controls** did Dick’s task, and the pattern looked **much like what Caramazza and Zurif observed**. Patients were grouped by aphasia type with the Western Aphasia Battery.","children":[]},{"text":"Each group had its own profile.","children":["**Elderly controls** scored close to 100% on all four types.","**Anomic** patients (whose main problem is finding words) scored about 89% on actives and subject clefts. They scored about 69% on object clefts and about 73% on passives.","**Conduction** patients scored about 75% on actives and subject clefts but about 42% on object clefts.","**Wernicke’s** patients scored low on every type, around 43% to 50%."]},{"text":"**Broca’s** patients scored about 61% on actives and 65% on subject clefts. They dropped to about 42% on object clefts and about 54% on passives. Object clefts and passives are **noncanonical** (they break the usual doer-first order). So they are the **syntactically complex** sentences.","children":[]},{"text":"Broca’s patients had two problems at once. Even on simple actives, they were far below the elderly controls. On complex sentences, they lost even more. So their pattern is a **general drop** plus an **extra cost** for complex structures.","children":[]}],"review-29":[{"text":"Healthy students under stress make the same kind of errors that Broca’s patients make, so the patients’ problem may be **performance** and not lost grammar. This is because researchers gave the task to **healthy undergraduates** under **conditions that stress the language system**.","children":["**Compressed** speech is speech played faster than normal.","A **low-pass filter** cuts the higher frequencies, so speech sounds muffled.","Noise was also added.","**Auditory digits** and **visual digits** were number lists to remember while listening, which uses up memory."]},{"text":"With **one stressor**, accuracy stayed high on actives and subject clefts. Object clefts suffered most. With noise, accuracy fell from about 97% on actives to about 86% on object clefts. Passives dropped to about 91%.","children":[]},{"text":"With **two stressors**, the drops got bigger. With muffled and sped-up speech together, accuracy was about 91% on actives and 90% on subject clefts. It fell to about 66% on object clefts and 68% on passives.","children":[]},{"text":"So **object clefts and passives are the most vulnerable** to every kind of stress. These students have no brain damage and no loss of grammar knowledge. Stress alone gave them an aphasia-like pattern. Complex sentences may need more processing resources, so any loss of resources hurts them first. Since that is true, Broca’s patients may still have the grammar but lack the resources to use it.","children":[]}],"review-30":[{"text":"Broca’s aphasics’ comprehension deficits do not seem to be specific to language. This is because **Saygin and colleagues (2004)** asked patients to **match an action to an object**, with and without words.","children":["In the **non-linguistic** version, a drawing had a boy pantomiming an action, like licking. Participants picked the matching object from pictures like an ice cream cone, a cake, broccoli and a rooster.","In the **linguistic** version, the action was given in words instead."]},{"text":"**Broca’s aphasics had difficulty with this task whether the stimuli were linguistic or not**.","children":["Without words, controls scored **98.5%** and aphasic patients **93.4%**.","With words, controls scored **99.4%** and aphasic patients **87.5%**."]},{"text":"Accuracy also depended on the **distractor** (the wrong choice offered).","children":["With **unrelated** distractors, controls scored 99.8% and patients 93.3%.","With **semantic** distractors (related in meaning to the right object), controls scored 99.2% and patients dropped to **86.3%**.","With **affordance-based** distractors, controls scored 97.7% and patients 91.6%. An **affordance** is an action an object allows, like another object that can also be licked."]},{"text":"The pantomime version takes language out but keeps meaning in. The patient still has to understand the action and link it to the right object, just as a verb links to its object. A purely grammar deficit predicts no trouble here. Since the patients still struggled, the problem seems to include **action meanings** in general.","children":[]}],"review-31":[{"text":"Doing badly on the pantomime version went with damage to **Broca’s area**, so Broca’s area does more than syntax. This is because **performance on the non-verbal version was significantly correlated with damage to Broca’s area**. Lesion maps marked the brain areas linked to trouble with **non-linguistic pantomime interpretation**. The maps show three horizontal slices of the brain. Red and yellow mark the strongest links between damage and poor performance. The strongest links are in the left frontal region in front of the **central sulcus**, which includes Broca’s area.","children":[]},{"text":"The task used no language at all.","children":["Participants only had to understand a pantomimed action and pick an object.","They did not have to say a sentence.","They did not have to work out the grammar of a spoken instruction."]},{"text":"Since no language was involved, patients with damage there struggled to connect an action with its meaning. So Broca’s area supports **understanding actions** as well as grammar. Any account of Broca’s area has to explain both the grammar findings and this trouble with actions.","children":[]}],"review-32":[{"text":"Researchers find out what infants hear by watching **head turns** and **sucking**. This is because infants cannot tell us, but these behaviors change when an infant notices something new.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **conditioned head turn procedure** (used in Janet Werker's infant studies lab), the infant learns to turn toward a sound when it changes.","children":["The infant sits on a parent's lap, and an experimenter keeps the infant looking forward with toys.","When the sound changes and the infant turns, a lighted, moving toy rewards the turn.","Once the infant has learned this, a head turn right after a change means the infant **heard the change**."]},{"text":"In **high-amplitude sucking**, the infant sucks on a pacifier hooked to a pressure recorder. Strong sucks make a sound play.","children":["When the same sound keeps playing, the infant gets bored and sucks less. This is **habituation** (getting used to a repeated sound).","If a new sound makes sucking **go back up**, the infant **noticed the change**."]},{"text":"Neither method needs the infant to name or make a sound. The experimenter picks exactly which sound difference to play. Since only the sound difference changes, a head turn or renewed sucking tells us whether infants hear the same differences adults hear.","children":[]}],"review-33":[{"text":"Four-month-olds noticed a change in **voice onset time** only when it crossed an adult category boundary. **Eimas and colleagues (1971)** found this using high-amplitude sucking. **Voice onset time**, or VOT, is the delay between releasing a stop consonant and the start of the vocal folds vibrating. With +100 milliseconds of VOT, voicing starts much later than with +10 milliseconds.","children":[]},{"text":"Infants heard one syllable over and over until their sucking dropped. Then the sound changed or stayed the same.","children":["In **20D**, the change was 20 milliseconds, from +20 to +40. The two sounds belong to **different** adult categories.","In **20S**, the change was also 20 milliseconds, from −20 to 0 or from +60 to +80. The two sounds belong to the **same** category.","In **0**, the **control** group, the sound never changed."]},{"text":"Only the 20D infants started sucking more again. In 20D, sucking jumped back up to around 50 per minute. In 20S, sucking barely recovered and kept dropping. In the control, sucking kept dropping to about 15 per minute.","children":[]},{"text":"This matters because 20D and 20S were the same size of change. So the only difference was **crossing the category boundary**. Since the control shows what repetition alone does, the jump in 20D must come from hearing a new category.","children":[]}],"review-34":[{"text":"Eimas's result was a big deal because it made categorical perception look **innate**. This is because categorical perception was thought to be the **first step** in turning speech into a **symbolic format**. A symbolic format stores a sound as a category, such as /b/. Later language processes would use that format. So finding it in infants meant the starting point for language was already there.","children":[]},{"text":"Four-month-olds are really little.","children":["Their **gross motor milestones** (big body movements) are things like handling objects with two hands. They are also just holding their head and chest up while lying face down (prone).","Their color vision and **visual acuity** (sharpness of vision) are still \"in progress.\"","They are only starting to make **vowel-like sounds** instead of creaky whines."]},{"text":"So this is very, very early for a skill that seems to be **just for language**. This is because infants were sorting speech sounds into categories before they could make those sounds. Their other skills were still immature. Since speech categories showed up so far ahead of everything else, the ability seemed built in, not learned.","children":[]}],"review-35":[{"text":"English-learning infants can hear non-English sound differences at six months, but they mostly lose them by twelve months. This is **perceptual narrowing** (losing sensitivity to sound differences your language does not use).","children":[]},{"text":"**Werker and Tees (1984)** tested English-learning infants on two non-English contrasts. One is a **Hindi** contrast between two \"ta\"-like sounds. The other is a **Salish** contrast between \"ki\" and \"qi\" from Nthlakampx, a Salish language. They counted how many infants reached a **discrimination criterion** at each age.","children":["At 6 to 8 months, about 95% passed for Hindi and about 80% for Salish.","At 8 to 10 months, about 67% passed for Hindi and about 52% for Salish.","At 10 to 12 months, only about 20% passed for Hindi and about 10% for Salish.","Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants passed for their own contrast close to 100% of the time."]},{"text":"The drop happens because of **language experience**. This is because infants who hear the contrast in their language keep it. English-learning infants never hear it, so they lose it by about their first birthday. This is **perceptual reorganization** in the first year of life.","children":[]},{"text":"Narrowing is related to **categorical perception** because infants are forming their native categories. Sounds their language puts in one category get harder to tell apart. So a non-English difference inside one English category ends up heard as a **within-category difference**.","children":[]}],"review-36":[{"text":"The compelling story says babies are born able to hear every speech contrast, and learning just throws out the ones they do not need. This is because it puts the infant findings together in three steps.","children":["Speech perception depends on **categorical perception** abilities that are a special part of the language system.","These abilities show up very early in development.","Children start out as **\"citizens of the world\"** (able to hear all contrasts)."]},{"text":"Being citizens of the world means infants have innate, **language-general** knowledge about speech (knowledge that works for the sounds of every language). So a newborn is ready for any human language. Then children quickly **tune** their hearing to the one language around them.","children":[]},{"text":"In this story, learning only **selects**. This is because the contrasts are already there from birth. Learning narrows down the **inventory** (the full set of contrasts the child can hear). It keeps the contrasts that matter in the native language. It drops the ones the native language does not use. Since learning only removes things, the native language just decides which innate categories survive.","children":[]}],"review-37":[{"text":"A child who hears a new word once cannot tell what it means, because one experience fits many possible meanings. This is called the **inductive problem of early word learning**. **Induction** is reasoning from particular examples to a general rule, and here the rule is the meaning of a word.","children":[]},{"text":"Say a child hears a new word while watching a cat. The word might mean **CAT**, **ANIMAL** or **PET**, which are categories at different levels. It might be **FELIX**, the name of this one cat. It might name a property, such as **FURRY** or **ALIVE**. It might name an action, such as **WALKING**. It might name a part, such as **WHISKERS** or **LEG**.","children":[]},{"text":"All of these possible meanings together are the **hypothesis space**. The child cannot pick one from a single scene. This is because every possible meaning fits what the child sees, and nothing in the scene singles out the one the speaker means.","children":[]},{"text":"This is a form of **Quine’s “gavagai” problem**. A linguist studying an unknown language hears a speaker say “gavagai” as a rabbit runs by. “Gavagai” might mean rabbit, but it might also mean undetached rabbit parts, a stage of a rabbit or something else.","children":[]},{"text":"Children have this problem with every new word, and they still learn words fast. Since they learn fast, something must cut down the possibilities. Joint attention, cues to what the speaker intends and default strategies do this.","children":[]}],"review-38":[{"text":"Children learn what words mean partly through **joint attention** (sharing an attentional focus with another person). In joint attention, two people look at the same object. Each one knows that the other is paying attention to it too.","children":[]},{"text":"Joint attention helps with word meaning. This is because when the adult says a word, the object they are both looking at is the most likely referent. So the word gets tied to what the speaker is paying attention to. This makes the **hypothesis space** (the set of possible meanings) smaller.","children":[]},{"text":"Infants can join an adult’s focus once they can follow gaze. **Gaze following** (looking where someone else is looking) **emerges around 9 months of age**. When an adult looks over at an object, the infant follows the adult’s eyes to it.","children":[]},{"text":"Gaze following **signals an understanding of reference**. This is because an infant who follows gaze understands that the adult’s attention is aimed at something specific. Since the infant knows what the adult is attending to, the infant can link the adult’s word to that object.","children":[]}],"review-39":[{"text":"**Eighteen-month-olds learned a new word** when an adult was interacting with them, but **not** when the word came from a voice behind a screen. **Baldwin and colleagues** found this, and it means that **joint attention optimizes word learning**.","children":[]},{"text":"There were two groups of infants. Both groups heard a novel word while playing with an object. In the **joint attention group**, an adult interacting with the baby labeled the object the baby was playing with. In the **no joint attention group**, a voice from behind a screen labeled the object the baby was playing with.","children":[]},{"text":"The two groups were the same in most ways. In both groups, the word came while the infant was focused on the same object. The only difference was whether a visible person was engaged with the infant and the object.","children":[]},{"text":"The 18-month-olds learned the novel word in the joint attention group but not in the no joint attention group. This is because hearing a word while seeing an object is not enough. Infants need evidence that a speaker means the word to refer to that object. Since the hidden voice gave the same word at the same moment, the difference in learning came from the visible, engaged speaker.","children":[]}],"review-40":[{"text":"Children narrow the **hypothesis space** (the set of possible meanings) for new words by using **intentional cues from the speaker**. An **intentional cue** is a signal of what the speaker means to refer to. One intentional cue is **eye gaze direction**. This works because when a speaker looks toward one toy, the eyes mark which object the speaker is paying attention to.","children":[]},{"text":"**Baldwin** taught new words to infants of 16 to 17 months and infants of 18 to 19 months. There were two conditions. In **follow-in labeling**, the adult looked at the toy that the infant was focused on while saying the new word. In **discrepant labeling**, the adult looked at a toy in a bucket while saying the new word. The toy in the bucket was a different toy from the one the infant was focused on.","children":[]},{"text":"The two conditions are needed for this reason. In follow-in labeling, the infant and the adult look at the same toy, so both cues point to one toy. In discrepant labeling, the infant’s focus and the speaker’s focus point to different toys. Infants who just attach a new word to whatever they are looking at would link it to their own toy. Infants who follow the speaker’s gaze would link it to the toy in the bucket. Since the two answers are different, comparing the conditions tells whether infants treat the speaker’s gaze as evidence of what the word means.","children":[]}],"review-41":[{"text":"Infants can use an adult’s **facial expression** to figure out which object a new word names. **Akhtar and colleagues** found that infants **used adults’ facial expressions to identify the intended referent of a novel word**.","children":[]},{"text":"This works because the adult’s emotional reaction to each object tells whether it is the one the adult meant. A satisfied expression means the speaker found the object the speaker wanted. A disappointed expression means the object is not the one the speaker meant. Since the expression picks out one object, infants linked the new word to that object.","children":[]},{"text":"These are intentional cues from the speaker, and there are two kinds here. **Affective cues** are emotional expressions. An expression can tell whether an object is the one the speaker was searching for. **Behavioral cues** are the speaker’s actions. An action, such as reaching for something, can tell what the speaker wants.","children":[]},{"text":"Several objects may be there when a word is spoken. The speaker’s reaction can single out one of them. Since the child has to read the reaction, learning a word involves understanding the goal behind the interaction.","children":[]}],"review-42":[{"text":"Children expect a new word to name **a whole object**. This is the **whole object assumption**. It is one of the **word learning strategies**, which are **default strategies that children bring to the word learning situation**. A **default** is a starting assumption that holds until evidence says otherwise.","children":[]},{"text":"These strategies are there to **narrow the hypothesis space** (the set of possible meanings) for new words. They give children a place to start when other cues are weak.","children":[]},{"text":"With the whole object assumption, the child does not expect a new word to name a part of the object. The child also does not expect it to name the object’s color, its texture or something it is doing. A child who hears a new word while looking at a cat will pick “cat” over “whiskers,” “furry” or “walking.”","children":[]},{"text":"This makes word learning faster. This is because the assumption gets rid of many unlikely meanings at once. It also fits the way adults usually name objects for children.","children":[]},{"text":"The assumption is only a starting preference. Later evidence can override it. Since it can be overridden, children do learn words for parts and properties once the evidence points that way.","children":[]}],"review-43":[{"text":"When a child hears a new word, the child gives it to an object that does not have a name yet. This is **mutual exclusivity**, from **Markman (1989)**.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because of a default that **objects can have only one name**. An object that already has a name is not expected to get a second one. So the implication is that **new words map to nameless objects**. Mutual exclusivity **emerges during the second half of the second year**, which is roughly between 18 and 24 months.","children":[]},{"text":"Say a child knows the word “dog” but has no word for a parrot, and both animals are in view. The child hears a new word. The dog already has a name, so the child decides the new word means the parrot. The child does not need a gaze cue or an emotional cue to make this choice.","children":[]},{"text":"Mutual exclusivity works together with the whole object assumption. The whole object assumption says a new word names an object. Mutual exclusivity says it names an object that has no name yet.","children":[]},{"text":"Mutual exclusivity is a default, so later learning can override it. This happens when an object turns out to have two names.","children":[]}],"review-44":[{"text":"Lenneberg (1967) said the **sensitive period** closes because language settles into one side of the brain. This is because his theory ties the sensitive period to the development of **cerebral dominance** (one hemisphere, usually the left, becoming specialized for language). He also called this developmental changes in **language laterality** (how much language depends on one side of the brain instead of both). Once language is fixed in the left hemisphere, the window for learning language closes.","children":[]},{"text":"Two kinds of evidence led him to the theory.","children":["The first was that brain development roughly lined up with language milestones. Cortical neurons grow quickly for about two years and then level off. There were no measurements between ages 2 and 18. Lenneberg linked the end of fast brain growth to the start of language learning.","The second was a review of **resilience after brain injury or surgery**. Children who lost the left hemisphere often went on to develop language. Adults did not get language back."]},{"text":"The theory is about the brain's **maturational state** (its stage of physical development). A young brain is less specialized, so it can move language to other tissue. A mature brain is specialized, so it has fewer ways to do that. Since a mature brain has fewer options, language learning gets harder.","children":[]},{"text":"**Second-language (L2) learning** is used as a **model system** for research on the sensitive period. A model system is a common, easily studied situation used to answer questions about a process that is hard to study directly.","children":[]}],"review-45":[{"text":"L2 learning gives a bigger comparison than first-language cases do. This is because most of what is known about **first-language (L1) sensitive periods** comes from **idiosyncratic** (particular to one individual) cases or relatively rare phenomena. The cases include children recovering from surgery that removed a hemisphere. They also include Genie, who was cut off from language until age 13. Each case comes with other unusual circumstances, such as injury, illness or abuse. So it is hard to figure out which factor caused the outcome.","children":[]},{"text":"In contrast, many people learn **second languages** at different times in their lives, often with somewhat **limited success**. L2 learners are common, so researchers can study large groups. They start at every age from early childhood to late adulthood. Their final success can be measured. So researchers can compare early starters with late starters.","children":[]},{"text":"Those limits on L2 success are used to figure out the **timing** of the sensitive or critical period. This is because later starters might reliably end up at lower levels. If they do, the age where the drop appears could mark when the sensitive period closes.","children":[]},{"text":"There is one complication, which is the **first language**. Every L2 learner already knows a language. That knowledge shapes how the new language is learned. Since L2 learners are common and start at every age, they still give a large comparison on timing.","children":[]}],"review-46":[{"text":"People who arrived in the United States younger ended up with better English grammar. **Johnson and Newport (1989)** found this when they tested for **critical period effects** in second-language learning. Their paper is about the influence of **maturational state** on learning English as a second language.","children":[]},{"text":"They ran the study because of **Lenneberg (1967)**. He hypothesized that language could be acquired only within a **critical period** (from early infancy until puberty). In its basic form, that idea is only about first-language acquisition. If it also applies to a second language, young children should learn a second language better than adults. They should also reach higher final proficiency.","children":[]},{"text":"To test this, they compared the English proficiency of **46 native Korean or Chinese speakers**. These people had arrived in the United States between the ages of 3 and 39. They had lived there between 3 and 26 years when they were tested. They did a **grammaticality judgment task** on a wide variety of structures of English grammar.","children":[]},{"text":"Earlier arrivals had a **clear and strong advantage** over later arrivals. Up to puberty, test performance was **linearly related** to age of arrival, so each extra year of age at arrival meant a lower score. After puberty, performance was **low but highly variable** and was unrelated to age of arrival.","children":[]},{"text":"The age effect was not an accident. This is because it did not come from differences in amount of experience with English, motivation, self-consciousness or American identification. It also appeared on every grammatical structure tested, even though later learners mastered some structures far better than others. Since the age effect held up, the authors concluded that a critical period for language acquisition **extends its effects to second-language acquisition**.","children":[]}],"review-47":[{"text":"The **Johnson and Newport (1989)** data have three main features.","children":[]},{"text":"The first is a **strong relationship between AOA and accuracy**. AOA is age of arrival. People who arrived earlier got higher scores.","children":[]},{"text":"The second is that **early learners**, up to about age 8, are often **near-perfect** (native-like, with scores close to native English speakers). Children who arrived after that, between about 8 and 15, scored progressively lower. Since the scores are already going down by then, the decline starts well before puberty.","children":[]},{"text":"The third is a **nearly random distribution of accuracy** for late arrivals. This does not mean the late arrivals were guessing. This is because late arrivals scored well above chance. “Nearly random” means that among late arrivals, age of arrival and score are only weakly related. Scores varied greatly from person to person. Two adults who arrived at the same age could end up with very different scores. Since age of arrival barely predicts an adult's score, the adult scores look nearly random.","children":[]},{"text":"Near-perfect scores for the youngest arrivals are the clearest sign of an early advantage. Adult arrivals scored lower still, with wide differences between individuals.","children":[]}],"review-48":[{"text":"Johnson and Newport (1989) see the **maturational state hypothesis** as more consistent with their data. This is because all of their participants had learned a first language normally. They had already used their capacity for acquiring languages. Later starters still learned the L2 less well. Since using the capacity did not protect it, the data fit the maturational state hypothesis, which is also named in the title of their paper.","children":[]},{"text":"The two versions of the **“critical period” hypothesis** start the same way. Both say that early in life, humans have a superior capacity for acquiring languages.","children":["The **exercise hypothesis** says that if the capacity is not exercised (used) during this time, it will disappear or decline with maturation. If the capacity is exercised, further language-learning abilities will remain intact throughout life. So an adult who learned an L1 normally should learn an L2 as well as a child does.","The **maturational state hypothesis** says the capacity disappears or declines with maturation, whether or not it has been used. So even a person with a normal L1 should learn an L2 less well as an adult."]},{"text":"Yes, this changes the interpretation of the **L1 critical period data**. This is because in cases like Genie, the capacity was never exercised. Both versions predict poor learning when the capacity was never exercised. Since both predict the same thing, the L1 cases cannot tell the two versions apart. Only second-language data can tell them apart, because only L2 learners exercised the capacity before learning another language.","children":[]}],"review-49":[{"text":"**Allopenna and colleagues (1994)** found the **cohort effect**. Words that start like the target compete early in recognition. A **cohort** is the set of words that share the same beginning sounds.","children":[]},{"text":"Participants saw a grid of pictures and shapes and followed spoken instructions.","children":["The **beaker** was the target.","The **beetle** starts like beaker.","The **speaker** rhymes with beaker.","The **carriage** is unrelated."]},{"text":"Looks were tracked for the first 1,000 milliseconds after the word began.","children":["Looks to the beaker and the beetle went up together early. This is because both fit the first sounds.","Looks to the beetle peaked near 0.2 around 500 milliseconds. They dropped once the sounds that tell the words apart came in.","Looks to the beaker kept going up toward 1 by about 1,000 milliseconds.","Looks to the speaker rose later and stayed small, near 0.1. This is because its overlap is at the end.","Looks to the carriage stayed near zero."]},{"text":"So words compete as soon as they match the incoming sounds. They drop out once the sounds stop matching. Since eyes follow this, eye movements track competition moment by moment.","children":[]}],"review-50":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey** checked whether a word in the other language competes just because it starts with the same sounds. They called these **cross-language competitors** (objects whose name in the other language starts like the target).","children":[]},{"text":"Their hypothesis was that if L1 words are always active, Russian speakers hearing English \"speaker\" should look at matches. This is because English **speaker** starts like Russian **спички (spichki)**, which means matches.","children":[]},{"text":"Each English target had an English and a Russian competitor.","children":["Speaker had spear and spichki (matches). Boot had book and buben (tambourine). Shark had shovel and sharik (balloon).","Chair had chess set and cherepaha (turtle). Marker had marbles and marka (stamp). Barbed wire had bark and barhat (velvet).","Plug had plum and platye (dress). Gun had gum and gaika (nut). Lock had lobster and lak dlea nogtei (nail polish)."]},{"text":"For Russian targets, it was flipped.","children":["Spichki (matches) had English spear and Russian spitsi (knitting needles).","Marka (stamp) had English marker and Russian marlya (cheese cloth).","Platye (dress) had English plum and Russian plashch (raincoat)."]},{"text":"The target and the competitor share only **sound**. Speakers and matches have nothing to do with each other in meaning. Since the overlap only exists if you know Russian, looks to the matches mean spichki was active.","children":[]}],"review-51":[{"text":"Yes, the first language stays active while bilinguals listen in their second language. We know this from Russian and English bilinguals who followed instructions in **English**. They looked at objects with similar Russian names far more than at unrelated objects.","children":[]},{"text":"The **between-language competitor** had a similar Russian name. The **within-language competitor** had a similar English name. Looks were counted as the percent of trials with a look at each object.","children":["With only the Russian-name competitor, looks were **18%**.","With only the English-name competitor, looks were also **18%**.","With both, the Russian-name one got 13% and the English-name one got 19%.","With no competitor, an unrelated control object got only **7%**."]},{"text":"With one competitor, both kinds drew looks **to the same extent**. So the Russian name pulled the eyes as strongly as a similar English word.","children":[]},{"text":"With both, the English-name competitor got more looks. The Russian-name competitor still got about **twice as many as the control**. Since bilinguals still picked the right object, the L1 words competed along the way without causing mistakes.","children":[]}],"review-52":[{"text":"No, English words did not reliably become active when the same bilinguals listened in **Russian**. We know this because looks to objects with similar English names were barely above the unrelated control.","children":[]},{"text":"Looks were counted as the percent of trials with a look at each object.","children":["With only the English-name competitor, looks were **8%**.","With only the Russian-name competitor, looks were 14%.","With both, the English-name one got 8% and the Russian-name one got 13%.","With no competitor, the control object got **5%**."]},{"text":"The difference between 8% and 5% is **not statistically significant** (it is small enough to be chance). The Russian-name competitor still drew extra looks, so the cohort effect showed up in Russian.","children":[]},{"text":"So the result goes one way only, which is called **asymmetric**.","children":["Russian words became active during English tasks.","English words did not reliably become active during Russian tasks."]},{"text":"Since the first language interferes with the second but not the reverse, the first language seems more firmly set up and easier to activate.","children":[]}],"review-53":[{"text":"In the **Revised Hierarchical Model** of **Kroll and Stewart**, activation flows more easily from an L2 word to an L1 word than the other way. This is because learners usually learn each L2 word by tying it to its L1 translation.","children":[]},{"text":"The model works by **spreading activation** (turning on one representation partly turns on the ones linked to it). It has three parts.","children":["One part is the L1 vocabulary.","Another part is the L2 vocabulary.","The third part is a store of **concepts** (meanings)."]},{"text":"**Lexical links** connect L1 and L2 words directly. **Conceptual links** connect each vocabulary to the concepts.","children":[]},{"text":"The L1 vocabulary is assumed to be **larger**. It is also **more strongly linked to concepts**. L2 words are linked to concepts more weakly.","children":[]},{"text":"The L2 vocabulary has strong forward links to the L1 vocabulary. The link from L2 to L1 is strong, and the link from L1 to L2 is weak. Since learning practices the L2 to L1 direction, that path gets strong. The direct path from L2 words to concepts stays weaker, at least early on.","children":[]}],"review-55":[{"text":"The model predicts that **balanced bilinguals** (people about equally good at both languages) are not \"two monolinguals in one\". This prediction is counter-intuitive. You might expect each language to run on its own.","children":[]},{"text":"The two languages compete all the time instead. This is because a balanced bilingual has similarly strong **conceptual links** for both languages. Each concept strongly activates its word in both languages. So both vocabularies light up whenever either is used.","children":[]},{"text":"Two processes are engaged all the time.","children":["**Competitive dynamics** means the words of the two languages keep competing to be picked.","**Inhibition** means holding back the word from the language not in use."]},{"text":"This prediction led to a lot of work on bilinguals' advantages in non-linguistic **cognitive control** tasks. Cognitive control is managing attention and holding back responses that do not fit. Since bilinguals constantly practice picking one language and holding back the other, they might get better at controlling attention in general.","children":[]}]}};
